{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788297748122,"sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-be7eb5341cbac234","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"ls src tests"},"output":"src:\nast.rs\ncheck\nemit_go.rs\nemit_jq.rs\nemit_js.rs\nemit_llvm.rs\nemit_lua.rs\nemit_py.rs\nemit_rs.rs\nemit_toylang.rs\nerror.rs\nfmt_tree.rs\ninput.rs\nlib.rs\nmain.rs\nparse.rs\nprelude.rs\ntags.rs\ntir.rs\nty.rs\n\ntests:\naliases.rs\nbackend_go.rs\nbackend_jq.rs\nbackend_js.rs\nbackend_llvm.rs\nbackend_py.rs\nbackend_rust.rs\nbare_application.rs\nchar.rs\ncorpus\ncorpus.rs\ndocs.rs\nenums.rs\neuler_real_data.rs\nexport_site.rs\nfmt_examples.rs\nfmt_project.rs\nfmt.rs\ngenerics.rs\nint64.rs\njsonlines.rs\nmatcher.rs\nopt.rs\nreductions.rs\nsnapshots\nstep_1.rs\nstep_2.rs\nstep_3.rs\nstep_4.rs\nstep_5.rs\nstreaming.rs\nstreams.rs\nsupport\ntag_corpus.rs\ntype_flow.rs\nunused.rs\nunwrap.rs\n","metadata":{"output":"src:\nast.rs\ncheck\nemit_go.rs\nemit_jq.rs\nemit_js.rs\nemit_llvm.rs\nemit_lua.rs\nemit_py.rs\nemit_rs.rs\nemit_toylang.rs\nerror.rs\nfmt_tree.rs\ninput.rs\nlib.rs\nmain.rs\nparse.rs\nprelude.rs\ntags.rs\ntir.rs\nty.rs\n\ntests:\naliases.rs\nbackend_go.rs\nbackend_jq.rs\nbackend_js.rs\nbackend_llvm.rs\nbackend_py.rs\nbackend_rust.rs\nbare_application.rs\nchar.rs\ncorpus\ncorpus.rs\ndocs.rs\nenums.rs\neuler_real_data.rs\nexport_site.rs\nfmt_examples.rs\nfmt_project.rs\nfmt.rs\ngenerics.rs\nint64.rs\njsonlines.rs\nmatcher.rs\nopt.rs\nreductions.rs\nsnapshots\nstep_1.rs\nstep_2.rs\nstep_3.rs\nstep_4.rs\nstep_5.rs\nstreaming.rs\nstreams.rs\nsupport\ntag_corpus.rs\ntype_flow.rs\nunused.rs\nunwrap.rs\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"ls src tests","time":{"start":1788297748117,"end":1788297748121}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Let me understand the codebase. This is a language design project (toylang) with Rust backends. I need to implement a streaming pipe-to-subprocess primitive: `lines | pipe_through(\"grep\", [\"foo\"]) | collect`.\n\nThe issue says it depends on Q35 (what stdout/stderr are, whether a program writes or returns). Let me look at the plans/questions.md Q35 and the shell-out-design board to understand the design shape.\n\nLet me explore the src and tests directories.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05eda1de50011El9P5muzJ6X7M","sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","messageID":"msg_05eda11d5001azXuBlwQLDT8xG"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788297748564,"sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-8f330384c3efac25","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"ls tests/corpus | head -50 && echo \"---count---\" && ls tests/corpus | wc -l"},"output":"adults.yaml\narith_add.yaml\narith_div_trunc.yaml\narith_mul.yaml\narith_neg.yaml\narith_precedence.yaml\narith_rem_sign.yaml\narith_sub.yaml\nbool_connectives.yaml\nbool_or_in_arm_body.yaml\nbool_or_splits_from_arms.yaml\nbool_precedence.yaml\nbool_result.yaml\nbool_short_circuit.yaml\ncall_without_parens.yaml\nchar_range_and_complement.yaml\nchars_unicode_honest.yaml\ncomparison_semantics.yaml\nconcat_chain.yaml\nconcat.yaml\ncond_branches_from_annotation.yaml\ncond_chain.yaml\ncond_false.yaml\ncond_in_map.yaml\ncond_true.yaml\ncontrol_char_escapes.yaml\ndiv_by_zero.yaml\nempty_keep_is_identity.yaml\nempty_result.yaml\nempty_vec_under_annotation.yaml\nenum_inputs_reject.yaml\nenum_inputs.yaml\nenum_input.yaml\nenum_match_default.yaml\nenum_match.yaml\nenum_payload_variant.yaml\nenum_recursive_extracted.yaml\nenum_recursive_generic.yaml\nenum_recursive_input.yaml\nenum_recursive_jsonlines.yaml\nenum_recursive_record.yaml\nenum_recursive_value.yaml\nenum_recursive_walk.yaml\nenum_scalar_input_reject.yaml\nenum_scalar_input.yaml\nenum_scalar_payload.yaml\nenum_string_ascription.yaml\nenum_unit_variant.yaml\nenum_variant_at_runtime.yaml\nenum_vec_payload.yaml\n---count---\n197\n","metadata":{"output":"adults.yaml\narith_add.yaml\narith_div_trunc.yaml\narith_mul.yaml\narith_neg.yaml\narith_precedence.yaml\narith_rem_sign.yaml\narith_sub.yaml\nbool_connectives.yaml\nbool_or_in_arm_body.yaml\nbool_or_splits_from_arms.yaml\nbool_precedence.yaml\nbool_result.yaml\nbool_short_circuit.yaml\ncall_without_parens.yaml\nchar_range_and_complement.yaml\nchars_unicode_honest.yaml\ncomparison_semantics.yaml\nconcat_chain.yaml\nconcat.yaml\ncond_branches_from_annotation.yaml\ncond_chain.yaml\ncond_false.yaml\ncond_in_map.yaml\ncond_true.yaml\ncontrol_char_escapes.yaml\ndiv_by_zero.yaml\nempty_keep_is_identity.yaml\nempty_result.yaml\nempty_vec_under_annotation.yaml\nenum_inputs_reject.yaml\nenum_inputs.yaml\nenum_input.yaml\nenum_match_default.yaml\nenum_match.yaml\nenum_payload_variant.yaml\nenum_recursive_extracted.yaml\nenum_recursive_generic.yaml\nenum_recursive_input.yaml\nenum_recursive_jsonlines.yaml\nenum_recursive_record.yaml\nenum_recursive_value.yaml\nenum_recursive_walk.yaml\nenum_scalar_input_reject.yaml\nenum_scalar_input.yaml\nenum_scalar_payload.yaml\nenum_string_ascription.yaml\nenum_unit_variant.yaml\nenum_variant_at_runtime.yaml\nenum_vec_payload.yaml\n---count---\n197\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"ls tests/corpus | head -50 && echo \"---count---\" && ls tests/corpus | wc -l","time":{"start":1788297748556,"end":1788297748562}},"id":"prt_05eda1e900012rIQfhA6wziyYr","sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","messageID":"msg_05eda11d5001azXuBlwQLDT8xG"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788297748936,"sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-ae929082777f13c8","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"grep -rn \"Q35\" plans/questions.md | head -20"},"output":"55:| [Q35](#q35-what-are-stdout-and-stderr-and-does-a-program-write-or-return) | What are stdout and stderr, and does a program write or return? | OPEN; `jsonlines` is now a top-level-only sink with no result type, which removes a placeholder answer without deciding the question |\n480:### Q35. What are stdout and stderr, and does a program write or return?\n541:  ([Q35](#q35-what-are-stdout-and-stderr-and-does-a-program-write-or-return)), which makes\n","metadata":{"output":"55:| [Q35](#q35-what-are-stdout-and-stderr-and-does-a-program-write-or-return) | What are stdout and stderr, and does a program write or return? | OPEN; `jsonlines` is now a top-level-only sink with no result type, which removes a placeholder answer without deciding the question |\n480:### Q35. What are stdout and stderr, and does a program write or return?\n541:  ([Q35](#q35-what-are-stdout-and-stderr-and-does-a-program-write-or-return)), which makes\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"grep -rn \"Q35\" plans/questions.md | head -20","time":{"start":1788297748929,"end":1788297748932}},"id":"prt_05eda203b001IKTq09pK5CRTpx","sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","messageID":"msg_05eda11d5001azXuBlwQLDT8xG"}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1788297748957,"sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","part":{"id":"prt_05eda21d8001gPyib9Ol22vWYp","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"5a1012ab7eb7388a8de2a55a732be7fdbf823735","messageID":"msg_05eda11d5001azXuBlwQLDT8xG","sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":23957,"input":4509,"output":132,"reasoning":116,"cache":{"write":0,"read":19200}},"cost":0.000644925}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788297751677,"sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","part":{"id":"prt_05eda2c72001aDvu4kPDZE01NW","messageID":"msg_05eda21e7001EU9bZbgSIYp3Uc","sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","snapshot":"5a1012ab7eb7388a8de2a55a732be7fdbf823735","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788297753521,"sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-986cb938641ab4a1","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/plans/questions.md","offset":480,"limit":120},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/plans/questions.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n480: ### Q35. What are stdout and stderr, and does a program write or return?\n481: \n482: This document mentions `stdout` once and `stderr` never. For a language whose subject is\n483: transforming data on a command line, that is not deferral, it is an oversight, and it is recorded\n484: here rather than quietly fixed because the absence shaped things: every question about streams so\n485: far has been about values coming *in*.\n486: \n487: What exists is one answer by default. A program is an expression, its value is rendered by the\n488: type-driven printer, and that is the whole of output. It has served: line-oriented output needed\n489: no side effect, because a `Str` containing newlines already is line-oriented output.\n490: \n491: What it does not answer:\n492: \n493: - **Does a program write, or return?** Returning is what makes a program an expression and what\n494:   keeps `map` reorderable, since a write is an effect and an effect is an ordering constraint.\n495:   Writing is what a long-running filter over a stream has to do, because holding the output until\n496:   the input ends is the thing streaming exists to avoid. These are not obviously reconcilable.\n497: - **Is stderr in the language or under it?** Every backend refuses in its own words today, and the\n498:   agreement harness deliberately checks only *that* they refuse. Making the message part of the\n499:   language means six backends must agree on it.\n500: - **Does output have a type?** Input does, and it is checked. Output is whatever the body renders\n501:   to, which means the printer is the only specification of the format.\n502: - **Does a stream of outputs exist at all**, or does a program produce one value whose rendering\n503:   happens to be long? jq answers the first; the design so far assumes the second without saying\n504:   so.\n505: \n506: Blocked on the same thing as [Q5](#q5-stream-lowering-strategy-across-the-three-backends): a\n507: program that writes as it goes is a program with an effect layer.\n508: \n509: The fused `jsonlines(f(inputs))` loop has since made write-as-it-goes real at the backend\n510: level, and [the streams decision](../draft.md#decided-stream-is-the-effect-layer-typed) gave the effect\n511: layer a type -- so the blockage above is gone, and the question is sharpened rather than\n512: answered. What was decided there about output is deliberately minimal: `jsonlines` is a sink,\n513: legal only as the program's outermost expression, with no result type. That removes the old\n514: placeholder (`jsonlines(...) : Str`, a type claiming the whole output exists as one value)\n515: without deciding whether stdout is a value, an effect, or something a program returns into.\n516: Everything in the bullet list above remains open.\n517: \n518: ### Q36. Does a real module system need imports, multiple files, and enforced privacy?\n519: \n520: One file exists: `prelude.toy`, always merged in whole, `pub` picking which of its definitions a\n521: program receives. What it does not have: a way to name what a program wants rather than receiving\n522: all of it; a way for a program's own file to export something another file imports; more than one\n523: file to import from at all; and enforced privacy, since a non-`pub` prelude definition today is\n524: not \"private to the prelude,\" it is simply never compiled, which forecloses a `pub` function\n525: calling a private helper.\n526: \n527: None of these were needed to get one function (`unlines`) out of six backends' worth of hand-\n528: written codegen, and building them speculatively risks shaping them around a prelude that has\n529: exactly one function in it. What would force an answer: a second prelude function needing an\n530: internal helper, at which point the non-`pub`-is-simply-absent rule stops being free.\n531: \n532: ### Q37. How do floats print, and what are NaN and Infinity in a JSON-shaped value model?\n533: \n534: The representation is [decided](../draft.md#decided-float-is-javascripts-double): IEEE 754 binary64,\n535: JavaScript's number. Everything observable about it is not, and each piece has to survive the\n536: agreement harness, which checks bytes.\n537: \n538: - **Printing.** Every backend must render the same double to the same text, and their defaults\n539:   do not agree on shortest-roundtrip versus fixed formatting, or on `1e21`-style switchover\n540:   points. The printer is currently the only specification of output format\n541:   ([Q35](#q35-what-are-stdout-and-stderr-and-does-a-program-write-or-return)), which makes\n542:   this a per-backend conformance rule to be stated by hand, the same lesson as\n543:   [backends can agree and still be wrong](../research-log/backends-can-agree-and-still-be-wrong.md).\n544: - **NaN and Infinity.** IEEE produces both; JSON can spell neither. A language whose values\n545:   are JSON-shaped either forbids them (a check on every producing operation), maps them to\n546:   something at the boundary (jq-style lossiness, the kind this design usually refuses), or\n547:   admits values its own output cannot carry.\n548: - **Division by zero.** The Int rule says a zero divisor is the only way arithmetic fails.\n549:   IEEE says `1.0 / 0.0` is `Infinity`, no failure at all. Keeping both means division's\n550:   behavior depends on its operand type; unifying means overriding one standard or the other.\n551: \n552: None of this blocks anything else, so it waits for `Float` to be forced by a real program the\n553: way `inputs` and `jsonlines` were.\n\n(End of file - total 553 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"### Q35. What are stdout and stderr, and does a program write or return?\n\nThis document mentions `stdout` once and `stderr` never. For a language whose subject is\ntransforming data on a command line, that is not deferral, it is an oversight, and it is recorded\nhere rather than quietly fixed because the absence shaped things: every question about streams so\nfar has been about values coming *in*.\n\nWhat exists is one answer by default. A program is an expression, its value is rendered by the\ntype-driven printer, and that is the whole of output. It has served: line-oriented output needed\nno side effect, because a `Str` containing newlines already is line-oriented output.\n\nWhat it does not answer:\n\n- **Does a program write, or return?** Returning is what makes a program an expression and what\n  keeps `map` reorderable, since a write is an effect and an effect is an ordering constraint.\n  Writing is what a long-running filter over a stream has to do, because holding the output until\n  the input ends is the thing streaming exists to avoid. These are not obviously reconcilable.\n- **Is stderr in the language or under it?** Every backend refuses in its own words today, and the\n  agreement harness deliberately checks only *that* they refuse. Making the message part of the\n  language means six backends must agree on it.","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/plans/questions.md","text":"### Q35. What are stdout and stderr, and does a program write or return?\n\nThis document mentions `stdout` once and `stderr` never. For a language whose subject is\ntransforming data on a command line, that is not deferral, it is an oversight, and it is recorded\nhere rather than quietly fixed because the absence shaped things: every question about streams so\nfar has been about values coming *in*.\n\nWhat exists is one answer by default. A program is an expression, its value is rendered by the\ntype-driven printer, and that is the whole of output. It has served: line-oriented output needed\nno side effect, because a `Str` containing newlines already is line-oriented output.\n\nWhat it does not answer:\n\n- **Does a program write, or return?** Returning is what makes a program an expression and what\n  keeps `map` reorderable, since a write is an effect and an effect is an ordering constraint.\n  Writing is what a long-running filter over a stream has to do, because holding the output until\n  the input ends is the thing streaming exists to avoid. These are not obviously reconcilable.\n- **Is stderr in the language or under it?** Every backend refuses in its own words today, and the\n  agreement harness deliberately checks only *that* they refuse. Making the message part of the\n  language means six backends must agree on it.\n- **Does output have a type?** Input does, and it is checked. Output is whatever the body renders\n  to, which means the printer is the only specification of the format.\n- **Does a stream of outputs exist at all**, or does a program produce one value whose rendering\n  happens to be long? jq answers the first; the design so far assumes the second without saying\n  so.\n\nBlocked on the same thing as [Q5](#q5-stream-lowering-strategy-across-the-three-backends): a\nprogram that writes as it goes is a program with an effect layer.\n\nThe fused `jsonlines(f(inputs))` loop has since made write-as-it-goes real at the backend\nlevel, and [the streams decision](../draft.md#decided-stream-is-the-effect-layer-typed) gave the effect\nlayer a type -- so the blockage above is gone, and the question is sharpened rather than\nanswered. What was decided there about output is deliberately minimal: `jsonlines` is a sink,\nlegal only as the program's outermost expression, with no result type. That removes the old\nplaceholder (`jsonlines(...) : Str`, a type claiming the whole output exists as one value)\nwithout deciding whether stdout is a value, an effect, or something a program returns into.\nEverything in the bullet list above remains open.\n\n### Q36. Does a real module system need imports, multiple files, and enforced privacy?\n\nOne file exists: `prelude.toy`, always merged in whole, `pub` picking which of its definitions a\nprogram receives. What it does not have: a way to name what a program wants rather than receiving\nall of it; a way for a program's own file to export something another file imports; more than one\nfile to import from at all; and enforced privacy, since a non-`pub` prelude definition today is\nnot \"private to the prelude,\" it is simply never compiled, which forecloses a `pub` function\ncalling a private helper.\n\nNone of these were needed to get one function (`unlines`) out of six backends' worth of hand-\nwritten codegen, and building them speculatively risks shaping them around a prelude that has\nexactly one function in it. What would force an answer: a second prelude function needing an\ninternal helper, at which point the non-`pub`-is-simply-absent rule stops being free.\n\n### Q37. How do floats print, and what are NaN and Infinity in a JSON-shaped value model?\n\nThe representation is [decided](../draft.md#decided-float-is-javascripts-double): IEEE 754 binary64,\nJavaScript's number. Everything observable about it is not, and each piece has to survive the\nagreement harness, which checks bytes.\n\n- **Printing.** Every backend must render the same double to the same text, and their defaults\n  do not agree on shortest-roundtrip versus fixed formatting, or on `1e21`-style switchover\n  points. The printer is currently the only specification of output format\n  ([Q35](#q35-what-are-stdout-and-stderr-and-does-a-program-write-or-return)), which makes\n  this a per-backend conformance rule to be stated by hand, the same lesson as\n  [backends can agree and still be wrong](../research-log/backends-can-agree-and-still-be-wrong.md).\n- **NaN and Infinity.** IEEE produces both; JSON can spell neither. A language whose values\n  are JSON-shaped either forbids them (a check on every producing operation), maps them to\n  something at the boundary (jq-style lossiness, the kind this design usually refuses), or\n  admits values its own output cannot carry.\n- **Division by zero.** The Int rule says a zero divisor is the only way arithmetic fails.\n  IEEE says `1.0 / 0.0` is `Infinity`, no failure at all. Keeping both means division's\n  behavior depends on its operand type; unifying means overriding one standard or the other.\n\nNone of this blocks anything else, so it waits for `Float` to be forced by a real program the\nway `inputs` and `jsonlines` were.","lineStart":480,"lineEnd":553,"totalLines":553,"truncated":false}},"title":"plans/questions.md","time":{"start":1788297753508,"end":1788297753520}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Let me look at Q35 in detail, and understand the current streaming model (lines, collect). Let me look at the plans/questions.md Q35 section, the streaming tests, and how lines/collect are implemented.\n\nLet me first look at the corpus streaming-related files and the streams tests.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05eda300f001xfU1LPFHLZJFne","sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","messageID":"msg_05eda21e7001EU9bZbgSIYp3Uc"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788297753770,"sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-ae7b5343bbe99736","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/tests/streams.rs"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/tests/streams.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: //! Streaming input, in its most minimal cut: `lines`, `collect`, and the rules that keep a\n2: //! single-use stream from ending up somewhere it cannot be honoured.\n3: //!\n4: //! What every backend agrees on when it runs lives in the corpus, as `lines_cat.yaml` and its\n5: //! siblings. This file holds the claims a corpus case cannot express: that a program is\n6: //! refused, and why.\n7: \n8: #[track_caller]\n9: fn err(src: &str) -> String {\n10:     toylang::compile(src).map(|_| ()).unwrap_err().to_string()\n11: }\n12: \n13: /// The containment bans: a stream cannot end up stored inside a Vec, a record, an enum payload,\n14: /// or another stream, because nothing can get a single-use value back out of one once it is in.\n15: mod containment {\n16:     use super::err;\n17: \n18:     /// Nothing can get a `Lines` value back out of a Vec once it is in one, and there is only\n19:     /// ever one real stdin to hold in the first place.\n20:     #[test]\n21:     fn lines_cannot_enter_a_vec() {\n22:         insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"str(1) | [lines]\"));\n23:     }\n24: \n25:     /// Same reasoning as the Vec case, for a record field.\n26:     #[test]\n27:     fn lines_cannot_enter_a_record() {\n28:         insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"{a: lines}\"));\n29:     }\n30: \n31:     /// The containment bans hold in the type grammar itself, not just at value construction\n32:     /// sites: an annotation cannot describe a stream as stored in a Vec.\n33:     #[test]\n34:     fn a_signature_cannot_put_a_stream_in_a_vec() {\n35:         insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"fn f(v: Vec<Stream<Str>>) -> Int = 0\\n\\n1\"));\n36:     }\n37: \n38:     /// Same ban for a record field, spelled in a signature.\n39:     #[test]\n40:     fn a_signature_cannot_put_a_stream_in_a_record() {\n41:         insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"fn f(r: {s: Stream<Str>}) -> Int = 0\\n\\n1\"));\n42:     }\n43: \n44:     /// And for an enum variant's payload, the one other annotation a value constructor reads.\n45:     #[test]\n46:     fn an_enum_payload_cannot_hold_a_stream() {\n47:         insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"enum E { v{s: Stream<Str>} }\\n\\n1\"));\n48:     }\n49: \n50:     /// The parens spelling puts a type directly in payload position, so the ban has to hold\n51:     /// there too, not only inside a record.\n52:     #[test]\n53:     fn a_scalar_enum_payload_cannot_be_a_stream() {\n54:         insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"enum E { v(Stream<Str>) }\\n\\n1\"));\n55:     }\n56: \n57:     /// A stream of streams has nothing it could yield: its entries would not be values.\n58:     #[test]\n59:     fn a_stream_cannot_hold_another_stream() {\n60:         insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"fn f(s: Stream<Stream<Str>>) -> Int = 0\\n\\n1\"));\n61:     }\n62: }\n63: \n64: /// The exactly-once rule: a stream-typed binding must be consumed exactly once, on every path,\n65: /// outside a mapper body, so that fusion always knows its pipeline's shape at compile time.\n66: mod linearity {\n67:     use super::err;\n68: \n69:     /// A stream has nothing to print: only `collect` turns it into a value. Caught for the\n70:     /// program's own result, which has no annotation to check against the way a function's\n71:     /// return type does.\n72:     #[test]\n73:     fn lines_cannot_be_the_programs_result() {\n74:         insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"lines\"));\n75:     }\n76: \n77:     /// `Stream` is spellable in a signature now -- the one thing the `Lines` design deliberately\n78:     /// withheld -- so a user function can take the stream and consume it itself.\n79:     #[test]\n80:     fn a_stream_signature_checks_end_to_end() {\n81:         assert!(\n82:             toylang::compile(\"fn f(s: Stream<Str>) -> Vec<Str> = collect(s)\\n\\nf(lines)\").is_ok()\n83:         );\n84:     }\n85: \n86:     /// Zero uses is an error: linear, not affine. Exactly-once can relax to at-most-once later\n87:     /// without breaking a program, while the reverse tightening would break every program that\n88:     /// dropped a stream.\n89:     #[test]\n90:     fn a_stream_parameter_must_be_consumed() {\n91:         insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"fn f(s: Stream<Str>) -> Int = 0\\n\\n1\"));\n92:     }\n93: \n94:     /// Two uses is the Python-generator mistake the single-use rule exists to prevent: the\n95:     /// second pass over an already-consumed iterator is silently empty.\n96:     #[test]\n97:     fn a_stream_parameter_cannot_be_consumed_twice() {\n98:         insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\n99:             \"fn f(s: Stream<Str>) -> Int = length(collect(s)) + length(collect(s))\\n\\n1\"\n100:         ));\n101:     }\n102: \n103:     /// `|` is the one construct that can silently drop its left side, so a stream piped into an\n104:     /// expression gets the same exactly-once rule a stream-typed parameter does.\n105:     #[test]\n106:     fn a_piped_stream_must_be_consumed() {\n107:         insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"lines | 0\"));\n108:     }\n109: \n110:     /// The generalization of \"contains `lines`, nothing to print\": any bare unconsumed stream as\n111:     /// the program's result, here one arriving through a user-written stream signature.\n112:     #[test]\n113:     fn a_program_cannot_result_in_a_bare_stream() {\n114:         insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\n115:             \"fn noisy(s: Stream<Str>) -> Stream<Str> = s | map(. + \\\"!\\\")\\n\\nnoisy(lines)\"\n116:         ));\n117:     }\n118: \n119:     /// Which branch runs is decided at runtime, and a pipeline's shape must not be: fusion has\n120:     /// to know its stages at compile time. Refusing is the reversible direction.\n121:     #[test]\n122:     fn a_conditional_cannot_yield_a_stream() {\n123:         insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\n124:             \"fn f(s: Stream<Str>) -> Stream<Str> = s if 1 == 1 else s\\n\\n1\"\n125:         ));\n126:     }\n127: \n128:     /// The same rule for a match's arms.\n129:     #[test]\n130:     fn a_match_cannot_yield_a_stream() {\n131:         insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\n132:             \"enum E { a, b }\\n\\nfn f(s: Stream<Str>) -> Stream<Str> = a | (a -> s or b -> s)\\n\\n1\"\n133:         ));\n134:     }\n135: \n136:     /// The elements of a map's result are stored, and a stream is not storable: the same\n137:     /// containment ban a Vec literal enforces, met before the Vec of streams could exist.\n138:     #[test]\n139:     fn a_map_body_cannot_be_a_stream() {\n140:         insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\n141:             \"fn f(s: Stream<Str>) -> Int = length([1] | map(s))\\n\\n1\"\n142:         ));\n143:     }\n144: \n145:     /// One spelled consumption, many runtime ones: a mapper's body runs once per element, so a\n146:     /// stream consumed there would be drained by the first element and empty for every later\n147:     /// one.\n148:     #[test]\n149:     fn a_stream_cannot_be_consumed_inside_a_mapper() {\n150:         insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\n151:             \"fn f(s: Stream<Str>) -> Vec<Int> = [1] | map(length(collect(s)))\\n\\n1\"\n152:         ));\n153:     }\n154: \n155:     /// A source read beside an unrelated piped value is not one chain either.\n156:     #[test]\n157:     fn a_pipes_stream_must_flow_in_from_its_left() {\n158:         insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"1 | (lines | map(. + \\\"!\\\"))\"));\n159:     }\n160: }\n161: \n162: /// The source rules: there is only one real stdin, so `lines`, `input`, and `inputs` cannot\n163: /// coexist or repeat, and a source is legal only in the program's own body -- never inside a\n164: /// mapper or a `fn`, both of which could run it more than once.\n165: mod sources {\n166:     use super::err;\n167: \n168:     /// There is only one real stdin, so a second read is refused at compile time rather than\n169:     /// silently handed nothing back, the way Python's own generators are.\n170:     #[test]\n171:     fn lines_cannot_be_read_twice() {\n172:         insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"[collect(lines), collect(lines)]\"));\n173:     }\n174: \n175:     /// Forced by jq specifically: raw-input mode, needed for `collect` to read lines rather than\n176:     /// JSON, changes what the whole invocation means, so it cannot coexist with `input` being\n177:     /// parsed as a JSON document in the same run. Verified against real jq before this rule was\n178:     /// added: `jq -Rn '[inputs]'` and ordinary `.`-is-the-document mode are mutually exclusive.\n179:     #[test]\n180:     fn input_and_lines_cannot_both_be_used() {\n181:         insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\n182:             \"fn f(x: Int) -> Int = x\\n\\nf(input) + (collect(lines) | 0)\"\n183:         ));\n184:     }\n185: \n186:     /// `inputs` reads the same real stdin `input` does, eagerly, so the two cannot coexist any\n187:     /// more than `input` and `lines` can.\n188:     #[test]\n189:     fn input_and_inputs_cannot_both_be_used() {\n190:         insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\n191:             \"fn f(x: Int) -> Int = x\\nfn g(x: Vec<Int>) -> Int = length(x)\\n\\nf(input) + g(collect(inputs))\"\n192:         ));\n193:     }\n194: \n195:     /// Forced by jq specifically, the same way `input`/`lines` is: raw-input mode for `lines`\n196:     /// and parsed-JSON mode for `inputs` are different invocation-wide flags, so one jq process\n197:     /// cannot run a program that asks for both.\n198:     #[test]\n199:     fn lines_and_inputs_cannot_both_be_used() {\n200:         insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\n201:             \"fn g(x: Vec<Int>) -> Int = length(x)\\n\\n(collect(lines) | 0) + g(collect(inputs))\"\n202:         ));\n203:     }\n204: \n205:     /// Like `input`, `inputs` has no type of its own until it is checked against one.\n206:     #[test]\n207:     fn inputs_needs_a_position_to_check_against() {\n208:         insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"inputs\"));\n209:     }\n210: \n211:     /// A second `inputs` would be a second stream claiming the same real stdin, refused exactly\n212:     /// the way a second `lines` is. This also retires the old element-type-agreement rule: with\n213:     /// one use, there is nothing left to disagree.\n214:     #[test]\n215:     fn inputs_cannot_be_read_twice() {\n216:         insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\n217:             \"fn f(s: Stream<Int>) -> Vec<Int> = collect(s)\\n\\nlength(f(inputs)) + length(f(inputs))\"\n218:         ));\n219:     }\n220: \n221:     /// `collect` is an ordinary function, so the bare-application rule reaches it like any\n222:     /// other: `collect lines`, with no parens at all, is one more way to spell the acceptance\n223:     /// program alongside `collect(lines)`.\n224:     #[test]\n225:     fn collect_takes_lines_with_or_without_parens() {\n226:         assert!(toylang::compile(\"collect(lines)\").is_ok());\n227:         assert!(toylang::compile(\"collect lines\").is_ok());\n228:     }\n229: \n230:     /// A Vec is already a value; `collect` is the exit from the effect layer, not a copy.\n231:     #[test]\n232:     fn collect_of_a_vec_is_refused() {\n233:         insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"collect([1, 2])\"));\n234:     }\n235: \n236:     /// `inputs` is born a stream now, so a Vec-wanted position names the eager spelling instead\n237:     /// of silently materializing.\n238:     #[test]\n239:     fn inputs_wanted_as_a_vec_names_the_eager_spelling() {\n240:         insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"fn g(x: Vec<Int>) -> Int = length(x)\\n\\ng(inputs)\"));\n241:     }\n242: \n243:     /// `input` is one whole value already in hand, which is exactly what a stream is not, so a\n244:     /// stream-typed position cannot ask for it.\n245:     #[test]\n246:     fn input_cannot_be_a_stream() {\n247:         insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\n248:             \"fn f(s: Stream<Str>) -> Vec<Str> = collect(s)\\n\\nf(input)\"\n249:         ));\n250:     }\n251: \n252:     /// The same once-per-element problem for the sources themselves.\n253:     #[test]\n254:     fn lines_cannot_be_read_inside_a_mapper() {\n255:         insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"length([1] | map(length(collect(lines))))\"));\n256:     }\n257: \n258:     #[test]\n259:     fn inputs_cannot_be_read_inside_a_mapper() {\n260:         insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\n261:             \"fn g(x: Vec<Int>) -> Int = length(x)\\n\\nlength([1] | map(g(collect(inputs))))\"\n262:         ));\n263:     }\n264: \n265:     /// A source is legal only in the program's own body, so a `fn` body cannot read one however\n266:     /// it is called. The rule is this blunt because anything finer leaked: see the reproducer\n267:     /// below.\n268:     #[test]\n269:     fn lines_cannot_be_read_inside_a_fn_body() {\n270:         insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"fn f(x: Int) -> Vec<Str> = collect(lines)\\n\\n1\"));\n271:     }\n272: \n273:     #[test]\n274:     fn inputs_cannot_be_read_inside_a_fn_body() {\n275:         insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"fn f(x: Int) -> Vec<Int> = collect(inputs)\\n\\n1\"));\n276:     }\n277: \n278:     /// The reproducer that forced the rule: the source is read in `f`'s body, the mapper only\n279:     /// sees an innocent `f(.)`, and before the fn-body ban this compiled and re-read stdin once\n280:     /// per element. The mapper-body check alone cannot catch it, because nothing at the call\n281:     /// site says a source is behind it.\n282:     #[test]\n283:     fn a_function_reading_a_source_cannot_be_called_from_a_mapper() {\n284:         insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\n285:             \"fn f(x: Int) -> Int = length(collect(lines)) + x\\n\\nlength([1, 2] | map(f(.)))\"\n286:         ));\n287:     }\n288: \n289:     /// A stream is born only at a source, so a function cannot conjure one: a stream result\n290:     /// flows in through a stream parameter, keeping every pipeline one chain from source to\n291:     /// sink.\n292:     #[test]\n293:     fn a_function_cannot_conjure_a_stream() {\n294:         insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"fn f(x: Int) -> Stream<Str> = lines\\n\\n1\"));\n295:     }\n296: }\n\n(End of file - total 296 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"//! Streaming input, in its most minimal cut: `lines`, `collect`, and the rules that keep a\n//! single-use stream from ending up somewhere it cannot be honoured.\n//!\n//! What every backend agrees on when it runs lives in the corpus, as `lines_cat.yaml` and its\n//! siblings. This file holds the claims a corpus case cannot express: that a program is\n//! refused, and why.\n\n#[track_caller]\nfn err(src: &str) -> String {\n    toylang::compile(src).map(|_| ()).unwrap_err().to_string()\n}\n\n/// The containment bans: a stream cannot end up stored inside a Vec, a record, an enum payload,\n/// or another stream, because nothing can get a single-use value back out of one once it is in.\nmod containment {\n    use super::err;\n\n    /// Nothing can get a `Lines` value back out of a Vec once it is in one, and there is only\n    /// ever one real stdin to hold in the first place.\n    #[test]","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/tests/streams.rs","text":"//! Streaming input, in its most minimal cut: `lines`, `collect`, and the rules that keep a\n//! single-use stream from ending up somewhere it cannot be honoured.\n//!\n//! What every backend agrees on when it runs lives in the corpus, as `lines_cat.yaml` and its\n//! siblings. This file holds the claims a corpus case cannot express: that a program is\n//! refused, and why.\n\n#[track_caller]\nfn err(src: &str) -> String {\n    toylang::compile(src).map(|_| ()).unwrap_err().to_string()\n}\n\n/// The containment bans: a stream cannot end up stored inside a Vec, a record, an enum payload,\n/// or another stream, because nothing can get a single-use value back out of one once it is in.\nmod containment {\n    use super::err;\n\n    /// Nothing can get a `Lines` value back out of a Vec once it is in one, and there is only\n    /// ever one real stdin to hold in the first place.\n    #[test]\n    fn lines_cannot_enter_a_vec() {\n        insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"str(1) | [lines]\"));\n    }\n\n    /// Same reasoning as the Vec case, for a record field.\n    #[test]\n    fn lines_cannot_enter_a_record() {\n        insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"{a: lines}\"));\n    }\n\n    /// The containment bans hold in the type grammar itself, not just at value construction\n    /// sites: an annotation cannot describe a stream as stored in a Vec.\n    #[test]\n    fn a_signature_cannot_put_a_stream_in_a_vec() {\n        insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"fn f(v: Vec<Stream<Str>>) -> Int = 0\\n\\n1\"));\n    }\n\n    /// Same ban for a record field, spelled in a signature.\n    #[test]\n    fn a_signature_cannot_put_a_stream_in_a_record() {\n        insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"fn f(r: {s: Stream<Str>}) -> Int = 0\\n\\n1\"));\n    }\n\n    /// And for an enum variant's payload, the one other annotation a value constructor reads.\n    #[test]\n    fn an_enum_payload_cannot_hold_a_stream() {\n        insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"enum E { v{s: Stream<Str>} }\\n\\n1\"));\n    }\n\n    /// The parens spelling puts a type directly in payload position, so the ban has to hold\n    /// there too, not only inside a record.\n    #[test]\n    fn a_scalar_enum_payload_cannot_be_a_stream() {\n        insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"enum E { v(Stream<Str>) }\\n\\n1\"));\n    }\n\n    /// A stream of streams has nothing it could yield: its entries would not be values.\n    #[test]\n    fn a_stream_cannot_hold_another_stream() {\n        insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"fn f(s: Stream<Stream<Str>>) -> Int = 0\\n\\n1\"));\n    }\n}\n\n/// The exactly-once rule: a stream-typed binding must be consumed exactly once, on every path,\n/// outside a mapper body, so that fusion always knows its pipeline's shape at compile time.\nmod linearity {\n    use super::err;\n\n    /// A stream has nothing to print: only `collect` turns it into a value. Caught for the\n    /// program's own result, which has no annotation to check against the way a function's\n    /// return type does.\n    #[test]\n    fn lines_cannot_be_the_programs_result() {\n        insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"lines\"));\n    }\n\n    /// `Stream` is spellable in a signature now -- the one thing the `Lines` design deliberately\n    /// withheld -- so a user function can take the stream and consume it itself.\n    #[test]\n    fn a_stream_signature_checks_end_to_end() {\n        assert!(\n            toylang::compile(\"fn f(s: Stream<Str>) -> Vec<Str> = collect(s)\\n\\nf(lines)\").is_ok()\n        );\n    }\n\n    /// Zero uses is an error: linear, not affine. Exactly-once can relax to at-most-once later\n    /// without breaking a program, while the reverse tightening would break every program that\n    /// dropped a stream.\n    #[test]\n    fn a_stream_parameter_must_be_consumed() {\n        insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"fn f(s: Stream<Str>) -> Int = 0\\n\\n1\"));\n    }\n\n    /// Two uses is the Python-generator mistake the single-use rule exists to prevent: the\n    /// second pass over an already-consumed iterator is silently empty.\n    #[test]\n    fn a_stream_parameter_cannot_be_consumed_twice() {\n        insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\n            \"fn f(s: Stream<Str>) -> Int = length(collect(s)) + length(collect(s))\\n\\n1\"\n        ));\n    }\n\n    /// `|` is the one construct that can silently drop its left side, so a stream piped into an\n    /// expression gets the same exactly-once rule a stream-typed parameter does.\n    #[test]\n    fn a_piped_stream_must_be_consumed() {\n        insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"lines | 0\"));\n    }\n\n    /// The generalization of \"contains `lines`, nothing to print\": any bare unconsumed stream as\n    /// the program's result, here one arriving through a user-written stream signature.\n    #[test]\n    fn a_program_cannot_result_in_a_bare_stream() {\n        insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\n            \"fn noisy(s: Stream<Str>) -> Stream<Str> = s | map(. + \\\"!\\\")\\n\\nnoisy(lines)\"\n        ));\n    }\n\n    /// Which branch runs is decided at runtime, and a pipeline's shape must not be: fusion has\n    /// to know its stages at compile time. Refusing is the reversible direction.\n    #[test]\n    fn a_conditional_cannot_yield_a_stream() {\n        insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\n            \"fn f(s: Stream<Str>) -> Stream<Str> = s if 1 == 1 else s\\n\\n1\"\n        ));\n    }\n\n    /// The same rule for a match's arms.\n    #[test]\n    fn a_match_cannot_yield_a_stream() {\n        insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\n            \"enum E { a, b }\\n\\nfn f(s: Stream<Str>) -> Stream<Str> = a | (a -> s or b -> s)\\n\\n1\"\n        ));\n    }\n\n    /// The elements of a map's result are stored, and a stream is not storable: the same\n    /// containment ban a Vec literal enforces, met before the Vec of streams could exist.\n    #[test]\n    fn a_map_body_cannot_be_a_stream() {\n        insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\n            \"fn f(s: Stream<Str>) -> Int = length([1] | map(s))\\n\\n1\"\n        ));\n    }\n\n    /// One spelled consumption, many runtime ones: a mapper's body runs once per element, so a\n    /// stream consumed there would be drained by the first element and empty for every later\n    /// one.\n    #[test]\n    fn a_stream_cannot_be_consumed_inside_a_mapper() {\n        insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\n            \"fn f(s: Stream<Str>) -> Vec<Int> = [1] | map(length(collect(s)))\\n\\n1\"\n        ));\n    }\n\n    /// A source read beside an unrelated piped value is not one chain either.\n    #[test]\n    fn a_pipes_stream_must_flow_in_from_its_left() {\n        insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"1 | (lines | map(. + \\\"!\\\"))\"));\n    }\n}\n\n/// The source rules: there is only one real stdin, so `lines`, `input`, and `inputs` cannot\n/// coexist or repeat, and a source is legal only in the program's own body -- never inside a\n/// mapper or a `fn`, both of which could run it more than once.\nmod sources {\n    use super::err;\n\n    /// There is only one real stdin, so a second read is refused at compile time rather than\n    /// silently handed nothing back, the way Python's own generators are.\n    #[test]\n    fn lines_cannot_be_read_twice() {\n        insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"[collect(lines), collect(lines)]\"));\n    }\n\n    /// Forced by jq specifically: raw-input mode, needed for `collect` to read lines rather than\n    /// JSON, changes what the whole invocation means, so it cannot coexist with `input` being\n    /// parsed as a JSON document in the same run. Verified against real jq before this rule was\n    /// added: `jq -Rn '[inputs]'` and ordinary `.`-is-the-document mode are mutually exclusive.\n    #[test]\n    fn input_and_lines_cannot_both_be_used() {\n        insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\n            \"fn f(x: Int) -> Int = x\\n\\nf(input) + (collect(lines) | 0)\"\n        ));\n    }\n\n    /// `inputs` reads the same real stdin `input` does, eagerly, so the two cannot coexist any\n    /// more than `input` and `lines` can.\n    #[test]\n    fn input_and_inputs_cannot_both_be_used() {\n        insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\n            \"fn f(x: Int) -> Int = x\\nfn g(x: Vec<Int>) -> Int = length(x)\\n\\nf(input) + g(collect(inputs))\"\n        ));\n    }\n\n    /// Forced by jq specifically, the same way `input`/`lines` is: raw-input mode for `lines`\n    /// and parsed-JSON mode for `inputs` are different invocation-wide flags, so one jq process\n    /// cannot run a program that asks for both.\n    #[test]\n    fn lines_and_inputs_cannot_both_be_used() {\n        insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\n            \"fn g(x: Vec<Int>) -> Int = length(x)\\n\\n(collect(lines) | 0) + g(collect(inputs))\"\n        ));\n    }\n\n    /// Like `input`, `inputs` has no type of its own until it is checked against one.\n    #[test]\n    fn inputs_needs_a_position_to_check_against() {\n        insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"inputs\"));\n    }\n\n    /// A second `inputs` would be a second stream claiming the same real stdin, refused exactly\n    /// the way a second `lines` is. This also retires the old element-type-agreement rule: with\n    /// one use, there is nothing left to disagree.\n    #[test]\n    fn inputs_cannot_be_read_twice() {\n        insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\n            \"fn f(s: Stream<Int>) -> Vec<Int> = collect(s)\\n\\nlength(f(inputs)) + length(f(inputs))\"\n        ));\n    }\n\n    /// `collect` is an ordinary function, so the bare-application rule reaches it like any\n    /// other: `collect lines`, with no parens at all, is one more way to spell the acceptance\n    /// program alongside `collect(lines)`.\n    #[test]\n    fn collect_takes_lines_with_or_without_parens() {\n        assert!(toylang::compile(\"collect(lines)\").is_ok());\n        assert!(toylang::compile(\"collect lines\").is_ok());\n    }\n\n    /// A Vec is already a value; `collect` is the exit from the effect layer, not a copy.\n    #[test]\n    fn collect_of_a_vec_is_refused() {\n        insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"collect([1, 2])\"));\n    }\n\n    /// `inputs` is born a stream now, so a Vec-wanted position names the eager spelling instead\n    /// of silently materializing.\n    #[test]\n    fn inputs_wanted_as_a_vec_names_the_eager_spelling() {\n        insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"fn g(x: Vec<Int>) -> Int = length(x)\\n\\ng(inputs)\"));\n    }\n\n    /// `input` is one whole value already in hand, which is exactly what a stream is not, so a\n    /// stream-typed position cannot ask for it.\n    #[test]\n    fn input_cannot_be_a_stream() {\n        insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\n            \"fn f(s: Stream<Str>) -> Vec<Str> = collect(s)\\n\\nf(input)\"\n        ));\n    }\n\n    /// The same once-per-element problem for the sources themselves.\n    #[test]\n    fn lines_cannot_be_read_inside_a_mapper() {\n        insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"length([1] | map(length(collect(lines))))\"));\n    }\n\n    #[test]\n    fn inputs_cannot_be_read_inside_a_mapper() {\n        insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\n            \"fn g(x: Vec<Int>) -> Int = length(x)\\n\\nlength([1] | map(g(collect(inputs))))\"\n        ));\n    }\n\n    /// A source is legal only in the program's own body, so a `fn` body cannot read one however\n    /// it is called. The rule is this blunt because anything finer leaked: see the reproducer\n    /// below.\n    #[test]\n    fn lines_cannot_be_read_inside_a_fn_body() {\n        insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"fn f(x: Int) -> Vec<Str> = collect(lines)\\n\\n1\"));\n    }\n\n    #[test]\n    fn inputs_cannot_be_read_inside_a_fn_body() {\n        insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"fn f(x: Int) -> Vec<Int> = collect(inputs)\\n\\n1\"));\n    }\n\n    /// The reproducer that forced the rule: the source is read in `f`'s body, the mapper only\n    /// sees an innocent `f(.)`, and before the fn-body ban this compiled and re-read stdin once\n    /// per element. The mapper-body check alone cannot catch it, because nothing at the call\n    /// site says a source is behind it.\n    #[test]\n    fn a_function_reading_a_source_cannot_be_called_from_a_mapper() {\n        insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\n            \"fn f(x: Int) -> Int = length(collect(lines)) + x\\n\\nlength([1, 2] | map(f(.)))\"\n        ));\n    }\n\n    /// A stream is born only at a source, so a function cannot conjure one: a stream result\n    /// flows in through a stream parameter, keeping every pipeline one chain from source to\n    /// sink.\n    #[test]\n    fn a_function_cannot_conjure_a_stream() {\n        insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"fn f(x: Int) -> Stream<Str> = lines\\n\\n1\"));\n    }\n}","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":296,"totalLines":296,"truncated":false}},"title":"tests/streams.rs","time":{"start":1788297753761,"end":1788297753768}},"id":"prt_05eda33a7001nD65giy0gOlcr6","sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","messageID":"msg_05eda21e7001EU9bZbgSIYp3Uc"}}
