:Lines => \"tl_collect_lines()\".to_string(),\nsrc/emit_py.rs:460:                    \"tl_jsonlines({}, lambda {e}: {})\",\nsrc/emit_rs.rs:171:fn tl_read_lines() -> Vec<String> {\nsrc/emit_rs.rs:408:    tl_read_lines()\nsrc/emit_rs.rs:443:const JSONLINES_HELPER: &str = r#\"fn tl_jsonlines<T>(v: &[T], f: fn(&T) -> String) -> String {\nsrc/emit_rs.rs:611:            reads_value || uses(\"tl_read_all_stdin(\") || uses(\"tl_read_lines(\"),\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"grep -rn \"lines\\b\" src/emit_*.rs | grep -v \"join_lines\\|//\" | head -40","time":{"start":1788297775045,"end":1788297775047}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"This is a substantial implementation. The task is to add `pipe_through(cmd, args)` to the language - a streaming pipe-to-subprocess primitive. It needs to work on all backends (go, jq, js, llvm, lua, py, rs).\n\nWait - \"Every case runs on every backend.\" So the corpus case `pipe_through` would run on all backends. But how would jq implement a subprocess? jq can't spawn subprocesses. Similarly lua/llvm?\n\nHmm. Let me look at how the backends handle `lines` and `collect` - and check whether some backends refuse. Actually the issue says: \"Every case runs on every backend. `output` means they all print it and agree; `refuses: true` means they all decline to run it.\" So if a backend can't do subprocesses, the corpus case would need `refuses: true` for the whole program. But then how do we test the pipe_through behavior?\n\nLet me look at the test harness - tests/corpus.rs - to understand how output/refuses work and how backends are configured. Also let me check which backends support streaming (lines/collect. The stream tests in tests/streams.rs compile and check errors; the corpus lines_cat.yaml runs on all backends.\n\nLet me check how backends handle `lines`.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05eda879e0012BU1Cg4mGcV5v4","sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","messageID":"msg_05eda6dae001nvBlXA4z2Qdt1K"}}
