{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788297793959,"sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-9b8c81612d4251b7","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"grep -rn \"pipe_through\\|streaming\\|subprocess\" tests/streaming.rs | head; echo \"=====\"; wc -l tests/streaming.rs"},"output":"66:        .expect(\"no output arrived before stdin closed -- this is reading all of stdin first, not streaming\");\n87:/// that boundary at all, since it runs embedded via `mlua` rather than as a subprocess `run_on`\n266:/// Lua, which validates through its own embedded host function rather than a subprocess's code at\n=====\n319 tests/streaming.rs\n","metadata":{"output":"66:        .expect(\"no output arrived before stdin closed -- this is reading all of stdin first, not streaming\");\n87:/// that boundary at all, since it runs embedded via `mlua` rather than as a subprocess `run_on`\n266:/// Lua, which validates through its own embedded host function rather than a subprocess's code at\n=====\n319 tests/streaming.rs\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"grep -rn \"pipe_through\\|streaming\\|subprocess\" tests/streaming.rs | head; echo \"=====\"; wc -l tests/streaming.rs","time":{"start":1788297793957,"end":1788297793959}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Let me look at how `lines` and `collect` are represented in the AST/TIR. Let me look at the parse, ast, tir files for `lines`, `collect`, `jsonlines`, `input`, `inputs`. Also look at the runtime for each backend to understand how `lines` is materialized.\n\nGiven the huge scope (6 backends: go, jq, js, llvm, lua, py, rs), I need to understand how a new builtin `pipe_through` would be implemented. Let me first understand the streaming architecture deeply.\n\nLet me look at how jsonlines (a sink) is handled - it's \"fused\" at the backend level. The streaming design: `lines` is a stream, operations like `map`, `select`, projection keep it a stream, `collect` turns it into a Vec, `jsonlines` is a sink.\n\nFor `pipe_through`, it takes a stream and outputs a stream (it transforms each... no, it pipes the whole stream through a subprocess). The shape is `lines | pipe_through(\"grep\", [\"foo\"]) | collect`. So pipe_through takes a stream and returns a stream. It's a stream-to-stream transform.\n\nLet me look at how streams are fused/lowered in the backends. Let me look at streaming.rs test and how fusion works. Let me also check the tir and the runtime.\n\nThis is really large. Let me delegate the exploration to understand the architecture, but I think I need to do it myself carefully since I'll be writing code.\n\nLet me focus. Let me look at how the fusion/lowering works for streaming. Let me read tests/streaming.rs and understand \"fusion\".","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05edac4c0001SXbj83Bukm3JCq","sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","messageID":"msg_05eda87e8001iwNC27M7REC022"}}
