{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788297802192,"sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-8a14fba0e5791210","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/src/tir.rs","limit":180,"offset":180},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/src/tir.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n180: /// position.\n181: #[derive(Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]\n182: pub enum Builtin {\n183:     /// `str(n)`, rendering an Int the way the printer does but reachable from a program.\n184:     IntToStr,\n185:     /// `i64(n)`, `Int -> Int64`: the one bridge between the two integer types, explicit\n186:     /// because nothing widens implicitly (kantord/toylang#83). A no-op on every backend whose\n187:     /// runtime integers are already 64 bits wide; JS builds a BigInt, and Go and Rust spell\n188:     /// the cast.\n189:     IntToI64,\n190:     /// `range(n)`, the integers from zero up to but not including n. Zero-based, matching jq,\n191:     /// Python, and this language's own indices.\n192:     Range,\n193:     /// `collect(s)`, `Stream<T> -> Vec<T>`: the one place a stream stops being a stream. What\n194:     /// comes back is an ordinary value, exactly as sized as it needs to be, with no trace left\n195:     /// of how it arrived.\n196:     Collect,\n197:     /// `jsonlines(v)`, printing each element of a `Vec<T>` on its own line rather than wrapping\n198:     /// the whole thing in `[...]`. Named for the format (jsonlines.org, also called NDJSON):\n199:     /// one JSON value per line. Polymorphic over the element type, so it is not in the fixed\n200:     /// signature table `builtin()` reads from; `synth` checks it directly, the way `map` and\n201:     /// `select` are checked from their own arm rather than through a table.\n202:     JsonLines,\n203:     /// `length(v)`, a Vec's length. A dense Vec already tracks this at runtime, so reading it out\n204:     /// costs nothing -- there is no fold or scan hiding behind the name.\n205:     Length,\n206:     /// `flatten(vv)`, flattening a `Vec<Vec<T>>` into one `Vec<T>`, for the case where the\n207:     /// number of inner Vecs is not known at the call site. Joining a fixed, known count of Vecs\n208:     /// is `+` instead (kantord/toylang#97, the add-trait reading of Q2 in plans/questions.md); this\n209:     /// builtin used to be named `concat` and cover both cases before that split.\n210:     Flatten,\n211:     /// `tail(v)`, every element but the first, `None` when `v` is empty. Consistent with how\n212:     /// `Index` already turns \"reaching past what's there\" into `Opt` rather than a runtime\n213:     /// failure.\n214:     Tail,\n215:     /// `fields(r)`, a record's field names in declaration order (kantord/toylang#63, the\n216:     /// accessor #60's ratification promised once order left type identity). The names come from\n217:     /// `r`'s checked type, not its runtime value, so every backend can emit them as a literal;\n218:     /// `r` is still evaluated for whatever it would otherwise do, the same as any other argument.\n219:     Fields,\n220:     /// `chars(s)`, `Str -> Vec<Char>`: every Unicode scalar value in `s`, in order. Decoded by\n221:     /// codepoint on every backend, so a character outside the Basic Multilingual Plane is one\n222:     /// element here even on a target whose own strings need a surrogate pair to spell it\n223:     /// (kantord/toylang#75).\n224:     Chars,\n225:     /// `sort(v)`, `Vec<T> -> Vec<T>`: `v`'s elements in ascending order by the same total order\n226:     /// `<` already gives `T`. One value in, one value out with no lawful Stream instance\n227:     /// (kantord/toylang#86, Q20 in plans/questions.md), so it is checked as a Vec-only builtin the way\n228:     /// `length`/`tail`/`flatten` are rather than through `select`/`map`'s cardinality\n229:     /// polymorphism. Restricted to the element types ordering already typechecks on --\n230:     /// Int, Int64, Str, Char -- so every backend can reach for its native ordering.\n231:     Sort,\n232:     /// `reverse(v)`, `Vec<T> -> Vec<T>`: `v`'s elements in the opposite order. Blocking for the\n233:     /// same reason `sort` is (the whole Vec has to be there before the first output element\n234:     /// is), but unrestricted in element type, since reversing needs no comparison.\n235:     Reverse,\n236:     /// `sum(v)`, the reduction of `+` over `v`'s elements, at the element type's own width: a\n237:     /// `Vec<Int>` sums to `Int`, a `Vec<Int64>` to `Int64`, and an empty Vec sums to 0. Each\n238:     /// addition wraps the way the language's `+` does, so a sum that leaves the width is the\n239:     /// same number a hand-written fold would produce (kantord/toylang#140).\n240:     Sum,\n241:     /// `max(v)`, the greatest of `v`'s elements, `Opt<T>` because an empty Vec has no maximum\n242:     /// -- the same answer indexing gives to absence (kantord/toylang#140). Restricted to the\n243:     /// same two integer element types `sum` takes, so a backend can reach for its native\n244:     /// maximum.\n245:     Max,\n246: }\n247: \n248: pub struct Func {\n249:     pub name: String,\n250:     /// `None` for a nullary function.\n251:     pub param: Option<String>,\n252:     pub param_ty: Option<Type>,\n253:     pub body: Tir,\n254: }\n255: \n256: pub struct Program {\n257:     pub funcs: Vec<Func>,\n258:     pub body: Tir,\n259:     /// The type stdin must have, if the program reads it.\n260:     pub input: Option<Type>,\n261:     /// The element type each line of stdin parses as, if the program reads `inputs`.\n262:     pub inputs: Option<Type>,\n263:     /// Whether the program reads `lines`. A separate flag from `input`, since the two are\n264:     /// unrelated readers of the same real stdin and a program using `lines` alone still needs\n265:     /// it connected, even though `input` is `None`.\n266:     pub uses_lines: bool,\n267:     /// Every enum the program declared, the prelude's included. A backend has no checker `Ctx`\n268:     /// to hand, and the variant list on a `Type::Enum` is a placeholder wherever a recursive\n269:     /// enum's payload reaches back to itself, so this travels with the tree: it is what\n270:     /// `ty::variants` re-derives from (kantord/toylang#94).\n271:     pub enums: Enums,\n272: }\n273: \n274: /// The element type a backend iterates over. Under eager lowering a stream is materialized as\n275: /// the Vec of its entries, so a Stream's element counts exactly as a Vec's here; the checker's\n276: /// `Type::elem` deliberately does not agree, keeping the reducers Vec-only at the surface.\n277: pub fn runtime_elem(ty: &Type) -> Option<&Type> {\n278:     match ty {\n279:         Type::Vec(t) | Type::Stream(t) => Some(t),\n280:         _ => None,\n281:     }\n282: }\n283: \n284: /// How many dimension layers wrap a scalar -- `Vec`s, plus the outermost `Stream` when there\n285: /// is one. Field access distributes over each of them.\n286: pub fn vec_depth(ty: &Type) -> usize {\n287:     let mut depth = 0;\n288:     let mut inner = ty;\n289:     while let Some(elem) = runtime_elem(inner) {\n290:         depth += 1;\n291:         inner = elem;\n292:     }\n293:     depth\n294: }\n295: \n296: /// One `map`/`select` applied between reading a record and printing it, in source order (the\n297: /// stage nearest the source first).\n298: pub enum Stage<'a> {\n299:     Map { param: LocalId, body: &'a Tir },\n300:     Select { param: LocalId, pred: &'a Tir },\n301: }\n302: \n303: /// What a fused loop reads one entry at a time: parsed JSON values, or raw lines.\n304: #[derive(Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]\n305: pub enum Source {\n306:     Inputs,\n307:     Lines,\n308: }\n309: \n310: /// A stream-typed pipeline ending in `jsonlines`, compiled as a read-one/transform-one/\n311: /// write-one loop. Decided by the types, not by a structural guess: the old `recognize_fusion`\n312: /// pattern match retired when `Stream` entered the type grammar (plans/streams.md step 5), so\n313: /// whether a program streams is now exactly whether its types say so.\n314: pub struct Fusion<'a> {\n315:     pub source: Source,\n316:     pub stages: Vec<Stage<'a>>,\n317: }\n318: \n319: /// What a stream chain bottoms out at: a source, a function's own parameter, or the local a\n320: /// `|` bound.\n321: enum Base<'a> {\n322:     Inputs,\n323:     Lines,\n324:     Var(&'a String),\n325:     Local(LocalId),\n326: }\n327: \n328: /// Append the per-element stages of the stream-typed `t`, returning what its chain bottoms out\n329: /// at.\n330: ///\n331: /// Total, not a recognizer: the checker leaves exactly these kinds able to carry a Stream type\n332: /// (sources, bindings, mappers, calls -- a conditional or match yielding one is refused, and\n333: /// projection is normalized to a Map), so the `unreachable!` arms are compiler bugs, never\n334: /// program shapes. That is the invariant this step lands: a stream-typed program that failed\n335: /// to fuse would silently materialize stdin, which is the defect the feature exists to remove.\n336: fn flatten<'a>(t: &'a Tir, program: &'a Program, stages: &mut Vec<Stage<'a>>) -> Base<'a> {\n337:     match &t.kind {\n338:         Kind::Inputs => Base::Inputs,\n339:         Kind::Lines => Base::Lines,\n340:         Kind::Var(name) => Base::Var(name),\n341:         Kind::Local(id) => Base::Local(*id),\n342:         Kind::Bind { local, value, body } => {\n343:             let base = flatten(value, program, stages);\n344:             match flatten(body, program, stages) {\n345:                 Base::Local(id) if id == *local => base,\n346:                 _ => unreachable!(\"a piped stream is consumed by the pipe's own body\"),\n347:             }\n348:         }\n349:         Kind::Map {\n350:             source,\n351:             param,\n352:             body,\n353:         } => {\n354:             let base = flatten(source, program, stages);\n355:             stages.push(Stage::Map {\n356:                 param: *param,\n357:                 body,\n358:             });\n359:             base\n\n(Showing lines 180-359 of 586. Use offset=360 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"/// position.\n#[derive(Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]\npub enum Builtin {\n    /// `str(n)`, rendering an Int the way the printer does but reachable from a program.\n    IntToStr,\n    /// `i64(n)`, `Int -> Int64`: the one bridge between the two integer types, explicit\n    /// because nothing widens implicitly (kantord/toylang#83). A no-op on every backend whose\n    /// runtime integers are already 64 bits wide; JS builds a BigInt, and Go and Rust spell\n    /// the cast.\n    IntToI64,\n    /// `range(n)`, the integers from zero up to but not including n. Zero-based, matching jq,\n    /// Python, and this language's own indices.\n    Range,\n    /// `collect(s)`, `Stream<T> -> Vec<T>`: the one place a stream stops being a stream. What\n    /// comes back is an ordinary value, exactly as sized as it needs to be, with no trace left\n    /// of how it arrived.\n    Collect,\n    /// `jsonlines(v)`, printing each element of a `Vec<T>` on its own line rather than wrapping\n    /// the whole thing in `[...]`. Named for the format (jsonlines.org, also called NDJSON):\n    /// one JSON value per line. Polymorphic over the element type, so it is not in the fixed","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/src/tir.rs","text":"/// position.\n#[derive(Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]\npub enum Builtin {\n    /// `str(n)`, rendering an Int the way the printer does but reachable from a program.\n    IntToStr,\n    /// `i64(n)`, `Int -> Int64`: the one bridge between the two integer types, explicit\n    /// because nothing widens implicitly (kantord/toylang#83). A no-op on every backend whose\n    /// runtime integers are already 64 bits wide; JS builds a BigInt, and Go and Rust spell\n    /// the cast.\n    IntToI64,\n    /// `range(n)`, the integers from zero up to but not including n. Zero-based, matching jq,\n    /// Python, and this language's own indices.\n    Range,\n    /// `collect(s)`, `Stream<T> -> Vec<T>`: the one place a stream stops being a stream. What\n    /// comes back is an ordinary value, exactly as sized as it needs to be, with no trace left\n    /// of how it arrived.\n    Collect,\n    /// `jsonlines(v)`, printing each element of a `Vec<T>` on its own line rather than wrapping\n    /// the whole thing in `[...]`. Named for the format (jsonlines.org, also called NDJSON):\n    /// one JSON value per line. Polymorphic over the element type, so it is not in the fixed\n    /// signature table `builtin()` reads from; `synth` checks it directly, the way `map` and\n    /// `select` are checked from their own arm rather than through a table.\n    JsonLines,\n    /// `length(v)`, a Vec's length. A dense Vec already tracks this at runtime, so reading it out\n    /// costs nothing -- there is no fold or scan hiding behind the name.\n    Length,\n    /// `flatten(vv)`, flattening a `Vec<Vec<T>>` into one `Vec<T>`, for the case where the\n    /// number of inner Vecs is not known at the call site. Joining a fixed, known count of Vecs\n    /// is `+` instead (kantord/toylang#97, the add-trait reading of Q2 in plans/questions.md); this\n    /// builtin used to be named `concat` and cover both cases before that split.\n    Flatten,\n    /// `tail(v)`, every element but the first, `None` when `v` is empty. Consistent with how\n    /// `Index` already turns \"reaching past what's there\" into `Opt` rather than a runtime\n    /// failure.\n    Tail,\n    /// `fields(r)`, a record's field names in declaration order (kantord/toylang#63, the\n    /// accessor #60's ratification promised once order left type identity). The names come from\n    /// `r`'s checked type, not its runtime value, so every backend can emit them as a literal;\n    /// `r` is still evaluated for whatever it would otherwise do, the same as any other argument.\n    Fields,\n    /// `chars(s)`, `Str -> Vec<Char>`: every Unicode scalar value in `s`, in order. Decoded by\n    /// codepoint on every backend, so a character outside the Basic Multilingual Plane is one\n    /// element here even on a target whose own strings need a surrogate pair to spell it\n    /// (kantord/toylang#75).\n    Chars,\n    /// `sort(v)`, `Vec<T> -> Vec<T>`: `v`'s elements in ascending order by the same total order\n    /// `<` already gives `T`. One value in, one value out with no lawful Stream instance\n    /// (kantord/toylang#86, Q20 in plans/questions.md), so it is checked as a Vec-only builtin the way\n    /// `length`/`tail`/`flatten` are rather than through `select`/`map`'s cardinality\n    /// polymorphism. Restricted to the element types ordering already typechecks on --\n    /// Int, Int64, Str, Char -- so every backend can reach for its native ordering.\n    Sort,\n    /// `reverse(v)`, `Vec<T> -> Vec<T>`: `v`'s elements in the opposite order. Blocking for the\n    /// same reason `sort` is (the whole Vec has to be there before the first output element\n    /// is), but unrestricted in element type, since reversing needs no comparison.\n    Reverse,\n    /// `sum(v)`, the reduction of `+` over `v`'s elements, at the element type's own width: a\n    /// `Vec<Int>` sums to `Int`, a `Vec<Int64>` to `Int64`, and an empty Vec sums to 0. Each\n    /// addition wraps the way the language's `+` does, so a sum that leaves the width is the\n    /// same number a hand-written fold would produce (kantord/toylang#140).\n    Sum,\n    /// `max(v)`, the greatest of `v`'s elements, `Opt<T>` because an empty Vec has no maximum\n    /// -- the same answer indexing gives to absence (kantord/toylang#140). Restricted to the\n    /// same two integer element types `sum` takes, so a backend can reach for its native\n    /// maximum.\n    Max,\n}\n\npub struct Func {\n    pub name: String,\n    /// `None` for a nullary function.\n    pub param: Option<String>,\n    pub param_ty: Option<Type>,\n    pub body: Tir,\n}\n\npub struct Program {\n    pub funcs: Vec<Func>,\n    pub body: Tir,\n    /// The type stdin must have, if the program reads it.\n    pub input: Option<Type>,\n    /// The element type each line of stdin parses as, if the program reads `inputs`.\n    pub inputs: Option<Type>,\n    /// Whether the program reads `lines`. A separate flag from `input`, since the two are\n    /// unrelated readers of the same real stdin and a program using `lines` alone still needs\n    /// it connected, even though `input` is `None`.\n    pub uses_lines: bool,\n    /// Every enum the program declared, the prelude's included. A backend has no checker `Ctx`\n    /// to hand, and the variant list on a `Type::Enum` is a placeholder wherever a recursive\n    /// enum's payload reaches back to itself, so this travels with the tree: it is what\n    /// `ty::variants` re-derives from (kantord/toylang#94).\n    pub enums: Enums,\n}\n\n/// The element type a backend iterates over. Under eager lowering a stream is materialized as\n/// the Vec of its entries, so a Stream's element counts exactly as a Vec's here; the checker's\n/// `Type::elem` deliberately does not agree, keeping the reducers Vec-only at the surface.\npub fn runtime_elem(ty: &Type) -> Option<&Type> {\n    match ty {\n        Type::Vec(t) | Type::Stream(t) => Some(t),\n        _ => None,\n    }\n}\n\n/// How many dimension layers wrap a scalar -- `Vec`s, plus the outermost `Stream` when there\n/// is one. Field access distributes over each of them.\npub fn vec_depth(ty: &Type) -> usize {\n    let mut depth = 0;\n    let mut inner = ty;\n    while let Some(elem) = runtime_elem(inner) {\n        depth += 1;\n        inner = elem;\n    }\n    depth\n}\n\n/// One `map`/`select` applied between reading a record and printing it, in source order (the\n/// stage nearest the source first).\npub enum Stage<'a> {\n    Map { param: LocalId, body: &'a Tir },\n    Select { param: LocalId, pred: &'a Tir },\n}\n\n/// What a fused loop reads one entry at a time: parsed JSON values, or raw lines.\n#[derive(Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]\npub enum Source {\n    Inputs,\n    Lines,\n}\n\n/// A stream-typed pipeline ending in `jsonlines`, compiled as a read-one/transform-one/\n/// write-one loop. Decided by the types, not by a structural guess: the old `recognize_fusion`\n/// pattern match retired when `Stream` entered the type grammar (plans/streams.md step 5), so\n/// whether a program streams is now exactly whether its types say so.\npub struct Fusion<'a> {\n    pub source: Source,\n    pub stages: Vec<Stage<'a>>,\n}\n\n/// What a stream chain bottoms out at: a source, a function's own parameter, or the local a\n/// `|` bound.\nenum Base<'a> {\n    Inputs,\n    Lines,\n    Var(&'a String),\n    Local(LocalId),\n}\n\n/// Append the per-element stages of the stream-typed `t`, returning what its chain bottoms out\n/// at.\n///\n/// Total, not a recognizer: the checker leaves exactly these kinds able to carry a Stream type\n/// (sources, bindings, mappers, calls -- a conditional or match yielding one is refused, and\n/// projection is normalized to a Map), so the `unreachable!` arms are compiler bugs, never\n/// program shapes. That is the invariant this step lands: a stream-typed program that failed\n/// to fuse would silently materialize stdin, which is the defect the feature exists to remove.\nfn flatten<'a>(t: &'a Tir, program: &'a Program, stages: &mut Vec<Stage<'a>>) -> Base<'a> {\n    match &t.kind {\n        Kind::Inputs => Base::Inputs,\n        Kind::Lines => Base::Lines,\n        Kind::Var(name) => Base::Var(name),\n        Kind::Local(id) => Base::Local(*id),\n        Kind::Bind { local, value, body } => {\n            let base = flatten(value, program, stages);\n            match flatten(body, program, stages) {\n                Base::Local(id) if id == *local => base,\n                _ => unreachable!(\"a piped stream is consumed by the pipe's own body\"),\n            }\n        }\n        Kind::Map {\n            source,\n            param,\n            body,\n        } => {\n            let base = flatten(source, program, stages);\n            stages.push(Stage::Map {\n                param: *param,\n                body,\n            });\n            base","lineStart":180,"lineEnd":359,"totalLines":586,"truncated":true}},"title":"src/tir.rs","time":{"start":1788297802173,"end":1788297802188}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Let me understand how streams work. `lines` is a source, `collect` and `jsonlines` are builtins. `pipe_through` needs to be a stream-to-stream transformation. This is complex.\n\nLet me look at how streaming lowering works. Let me search for how `jsonlines` is \"fused\" - it's a sink. The key insight: `pipe_through` is different - it's not a sink, it takes a stream and produces a stream.\n\nLet me look at the tir.rs around lines 190-440 to understand the streaming representation.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05edae7ac001N96kuJDRbhtIT1","sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","messageID":"msg_05edad414001E4hrLHCG3fGKg6"}}
