{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788297813912,"sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-bd3ce3d19331b773","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/src/tir.rs","limit":227,"offset":360},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/src/tir.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n360:         }\n361:         Kind::Select {\n362:             source,\n363:             param,\n364:             pred,\n365:         } => {\n366:             let base = flatten(source, program, stages);\n367:             stages.push(Stage::Select {\n368:                 param: *param,\n369:                 pred,\n370:             });\n371:             base\n372:         }\n373:         // The argument's stages first, then the callee's body inlined: its chain bottoms at\n374:         // its own parameter (a stream-returning function must take a stream, and linearity\n375:         // makes the parameter the base of whatever it returns).\n376:         Kind::Call { func, arg } => {\n377:             let f = program\n378:                 .funcs\n379:                 .iter()\n380:                 .find(|f| &f.name == func)\n381:                 .expect(\"the checker resolved every call\");\n382:             // A stream-returning function must take a stream (see `signatures`), so a call\n383:             // reaching here -- one whose result is stream-typed -- always carries an argument.\n384:             let arg = arg\n385:                 .as_deref()\n386:                 .expect(\"a stream-returning function is never nullary\");\n387:             let base = flatten(arg, program, stages);\n388:             let param = f\n389:                 .param\n390:                 .as_deref()\n391:                 .expect(\"a stream-returning function is never nullary\");\n392:             match flatten(&f.body, program, stages) {\n393:                 Base::Var(name) if name == param => base,\n394:                 _ => unreachable!(\"a stream-returning function's chain bottoms at its parameter\"),\n395:             }\n396:         }\n397:         _ => unreachable!(\"the checker leaves no other kind stream-typed\"),\n398:     }\n399: }\n400: \n401: /// The type question `streams_inputs` used to answer by pattern match: a `jsonlines` program\n402: /// whose argument is stream-typed fuses, and one whose argument is a Vec (already collected)\n403: /// stays an ordinary value and runs eagerly, exactly as its types say.\n404: pub fn fusion(program: &Program) -> Option<Fusion<'_>> {\n405:     let Kind::Builtin {\n406:         which: Builtin::JsonLines,\n407:         arg,\n408:     } = &program.body.kind\n409:     else {\n410:         return None;\n411:     };\n412:     if !matches!(arg.ty, Type::Stream(_)) {\n413:         return None;\n414:     }\n415:     let mut stages = Vec::new();\n416:     let source = match flatten(arg, program, &mut stages) {\n417:         Base::Inputs => Source::Inputs,\n418:         Base::Lines => Source::Lines,\n419:         Base::Var(_) | Base::Local(_) => {\n420:             unreachable!(\"a program-level stream chain bottoms at its source\")\n421:         }\n422:     };\n423:     Some(Fusion { source, stages })\n424: }\n425: \n426: /// Every enum type the program prints that can hold another of its own type, deduplicated and\n427: /// in the order the walk meets them.\n428: ///\n429: /// A backend writes its printer by expanding a type inline, which is exactly what does not\n430: /// terminate on one of these. So each gets a named function the expansion can call back into\n431: /// instead, and this is the list of functions to write (kantord/toylang#94). Only what is\n432: /// printed: a program can hold a recursive value, match on it and print an Int, and a printer\n433: /// nobody calls is dead code that Go, at least, would then demand an import for.\n434: pub fn printed_recursive_enums(program: &Program) -> Vec<Type> {\n435:     let mut printed = vec![program.body.ty.clone()];\n436:     let mut collect_printed = |t: &Tir| {\n437:         if let Kind::Builtin {\n438:             which: Builtin::JsonLines,\n439:             arg,\n440:         } = &t.kind\n441:         {\n442:             printed.push(\n443:                 runtime_elem(&arg.ty)\n444:                     .expect(\"jsonlines takes a Vec or a stream\")\n445:                     .clone(),\n446:             );\n447:         }\n448:     };\n449:     for f in &program.funcs {\n450:         each_node(&f.body, &mut collect_printed);\n451:     }\n452:     each_node(&program.body, &mut collect_printed);\n453: \n454:     let mut found = Vec::new();\n455:     let mut seen = Vec::new();\n456:     for ty in &printed {\n457:         reachable_enums(&program.enums, ty, &mut seen, &mut found);\n458:     }\n459:     found.retain(|ty| crate::ty::is_recursive(&program.enums, ty));\n460:     found\n461: }\n462: \n463: /// Append every enum type nested anywhere in `ty` to `found`, descending through payloads read\n464: /// from the registry rather than off the type. `seen` is what stops a recursive one: its own\n465: /// payload leads back to a type already visited.\n466: fn reachable_enums(enums: &Enums, ty: &Type, seen: &mut Vec<Type>, found: &mut Vec<Type>) {\n467:     match ty {\n468:         Type::Vec(e) | Type::Stream(e) => reachable_enums(enums, e, seen, found),\n469:         Type::Record(fields) => {\n470:             for (_, f) in fields {\n471:                 reachable_enums(enums, f, seen, found);\n472:             }\n473:         }\n474:         Type::Enum { name, args, .. } => {\n475:             if seen.contains(ty) {\n476:                 return;\n477:             }\n478:             seen.push(ty.clone());\n479:             found.push(ty.clone());\n480:             for arg in args {\n481:                 reachable_enums(enums, arg, seen, found);\n482:             }\n483:             for (_, payload) in crate::ty::variants_of(enums, name, args) {\n484:                 if let Some(p) = payload {\n485:                     reachable_enums(enums, &p, seen, found);\n486:                 }\n487:             }\n488:         }\n489:         _ => {}\n490:     }\n491: }\n492: \n493: /// Every node in the tree, `t` itself included, in no particular order. The backends each walk\n494: /// the tree their own way, gathering what their own target needs; this is for the questions\n495: /// that are the same on every target.\n496: fn each_node(t: &Tir, f: &mut impl FnMut(&Tir)) {\n497:     f(t);\n498:     match &t.kind {\n499:         Kind::Str(_)\n500:         | Kind::Int(_)\n501:         | Kind::Var(_)\n502:         | Kind::Local(_)\n503:         | Kind::Input\n504:         | Kind::Inputs\n505:         | Kind::Lines => {}\n506:         Kind::VecLit(items) => items.iter().for_each(|i| each_node(i, f)),\n507:         Kind::RecordLit { fields } => fields.iter().for_each(|(_, v)| each_node(v, f)),\n508:         Kind::EnumLit { payload, .. } => {\n509:             if let Some(p) = payload {\n510:                 each_node(p, f);\n511:             }\n512:         }\n513:         Kind::Call { arg, .. } => {\n514:             if let Some(a) = arg {\n515:                 each_node(a, f);\n516:             }\n517:         }\n518:         Kind::Concat(l, r)\n519:         | Kind::Compare { lhs: l, rhs: r, .. }\n520:         | Kind::Logic { lhs: l, rhs: r, .. }\n521:         | Kind::Arith { lhs: l, rhs: r, .. } => {\n522:             each_node(l, f);\n523:             each_node(r, f);\n524:         }\n525:         Kind::Not(base) => each_node(base, f),\n526:         Kind::Cond {\n527:             cond,\n528:             then,\n529:             otherwise,\n530:         } => {\n531:             each_node(cond, f);\n532:             each_node(then, f);\n533:             each_node(otherwise, f);\n534:         }\n535:         Kind::Bind { value, body, .. } => {\n536:             each_node(value, f);\n537:             each_node(body, f);\n538:         }\n539:         Kind::Map { source, body, .. } | Kind::OptMap { source, body, .. } => {\n540:             each_node(source, f);\n541:             each_node(body, f);\n542:         }\n543:         Kind::Select { source, pred, .. } => {\n544:             each_node(source, f);\n545:             each_node(pred, f);\n546:         }\n547:         Kind::Field { base, .. } | Kind::Unwrap { base } => each_node(base, f),\n548:         Kind::Builtin { arg, .. } => each_node(arg, f),\n549:         Kind::Index { base, index, .. } => {\n550:             each_node(base, f);\n551:             each_node(index, f);\n552:         }\n553:         Kind::Match { subject, arms, .. } => {\n554:             each_node(subject, f);\n555:             for arm in arms {\n556:                 if let Some(g) = &arm.guard {\n557:                     each_node(g, f);\n558:                 }\n559:                 each_node(&arm.body, f);\n560:             }\n561:         }\n562:     }\n563: }\n564: \n565: /// Whether `t` makes a self-call to `name` in a tail position: a call whose result is the\n566: /// function's result, with nothing left to do after it. This is what lets the js and py\n567: /// emitters lower a self-tail-recursive function to a loop, the constant-stack contract\n568: /// (kantord/toylang#141).\n569: ///\n570: /// Only `Cond` branches, a total `Match`'s arm bodies, and a `Bind`'s body put their child in\n571: /// tail position; a partial `Match` wraps every arm body in `{some: ...}`, so nothing there is\n572: /// a tail call, and a call feeding an operator (`f(x) + 1`) is a genuine recursion the contract\n573: /// does not cover.\n574: pub fn has_tail_call(name: &str, t: &Tir) -> bool {\n575:     match &t.kind {\n576:         Kind::Call { func, .. } => func == name,\n577:         Kind::Cond { then, otherwise, .. } => {\n578:             has_tail_call(name, then) || has_tail_call(name, otherwise)\n579:         }\n580:         Kind::Bind { body, .. } => has_tail_call(name, body),\n581:         Kind::Match { arms, partial, .. } if !partial => {\n582:             arms.iter().any(|a| has_tail_call(name, &a.body))\n583:         }\n584:         _ => false,\n585:     }\n586: }\n\n(End of file - total 586 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"        }\n        Kind::Select {\n            source,\n            param,\n            pred,\n        } => {\n            let base = flatten(source, program, stages);\n            stages.push(Stage::Select {\n                param: *param,\n                pred,\n            });\n            base\n        }\n        // The argument's stages first, then the callee's body inlined: its chain bottoms at\n        // its own parameter (a stream-returning function must take a stream, and linearity\n        // makes the parameter the base of whatever it returns).\n        Kind::Call { func, arg } => {\n            let f = program\n                .funcs\n                .iter()","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/src/tir.rs","text":"        }\n        Kind::Select {\n            source,\n            param,\n            pred,\n        } => {\n            let base = flatten(source, program, stages);\n            stages.push(Stage::Select {\n                param: *param,\n                pred,\n            });\n            base\n        }\n        // The argument's stages first, then the callee's body inlined: its chain bottoms at\n        // its own parameter (a stream-returning function must take a stream, and linearity\n        // makes the parameter the base of whatever it returns).\n        Kind::Call { func, arg } => {\n            let f = program\n                .funcs\n                .iter()\n                .find(|f| &f.name == func)\n                .expect(\"the checker resolved every call\");\n            // A stream-returning function must take a stream (see `signatures`), so a call\n            // reaching here -- one whose result is stream-typed -- always carries an argument.\n            let arg = arg\n                .as_deref()\n                .expect(\"a stream-returning function is never nullary\");\n            let base = flatten(arg, program, stages);\n            let param = f\n                .param\n                .as_deref()\n                .expect(\"a stream-returning function is never nullary\");\n            match flatten(&f.body, program, stages) {\n                Base::Var(name) if name == param => base,\n                _ => unreachable!(\"a stream-returning function's chain bottoms at its parameter\"),\n            }\n        }\n        _ => unreachable!(\"the checker leaves no other kind stream-typed\"),\n    }\n}\n\n/// The type question `streams_inputs` used to answer by pattern match: a `jsonlines` program\n/// whose argument is stream-typed fuses, and one whose argument is a Vec (already collected)\n/// stays an ordinary value and runs eagerly, exactly as its types say.\npub fn fusion(program: &Program) -> Option<Fusion<'_>> {\n    let Kind::Builtin {\n        which: Builtin::JsonLines,\n        arg,\n    } = &program.body.kind\n    else {\n        return None;\n    };\n    if !matches!(arg.ty, Type::Stream(_)) {\n        return None;\n    }\n    let mut stages = Vec::new();\n    let source = match flatten(arg, program, &mut stages) {\n        Base::Inputs => Source::Inputs,\n        Base::Lines => Source::Lines,\n        Base::Var(_) | Base::Local(_) => {\n            unreachable!(\"a program-level stream chain bottoms at its source\")\n        }\n    };\n    Some(Fusion { source, stages })\n}\n\n/// Every enum type the program prints that can hold another of its own type, deduplicated and\n/// in the order the walk meets them.\n///\n/// A backend writes its printer by expanding a type inline, which is exactly what does not\n/// terminate on one of these. So each gets a named function the expansion can call back into\n/// instead, and this is the list of functions to write (kantord/toylang#94). Only what is\n/// printed: a program can hold a recursive value, match on it and print an Int, and a printer\n/// nobody calls is dead code that Go, at least, would then demand an import for.\npub fn printed_recursive_enums(program: &Program) -> Vec<Type> {\n    let mut printed = vec![program.body.ty.clone()];\n    let mut collect_printed = |t: &Tir| {\n        if let Kind::Builtin {\n            which: Builtin::JsonLines,\n            arg,\n        } = &t.kind\n        {\n            printed.push(\n                runtime_elem(&arg.ty)\n                    .expect(\"jsonlines takes a Vec or a stream\")\n                    .clone(),\n            );\n        }\n    };\n    for f in &program.funcs {\n        each_node(&f.body, &mut collect_printed);\n    }\n    each_node(&program.body, &mut collect_printed);\n\n    let mut found = Vec::new();\n    let mut seen = Vec::new();\n    for ty in &printed {\n        reachable_enums(&program.enums, ty, &mut seen, &mut found);\n    }\n    found.retain(|ty| crate::ty::is_recursive(&program.enums, ty));\n    found\n}\n\n/// Append every enum type nested anywhere in `ty` to `found`, descending through payloads read\n/// from the registry rather than off the type. `seen` is what stops a recursive one: its own\n/// payload leads back to a type already visited.\nfn reachable_enums(enums: &Enums, ty: &Type, seen: &mut Vec<Type>, found: &mut Vec<Type>) {\n    match ty {\n        Type::Vec(e) | Type::Stream(e) => reachable_enums(enums, e, seen, found),\n        Type::Record(fields) => {\n            for (_, f) in fields {\n                reachable_enums(enums, f, seen, found);\n            }\n        }\n        Type::Enum { name, args, .. } => {\n            if seen.contains(ty) {\n                return;\n            }\n            seen.push(ty.clone());\n            found.push(ty.clone());\n            for arg in args {\n                reachable_enums(enums, arg, seen, found);\n            }\n            for (_, payload) in crate::ty::variants_of(enums, name, args) {\n                if let Some(p) = payload {\n                    reachable_enums(enums, &p, seen, found);\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        _ => {}\n    }\n}\n\n/// Every node in the tree, `t` itself included, in no particular order. The backends each walk\n/// the tree their own way, gathering what their own target needs; this is for the questions\n/// that are the same on every target.\nfn each_node(t: &Tir, f: &mut impl FnMut(&Tir)) {\n    f(t);\n    match &t.kind {\n        Kind::Str(_)\n        | Kind::Int(_)\n        | Kind::Var(_)\n        | Kind::Local(_)\n        | Kind::Input\n        | Kind::Inputs\n        | Kind::Lines => {}\n        Kind::VecLit(items) => items.iter().for_each(|i| each_node(i, f)),\n        Kind::RecordLit { fields } => fields.iter().for_each(|(_, v)| each_node(v, f)),\n        Kind::EnumLit { payload, .. } => {\n            if let Some(p) = payload {\n                each_node(p, f);\n            }\n        }\n        Kind::Call { arg, .. } => {\n            if let Some(a) = arg {\n                each_node(a, f);\n            }\n        }\n        Kind::Concat(l, r)\n        | Kind::Compare { lhs: l, rhs: r, .. }\n        | Kind::Logic { lhs: l, rhs: r, .. }\n        | Kind::Arith { lhs: l, rhs: r, .. } => {\n            each_node(l, f);\n            each_node(r, f);\n        }\n        Kind::Not(base) => each_node(base, f),\n        Kind::Cond {\n            cond,\n            then,\n            otherwise,\n        } => {\n            each_node(cond, f);\n            each_node(then, f);\n            each_node(otherwise, f);\n        }\n        Kind::Bind { value, body, .. } => {\n            each_node(value, f);\n            each_node(body, f);\n        }\n        Kind::Map { source, body, .. } | Kind::OptMap { source, body, .. } => {\n            each_node(source, f);\n            each_node(body, f);\n        }\n        Kind::Select { source, pred, .. } => {\n            each_node(source, f);\n            each_node(pred, f);\n        }\n        Kind::Field { base, .. } | Kind::Unwrap { base } => each_node(base, f),\n        Kind::Builtin { arg, .. } => each_node(arg, f),\n        Kind::Index { base, index, .. } => {\n            each_node(base, f);\n            each_node(index, f);\n        }\n        Kind::Match { subject, arms, .. } => {\n            each_node(subject, f);\n            for arm in arms {\n                if let Some(g) = &arm.guard {\n                    each_node(g, f);\n                }\n                each_node(&arm.body, f);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n/// Whether `t` makes a self-call to `name` in a tail position: a call whose result is the\n/// function's result, with nothing left to do after it. This is what lets the js and py\n/// emitters lower a self-tail-recursive function to a loop, the constant-stack contract\n/// (kantord/toylang#141).\n///\n/// Only `Cond` branches, a total `Match`'s arm bodies, and a `Bind`'s body put their child in\n/// tail position; a partial `Match` wraps every arm body in `{some: ...}`, so nothing there is\n/// a tail call, and a call feeding an operator (`f(x) + 1`) is a genuine recursion the contract\n/// does not cover.\npub fn has_tail_call(name: &str, t: &Tir) -> bool {\n    match &t.kind {\n        Kind::Call { func, .. } => func == name,\n        Kind::Cond { then, otherwise, .. } => {\n            has_tail_call(name, then) || has_tail_call(name, otherwise)\n        }\n        Kind::Bind { body, .. } => has_tail_call(name, body),\n        Kind::Match { arms, partial, .. } if !partial => {\n            arms.iter().any(|a| has_tail_call(name, &a.body))\n        }\n        _ => false,\n    }\n}","lineStart":360,"lineEnd":586,"totalLines":586,"truncated":false}},"title":"src/tir.rs","time":{"start":1788297813892,"end":1788297813909}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"This is getting complex. Let me understand the streaming model fully. Let me read the rest of tir.rs (flatten function) and understand how `jsonlines` fusion works, and how the backends lower it.\n\nThe key architecture:\n- Streams are `Stream<T>` types.\n- `lines` is a source with base `Lines`.\n- `map`/`select`/`projection`/`|` create stages.\n- `collect` converts stream to Vec.\n- `jsonlines` is a sink.\n\nFor `pipe_through(cmd, args)`, it takes a stream and produces a stream. It should be a stream-to-stream transform. In the flatten representation, `pipe_through` would be another Stage.\n\nLet me read the rest of tir.rs and how jsonlines fusion is lowered in each backend.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05edb19dc0018XXOu4dp6qrFur","sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","messageID":"msg_05edaf1df001E3By5wnD2OrAZ6"}}
