{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788297852280,"sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_e55bc1e01eb447f7a18c7695","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/src/lib.rs"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/src/lib.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: pub mod ast;\n2: pub mod check;\n3: pub mod emit_go;\n4: pub mod emit_jq;\n5: pub mod emit_js;\n6: pub mod emit_llvm;\n7: pub mod emit_lua;\n8: pub mod emit_py;\n9: pub mod emit_rs;\n10: pub mod emit_toylang;\n11: pub mod error;\n12: pub mod fmt_tree;\n13: pub mod input;\n14: pub mod parse;\n15: pub mod prelude;\n16: pub mod tags;\n17: pub mod tir;\n18: pub mod ty;\n19: \n20: use std::cell::RefCell;\n21: use std::io::{BufRead, Write};\n22: use std::rc::Rc;\n23: \n24: use anyhow::{Context, Result};\n25: use error::Error;\n26: use tir::Program;\n27: \n28: #[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]\n29: pub enum Backend {\n30:     Lua,\n31:     Js,\n32:     Native,\n33:     Jq,\n34:     Go,\n35:     Py,\n36:     Rust,\n37: }\n38: \n39: impl Backend {\n40:     /// The backends that must agree on every corpus program. Native and Rust each joined once\n41:     /// they could compile the whole language; until then they were kept out rather than allowed\n42:     /// to turn the harness permanently red, tracked by tests/backend_llvm.rs and\n43:     /// tests/backend_rust.rs respectively.\n44:     pub const ALL: [Backend; 7] = [\n45:         Backend::Lua,\n46:         Backend::Js,\n47:         Backend::Native,\n48:         Backend::Jq,\n49:         Backend::Go,\n50:         Backend::Py,\n51:         Backend::Rust,\n52:     ];\n53: \n54:     /// The spelling used on the command line and in a corpus case's `snapshot` list.\n55:     pub fn from_name(name: &str) -> Option<Backend> {\n56:         Backend::ALL.into_iter().find(|b| b.name() == name)\n57:     }\n58: \n59:     pub fn name(self) -> &'static str {\n60:         match self {\n61:             Backend::Lua => \"lua\",\n62:             Backend::Js => \"js\",\n63:             Backend::Native => \"native\",\n64:             Backend::Jq => \"jq\",\n65:             Backend::Go => \"go\",\n66:             Backend::Py => \"py\",\n67:             Backend::Rust => \"rust\",\n68:         }\n69:     }\n70: \n71:     pub fn emit(self, program: &Program) -> Result<String, String> {\n72:         match self {\n73:             Backend::Lua => Ok(emit_lua::emit(program)),\n74:             Backend::Js => Ok(emit_js::emit(program)),\n75:             Backend::Native => emit_llvm::to_ir(program),\n76:             Backend::Jq => emit_jq::emit(program),\n77:             Backend::Go => Ok(emit_go::emit(program)),\n78:             Backend::Py => Ok(emit_py::emit(program)),\n79:             Backend::Rust => Ok(emit_rs::emit(program)),\n80:         }\n81:     }\n82: }\n83: \n84: pub fn compile(src: &str) -> Result<Program, Error> {\n85:     let mut file = parse::parse(src)?;\n86:     prelude::inject(&mut file);\n87:     check::check(&file)\n88: }\n89: \n90: /// Parses `src` and re-renders it in the canonical toylang style, without checking or injecting\n91: /// the prelude -- a formatter has to work on a program that does not type-check, and it must\n92: /// never print the prelude definitions `compile` splices in for its own purposes.\n93: pub fn fmt(src: &str) -> Result<String, Error> {\n94:     emit_toylang::format_source(src)\n95: }\n96: \n97: pub fn run(src: &str) -> Result<String> {\n98:     run_on(src, None, Backend::Lua)\n99: }\n100: \n101: pub fn run_with_input(src: &str, stdin: Option<&str>) -> Result<String> {\n102:     run_on(src, stdin, Backend::Lua)\n103: }\n104: \n105: /// Whether the generated code for `program` reads `inputs` for itself, one record at a time,\n106: /// rather than needing the whole thing parsed and handed over up front. A type question now,\n107: /// not a shape guess: exactly the programs `tir::fusion` reads from `inputs` -- every backend\n108: /// has a fused loop to emit. An aggregate like `length(collect(inputs))` still has no way to\n109: /// stream no matter which backend runs it. Public so `main.rs` can decide, before it has read\n110: /// anything, whether to drain real stdin itself or hand it to `run_on` untouched.\n111: pub fn streams_inputs(program: &tir::Program) -> bool {\n112:     matches!(tir::fusion(program), Some(f) if f.source == tir::Source::Inputs)\n113: }\n114: \n115: /// Compile and run, capturing what the program printed -- except when `stdin` is `None` and\n116: /// `streams_inputs` says the backend reads `inputs` for itself, in which case the real stdin and\n117: /// stdout are connected straight through (`Feed::Live`) and there is nothing left to capture.\n118: /// Capturing otherwise keeps the tests to one call: it also means output is held in memory, which\n119: /// is fine for every program whose result has statically known length, and is exactly the case\n120: /// `Feed::Live` exists for on the ones that no longer do.\n121: pub fn run_on(src: &str, stdin: Option<&str>, backend: Backend) -> Result<String> {\n122:     let program = compile(src)?;\n123: \n124:     // `stdin.is_none()` is the same convention `uses_lines` already uses below: nothing was\n125:     // supplied, which only happens on the real command line, never from a test fixture. Only\n126:     // then is there real live stdin worth handing straight to a subprocess backend rather than\n127:     // something already sitting in memory to validate up front.\n128:     let live_inputs = stdin.is_none() && streams_inputs(&program);\n129:     // Lua is the one backend with no subprocess of its own: `run_lua` itself decides how\n130:     // `inputs` reaches the running chunk, by injecting either a pre-populated global or a\n131:     // per-call function, and the emitted source commits to one of those two shapes purely from\n132:     // `tir::fusion`, independent of whether a fixture or the real command line supplied the\n133:     // bytes. So unlike `live_inputs` above, this also has to hold for a fixture-fed test, or\n134:     // the host would inject the global while the fused source calls the function.\n135:     let lua_fused = matches!(backend, Backend::Lua) && streams_inputs(&program);\n136: \n137:     // The input is checked against the declared type once, here, rather than by each backend.\n138:     // What a backend receives has already been parsed, so no backend re-decides what is valid.\n139:     let value = match (&program.input, stdin) {\n140:         (Some(ty), Some(text)) => {\n141:             let value: serde_json::Value = serde_json::from_str(text)?;\n142:             input::validate(&program.enums, &value, ty, \"input\").map_err(anyhow::Error::msg)?;\n143:             Some(value)\n144:         }\n145:         (Some(ty), None) => anyhow::bail!(\"this program reads input, of type {ty}\"),\n146:         (None, _) => None,\n147:     };\n148: \n149:     // `inputs` is eager like `input`, not incremental like `lines`, so it normally goes through\n150:     // the same validate-then-re-serialize step: every backend receives canonical bytes, one\n151:     // compact JSON value per line, rather than whatever formatting the original text happened to\n152:     // use. `live_inputs` and `lua_fused` are the exceptions -- nothing is precomputed for them,\n153:     // because the whole point of a fused loop is not reading the whole stream before the first\n154:     // record is handled. Validation still happens record-by-record on the host: `run_lua`'s\n155:     // `next_input` function does it for the Lua backend it is embedded in, and `Feed::LiveInputs`\n156:     // below does it for every other, subprocess-based backend, since generated code cannot be\n157:     // trusted to reimplement `input::validate` correctly in six different target languages.\n158:     let inputs_values = if live_inputs || lua_fused {\n159:         None\n160:     } else {\n161:         match (&program.inputs, stdin) {\n162:             (Some(elem_ty), Some(text)) => {\n163:                 let mut values = Vec::new();\n164:                 for line in text.lines().filter(|l| !l.trim().is_empty()) {\n165:                     let value: serde_json::Value = serde_json::from_str(line)?;\n166:                     input::validate(&program.enums, &value, elem_ty, \"inputs\")\n167:                         .map_err(anyhow::Error::msg)?;\n168:                     values.push(value);\n169:                 }\n170:                 Some(values)\n171:             }\n172:             (Some(elem_ty), None) => {\n173:                 anyhow::bail!(\"this program reads inputs, of type Vec<{elem_ty}>\");\n174:             }\n175:             (None, _) => None,\n176:         }\n177:     };\n178: \n179:     // What a backend's stdin should be connected to. `input` always has known bytes: the\n180:     // re-serialized, already-validated value. `lines` has known bytes only when a caller (a\n181:     // corpus fixture, most often) supplied them directly -- and when it does, they are piped in\n182:     // verbatim rather than pre-split, so a backend's own splitting genuinely runs against them\n183:     // rather than being bypassed by the test that is supposed to exercise it. Only when nothing\n184:     // was supplied, which is the real command-line case, does `lines` connect the real stdin\n185:     // straight through, with no Rust-side buffering in between -- the one thing that would make\n186:     // its streaming indistinguishable from having read everything first. A live, fused `inputs`\n187:     // program has no type to lean on the way `lines` does (raw text is always valid `Str`), so it\n188:     // cannot connect the real stdin straight through: `Feed::LiveInputs` reads and validates one\n189:     // record at a time itself and forwards only what passes, real streaming with no read-it-all-\n190:     // first buffering, but through Rust rather than direct fd inheritance. The Lua backend is the\n191:     // one exception -- it validates through its own `next_input` function instead, direct from\n192:     // `Feed::Live`, since it runs embedded rather than as a subprocess with a pipe to mediate.\n193:     // A fused Lua program run against a fixture is not live, but still needs the raw bytes\n194:     // verbatim rather than the eager re-serialized form, since `run_lua`'s injected function does\n195:     // its own splitting the same way `lines` always has.\n196:     let feed = if program.uses_lines {\n197:         match stdin {\n198:             Some(text) => Feed::Text(text.to_string()),\n199:             None => Feed::Live,\n200:         }\n201:     } else if live_inputs {\n202:         live_inputs_feed(&program, backend)\n203:     } else if lua_fused {\n204:         Feed::Text(stdin.map(str::to_string).unwrap_or_default())\n205:     } else if let Some(values) = &inputs_values {\n206:         Feed::Text(\n207:             values\n208:                 .iter()\n209:                 .map(|v| v.to_string())\n210:                 .collect::<Vec<_>>()\n211:                 .join(\"\\n\"),\n212:         )\n213:     } else {\n214:         Feed::Text(value.as_ref().map(|v| v.to_string()).unwrap_or_default())\n215:     };\n216: \n217:     match backend {\n218:         Backend::Lua => run_lua(\n219:             &emit_lua::emit(&program),\n220:             &program.enums,\n221:             value.as_ref(),\n222:             inputs_values.as_ref(),\n223:             program.inputs.as_ref(),\n224:             &feed,\n225:         ),\n226:         Backend::Js => run_node(&emit_js::emit(&program), &feed),\n227:         Backend::Jq => run_jq(\n228:             &emit_jq::emit(&program).map_err(anyhow::Error::msg)?,\n229:             JqInvocation {\n230:                 has_value: value.is_some(),\n231:                 raw: program.body.ty == ty::Type::Str,\n232:                 uses_lines: program.uses_lines,\n233:             },\n234:             &feed,\n235:         ),\n236:         Backend::Go => run_go(&emit_go::emit(&program), &feed),\n237:         Backend::Py => run_py(&emit_py::emit(&program), &feed),\n238:         Backend::Native => {\n239:             let dir = tempfile::tempdir()?;\n240:             let exe = dir.path().join(\"program\");\n241:             link(&program, &exe)?;\n242:             run_binary(&exe, &feed)\n243:         }\n244:         Backend::Rust => {\n245:             let dir = tempfile::tempdir()?;\n246:             let exe = dir.path().join(\"program\");\n247:             link_rust(&emit_rs::emit(&program), &exe)?;\n248:             run_binary(&exe, &feed)\n249:         }\n250:     }\n251: }\n252: \n253: /// The feed for a live, fused `inputs` program: real stdin, but validated. Lua is the exception,\n254: /// since `run_lua`'s own `next_input` function validates directly off `Feed::Live` rather than\n255: /// needing `Feed::LiveInputs` to mediate a pipe it has no subprocess to write into.\n256: fn live_inputs_feed(program: &Program, backend: Backend) -> Feed {\n257:     if matches!(backend, Backend::Lua) {\n258:         Feed::Live\n259:     } else {\n260:         Feed::LiveInputs(\n261:             program\n262:                 .inputs\n263:                 .clone()\n264:                 .expect(\"live_inputs implies an inputs type\"),\n265:             program.enums.clone(),\n266:         )\n267:     }\n268: }\n269: \n270: /// What a subprocess backend's stdin is connected to.\n271: enum Feed {\n272:     /// Known bytes, written through a pipe: `input`'s re-serialized value, a `lines` program's\n273:     /// fixture text verbatim, or nothing for a program that reads no stdin at all.\n274:     Text(String),\n275:     /// The real process stdin, inherited rather than piped. Reachable only for a `lines`\n276:     /// program run with no fixture supplied.\n277:     Live,\n278:     /// The real process stdin, read and validated one record at a time against the element type,\n279:     /// then forwarded through a pipe -- real streaming, but mediated by the host rather than\n280:     /// inherited directly, which is what lets `input::validate` see every record before a\n281:     /// subprocess backend's own, untrusted parse of it does. Reachable only for a live, fused\n282:     /// `inputs` program on a backend other than Lua (see `write_to`).\n283:     LiveInputs(ty::Type, ty::Enums),\n284: }\n285: \n286: impl Feed {\n287:     fn stdio(&self) -> std::process::Stdio {\n288:         match self {\n289:             Feed::Text(_) | Feed::LiveInputs(..) => std::process::Stdio::piped(),\n290:             Feed::Live => std::process::Stdio::inherit(),\n291:         }\n292:     }\n293: \n294:     fn write_to(&self, child: &mut std::process::Child) -> Result<()> {\n295:         match self {\n296:             Feed::Text(text) => {\n297:                 child\n298:                     .stdin\n299:                     .take()\n300:                     .expect(\"piped\")\n301:                     .write_all(text.as_bytes())?;\n302:             }\n303:             Feed::Live => {}\n304:             Feed::LiveInputs(elem_ty, enums) => {\n305:                 let mut stdin = child.stdin.take().expect(\"piped\");\n306:                 for line in std::io::stdin().lock().lines() {\n307:                     let line = line?;\n308:                     if line.trim().is_empty() {\n309:                         continue;\n310:                     }\n311:                     let value: serde_json::Value = serde_json::from_str(&line)?;\n312:                     if let Err(msg) = input::validate(enums, &value, elem_ty, \"inputs\") {\n313:                         // Close the pipe so the child sees EOF and can finish printing whatever\n314:                         // it already validly received, then wait for it before reporting the\n315:                         // refusal -- otherwise this leaves an unreaped child behind.\n316:                         drop(stdin);\n317:                         child.wait()?;\n318:                         return Err(anyhow::Error::msg(msg));\n319:                     }\n320:                     writeln!(stdin, \"{line}\")?;\n321:                 }\n322:             }\n323:         }\n324:         Ok(())\n325:     }\n326: }\n327: \n328: /// Compile to a native executable at `out`.\n329: ///\n330: /// LLVM produces an object file, which is not a program, so this shells out to `cc` for the\n331: /// link. That is a toolchain requirement the Lua backend does not have, since mlua vendors its\n332: /// interpreter.\n333: pub fn link(program: &Program, out: &std::path::Path) -> Result<()> {\n334:     let dir = tempfile::tempdir()?;\n335:     let object = dir.path().join(\"program.o\");\n336:     emit_llvm::compile_to_object(program, &object).map_err(anyhow::Error::msg)?;\n337: \n338:     // The runtime is compiled alongside rather than shipped as a library, which keeps the build\n339:     // to one `cc` call and means there is nothing to install.\n340:     let runtime = dir.path().join(\"toylang.c\");\n341:     std::fs::write(&runtime, emit_llvm::RUNTIME_C)?;\n342: \n343:     let status = std::process::Command::new(\"cc\")\n344:         .arg(&object)\n345:         .arg(&runtime)\n346:         .arg(\"-o\")\n347:         .arg(out)\n348:         .status()\n349:         .context(\"could not run `cc`\")?;\n350:     if !status.success() {\n351:         anyhow::bail!(\"cc failed to link: {status}\");\n352:     }\n353:     Ok(())\n354: }\n355: \n356: /// Compile Rust source to an executable at `out`. One `rustc` call on one self-contained file,\n357: /// no external crate and no Cargo project, the same reason `link` above is one `cc` call: the\n358: /// generated source depends on nothing this compiler did not already assume was installed.\n359: /// Public for the same reason as `link`: `tests/backend_rust.rs` calls it directly, separately\n360: /// from running the result, so a genuine codegen gap (`rustc` rejects the source) is told apart\n361: /// from a legitimate runtime refusal (`rustc` succeeds, the binary exits non-zero).\n362: pub fn link_rust(source: &str, out: &std::path::Path) -> Result<()> {\n363:     let dir = tempfile::tempdir()?;\n364:     let src = dir.path().join(\"program.rs\");\n365:     std::fs::write(&src, source)?;\n366: \n367:     let result = std::process::Command::new(\"rustc\")\n368:         .arg(\"--edition\")\n369:         .arg(\"2021\")\n370:         .arg(&src)\n371:         .arg(\"-o\")\n372:         .arg(out)\n373:         .output()\n374:         .context(\"could not run `rustc`\")?;\n375:     if !result.status.success() {\n376:         anyhow::bail!(\n377:             \"rustc failed to compile: {}\",\n378:             String::from_utf8_lossy(&result.stderr)\n379:         );\n380:     }\n381:     Ok(())\n382: }\n383: \n384: /// Runs `cmd` (already given whatever args the caller needs) connected to `feed`, and returns\n385: /// what it printed.\n386: ///\n387: /// When `feed` is `Feed::Live` or `Feed::LiveInputs` there is no test harness reading the result\n388: /// back: stdout and stderr are inherited straight through to the real terminal or pipe instead of\n389: /// being captured, which is what lets a program that streams actually show output as it produces\n390: /// it rather than all at once when it exits. `Ok(String::new())` reflects that nothing is left to\n391: /// hand back -- it already went to the real stdout directly. `feed.write_to` reports a validation\n392: /// refusal for `Feed::LiveInputs` as an `Err` before this function's own `child.wait()` runs, so\n393: /// that path never reaches here at all.\n394: fn run_subprocess(mut cmd: std::process::Command, label: &str, feed: &Feed) -> Result<String> {\n395:     let live = matches!(feed, Feed::Live | Feed::LiveInputs(..));\n396:     let mut child = cmd\n397:         .stdin(feed.stdio())\n398:         .stdout(if live {\n399:             std::process::Stdio::inherit()\n400:         } else {\n401:             std::process::Stdio::piped()\n402:         })\n403:         .stderr(if live {\n404:             std::process::Stdio::inherit()\n405:         } else {\n406:             std::process::Stdio::piped()\n407:         })\n408:         .spawn()\n409:         .with_context(|| format!(\"could not run `{label}`\"))?;\n410: \n411:     feed.write_to(&mut child)?;\n412: \n413:     if live {\n414:         let status = child.wait()?;\n415:         if !status.success() {\n416:             anyhow::bail!(\"{label} failed\");\n417:         }\n418:         return Ok(String::new());\n419:     }\n420: \n421:     let out = child.wait_with_output()?;\n422:     if !out.status.success() {\n423:         anyhow::bail!(\"{label} failed: {}\", String::from_utf8_lossy(&out.stderr));\n424:     }\n425:     Ok(String::from_utf8(out.stdout)?)\n426: }\n427: \n428: fn run_binary(exe: &std::path::Path, feed: &Feed) -> Result<String> {\n429:     run_subprocess(\n430:         std::process::Command::new(exe),\n431:         \"the compiled program\",\n432:         feed,\n433:     )\n434: }\n435: \n436: /// jq puts stdin in `.`, so a program that reads no input runs with `-n` rather than waiting on\n437: /// a terminal. `has_value` rather than the `Feed` itself, since a `lines` program run live\n438: /// (`Feed::Live`) still has real stdin coming and must not get `-n` on that account.\n439: /// The three facts about a program that shape a jq invocation's flags. Each is derived from\n440: /// the compiled program, not a caller identity -- the fn-params-excessive-bools finding on the\n441: /// old three-bool signature was escalated, and the settled answer was this struct: the facts\n442: /// travel under their names.\n443: struct JqInvocation {\n444:     has_value: bool,\n445:     raw: bool,\n446:     uses_lines: bool,\n447: }\n448: \n449: fn run_jq(source: &str, inv: JqInvocation, feed: &Feed) -> Result<String> {\n450:     let JqInvocation {\n451:         has_value,\n452:         raw,\n453:         uses_lines,\n454:     } = inv;\n455:     let mut cmd = std::process::Command::new(\"jq\");\n456:     // jq's stdout is fully buffered rather than line-buffered whenever it is not a terminal, the\n457:     // same as any other libc stdio program, so a filter over `inputs` piped into another process\n458:     // would otherwise sit on every result until the run ends. `run_subprocess` only inherits\n459:     // stdout instead of capturing it for a live run, so this is still what makes that case show a\n460:     // result as soon as jq produces one rather than at exit.\n461:     cmd.arg(\"--unbuffered\");\n462:     cmd.arg(\"-c\");\n463:     // `-r` decides from the runtime value, so it would print a present Opt<Str> raw and an\n464:     // absent one as the word null. The rule here is the type's, as on every other backend.\n465:     if raw {\n466:         cmd.arg(\"-r\");\n467:     }\n468:     if !has_value && !uses_lines {\n469:         cmd.arg(\"-n\");\n470:     }\n471:     // Raw-input mode, needed for `[ inputs ]` to read lines rather than JSON, and forced by the\n472:     // checker to be the only way `lines` is used in this invocation: mixing it with `input` was\n473:     // rejected there, because `-R` changes what the whole invocation means, not just one call.\n474:     // `-n` here is not \"no input coming\"; it is what raw-input mode needs regardless, since\n475:     // `[ inputs ]` reads for itself rather than through the implicit `.` a normal run would use.\n476:     if uses_lines {\n477:         cmd.arg(\"-R\").arg(\"-n\");\n478:     }\n479:     cmd.arg(source);\n480:     run_subprocess(cmd, \"jq\", feed)\n481: }\n482: \n483: fn run_lua(\n484:     source: &str,\n485:     enums: &ty::Enums,\n486:     value: Option<&serde_json::Value>,\n487:     inputs_values: Option<&Vec<serde_json::Value>>,\n488:     inputs_elem_ty: Option<&ty::Type>,\n489:     feed: &Feed,\n490: ) -> Result<String> {\n491:     let lua = mlua::Lua::new();\n492:     if let Some(value) = value {\n493:         lua.globals()\n494:             .set(emit_lua::INPUT, input::to_lua(&lua, value)?)?;\n495:     }\n496:     if let Some(values) = inputs_values {\n497:         let array = serde_json::Value::Array(values.clone());\n498:         lua.globals()\n499:             .set(emit_lua::INPUTS, input::to_lua(&lua, &array)?)?;\n500:     } else if let Some(elem_ty) = inputs_elem_ty {\n501:         // `inputs_values` is `None` here for one of two reasons: the program does not read\n502:         // `inputs` at all (then `inputs_elem_ty` is also `None`, and neither branch runs), or\n503:         // `run_on` recognized this as a fused program and skipped precomputing the whole Vec on\n504:         // purpose (`live_inputs` or `lua_fused` there). mlua is embedded in this same process, so\n505:         // there is no separate JSON parser to write in Lua source the way Rust's own is -- the\n506:         // generated source just calls this function for the next already-validated, already-\n507:         // converted record. Reading from `feed` rather than always the real stdin is what keeps\n508:         // this correct for a fixture-tested fused program too: `feed` is `Feed::Text` there, the\n509:         // same raw bytes a subprocess backend would have been piped.\n510:         let elem_ty = elem_ty.clone();\n511:         let enums = enums.clone();\n512:         let reader: RefCell<Box<dyn std::io::BufRead>> = RefCell::new(match feed {\n513:             Feed::Live => Box::new(std::io::BufReader::new(std::io::stdin())),\n514:             Feed::Text(text) => Box::new(std::io::Cursor::new(text.clone().into_bytes())),\n515:             // `run_on` never builds this feed for the Lua backend: `live_inputs` there stays\n516:             // `Feed::Live`, since this function's own reader validates each record itself.\n517:             Feed::LiveInputs(..) => unreachable!(\"run_on keeps the Lua backend on Feed::Live\"),\n518:         });\n519:         let next_input = lua.create_function(move |lua, ()| {\n520:             use std::io::BufRead;\n521:             loop {\n522:                 let mut line = String::new();\n523:                 let n = reader\n524:                     .borrow_mut()\n525:                     .read_line(&mut line)\n526:                     .map_err(mlua::Error::external)?;\n527:                 if n == 0 {\n528:                     return Ok(mlua::Value::Nil);\n529:                 }\n530:                 let line = line.strip_suffix('\\n').unwrap_or(&line);\n531:                 if line.trim().is_empty() {\n532:                     continue;\n533:                 }\n534:                 let value: serde_json::Value =\n535:                     serde_json::from_str(line).map_err(mlua::Error::external)?;\n536:                 input::validate(&enums, &value, &elem_ty, \"inputs\")\n537:                     .map_err(mlua::Error::RuntimeError)?;\n538:                 return input::to_lua(lua, &value);\n539:             }\n540:         })?;\n541:         lua.globals().set(emit_lua::NEXT_INPUT, next_input)?;\n542:     }\n543:     // mlua is embedded in this same process, so `io.lines()` in the emitted source reads the\n544:     // real stdin directly -- verified against a live pipe, and exactly what a `lines` program\n545:     // run for real should do. A fixture is different: cargo test runs many tests concurrently\n546:     // in one process, so redirecting the process's own fd 0 would race with all of them, and is\n547:     // not done. Instead the fixture is written to a file of its own and `io.lines` is pointed at\n548:     // it, which keeps Lua's own real line-splitting in the loop rather than reimplementing it in\n549:     // Rust and only testing that reimplementation.\n550:     let _fixture_dir;\n551:     if let Feed::Text(text) = feed {\n552:         let dir = tempfile::tempdir()?;\n553:         let path = dir.path().join(\"stdin.txt\");\n554:         std::fs::write(&path, text)?;\n555:         let path = path\n556:             .to_str()\n557:             .expect(\"a temp path is valid UTF-8\")\n558:             .to_string();\n559:         let io: mlua::Table = lua.globals().get(\"io\")?;\n560:         let real_lines: mlua::Function = io.get(\"lines\")?;\n561:         let fixture =\n562:             lua.create_function(move |_, ()| real_lines.call::<mlua::Value>(path.clone()))?;\n563:         io.set(\"lines\", fixture)?;\n564:         _fixture_dir = Some(dir);\n565:     }\n566: \n567:     // A live run has no test harness reading the result back, the same distinction\n568:     // `run_subprocess` makes for the OS-process backends: `print` writes straight to the real\n569:     // stdout and flushes per call instead of buffering into a string only handed back once the\n570:     // whole chunk finishes.\n571:     let live = matches!(feed, Feed::Live);\n572:     let captured = Rc::new(RefCell::new(String::new()));\n573:     let sink = Rc::clone(&captured);\n574:     let print = lua.create_function(move |_, s: String| {\n575:         if live {\n576:             let mut stdout = std::io::stdout();\n577:             writeln!(stdout, \"{s}\").map_err(mlua::Error::external)?;\n578:             stdout.flush().map_err(mlua::Error::external)?;\n579:         } else {\n580:             sink.borrow_mut().push_str(&s);\n581:             sink.borrow_mut().push('\\n');\n582:         }\n583:         Ok(())\n584:     })?;\n585:     lua.globals().set(\"print\", print)?;\n586: \n587:     lua.load(source).exec()?;\n588: \n589:     if live {\n590:         return Ok(String::new());\n591:     }\n592:     let out = captured.borrow().clone();\n593:     Ok(out)\n594: }\n595: \n596: /// Runs through `python3`. Like `node`, this is an interpreter that has to be on the machine\n597: /// rather than one vendored into the build.\n598: fn run_py(source: &str, feed: &Feed) -> Result<String> {\n599:     let dir = tempfile::tempdir()?;\n600:     let path = dir.path().join(\"program.py\");\n601:     std::fs::write(&path, source)?;\n602: \n603:     let mut cmd = std::process::Command::new(\"python3\");\n604:     cmd.arg(&path);\n605:     run_subprocess(cmd, \"python3\", feed)\n606: }\n607: \n608: /// Runs through `go run`, which compiles and executes in one step. Go has no interpreter, so\n609: /// this is the second backend that needs a real toolchain; like `node` and `cc`, a missing one\n610: /// is an error rather than a skipped backend.\n611: fn run_go(source: &str, feed: &Feed) -> Result<String> {\n612:     let dir = tempfile::tempdir()?;\n613:     let path = dir.path().join(\"main.go\");\n614:     std::fs::write(&path, source)?;\n615: \n616:     let mut cmd = std::process::Command::new(\"go\");\n617:     cmd.arg(\"run\").arg(&path);\n618:     run_subprocess(cmd, \"go\", feed)\n619: }\n620: \n621: /// Runs through `node`, which must be present. A missing toolchain is an error rather than a\n622: /// quietly skipped backend: a report that says two backends agreed when only one ran is worse\n623: /// than no report.\n624: fn run_node(source: &str, feed: &Feed) -> Result<String> {\n625:     let dir = tempfile::tempdir()?;\n626:     let path = dir.path().join(\"program.js\");\n627:     std::fs::write(&path, source)?;\n628: \n629:     let mut cmd = std::process::Command::new(\"node\");\n630:     cmd.arg(&path);\n631:     run_subprocess(cmd, \"node\", feed)\n632: }\n633: \n634: impl std::error::Error for Error {}\n\n(End of file - total 634 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"pub mod ast;\npub mod check;\npub mod emit_go;\npub mod emit_jq;\npub mod emit_js;\npub mod emit_llvm;\npub mod emit_lua;\npub mod emit_py;\npub mod emit_rs;\npub mod emit_toylang;\npub mod error;\npub mod fmt_tree;\npub mod input;\npub mod parse;\npub mod prelude;\npub mod tags;\npub mod tir;\npub mod ty;\n\nuse std::cell::RefCell;","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/src/lib.rs","text":"pub mod ast;\npub mod check;\npub mod emit_go;\npub mod emit_jq;\npub mod emit_js;\npub mod emit_llvm;\npub mod emit_lua;\npub mod emit_py;\npub mod emit_rs;\npub mod emit_toylang;\npub mod error;\npub mod fmt_tree;\npub mod input;\npub mod parse;\npub mod prelude;\npub mod tags;\npub mod tir;\npub mod ty;\n\nuse std::cell::RefCell;\nuse std::io::{BufRead, Write};\nuse std::rc::Rc;\n\nuse anyhow::{Context, Result};\nuse error::Error;\nuse tir::Program;\n\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]\npub enum Backend {\n    Lua,\n    Js,\n    Native,\n    Jq,\n    Go,\n    Py,\n    Rust,\n}\n\nimpl Backend {\n    /// The backends that must agree on every corpus program. Native and Rust each joined once\n    /// they could compile the whole language; until then they were kept out rather than allowed\n    /// to turn the harness permanently red, tracked by tests/backend_llvm.rs and\n    /// tests/backend_rust.rs respectively.\n    pub const ALL: [Backend; 7] = [\n        Backend::Lua,\n        Backend::Js,\n        Backend::Native,\n        Backend::Jq,\n        Backend::Go,\n        Backend::Py,\n        Backend::Rust,\n    ];\n\n    /// The spelling used on the command line and in a corpus case's `snapshot` list.\n    pub fn from_name(name: &str) -> Option<Backend> {\n        Backend::ALL.into_iter().find(|b| b.name() == name)\n    }\n\n    pub fn name(self) -> &'static str {\n        match self {\n            Backend::Lua => \"lua\",\n            Backend::Js => \"js\",\n            Backend::Native => \"native\",\n            Backend::Jq => \"jq\",\n            Backend::Go => \"go\",\n            Backend::Py => \"py\",\n            Backend::Rust => \"rust\",\n        }\n    }\n\n    pub fn emit(self, program: &Program) -> Result<String, String> {\n        match self {\n            Backend::Lua => Ok(emit_lua::emit(program)),\n            Backend::Js => Ok(emit_js::emit(program)),\n            Backend::Native => emit_llvm::to_ir(program),\n            Backend::Jq => emit_jq::emit(program),\n            Backend::Go => Ok(emit_go::emit(program)),\n            Backend::Py => Ok(emit_py::emit(program)),\n            Backend::Rust => Ok(emit_rs::emit(program)),\n        }\n    }\n}\n\npub fn compile(src: &str) -> Result<Program, Error> {\n    let mut file = parse::parse(src)?;\n    prelude::inject(&mut file);\n    check::check(&file)\n}\n\n/// Parses `src` and re-renders it in the canonical toylang style, without checking or injecting\n/// the prelude -- a formatter has to work on a program that does not type-check, and it must\n/// never print the prelude definitions `compile` splices in for its own purposes.\npub fn fmt(src: &str) -> Result<String, Error> {\n    emit_toylang::format_source(src)\n}\n\npub fn run(src: &str) -> Result<String> {\n    run_on(src, None, Backend::Lua)\n}\n\npub fn run_with_input(src: &str, stdin: Option<&str>) -> Result<String> {\n    run_on(src, stdin, Backend::Lua)\n}\n\n/// Whether the generated code for `program` reads `inputs` for itself, one record at a time,\n/// rather than needing the whole thing parsed and handed over up front. A type question now,\n/// not a shape guess: exactly the programs `tir::fusion` reads from `inputs` -- every backend\n/// has a fused loop to emit. An aggregate like `length(collect(inputs))` still has no way to\n/// stream no matter which backend runs it. Public so `main.rs` can decide, before it has read\n/// anything, whether to drain real stdin itself or hand it to `run_on` untouched.\npub fn streams_inputs(program: &tir::Program) -> bool {\n    matches!(tir::fusion(program), Some(f) if f.source == tir::Source::Inputs)\n}\n\n/// Compile and run, capturing what the program printed -- except when `stdin` is `None` and\n/// `streams_inputs` says the backend reads `inputs` for itself, in which case the real stdin and\n/// stdout are connected straight through (`Feed::Live`) and there is nothing left to capture.\n/// Capturing otherwise keeps the tests to one call: it also means output is held in memory, which\n/// is fine for every program whose result has statically known length, and is exactly the case\n/// `Feed::Live` exists for on the ones that no longer do.\npub fn run_on(src: &str, stdin: Option<&str>, backend: Backend) -> Result<String> {\n    let program = compile(src)?;\n\n    // `stdin.is_none()` is the same convention `uses_lines` already uses below: nothing was\n    // supplied, which only happens on the real command line, never from a test fixture. Only\n    // then is there real live stdin worth handing straight to a subprocess backend rather than\n    // something already sitting in memory to validate up front.\n    let live_inputs = stdin.is_none() && streams_inputs(&program);\n    // Lua is the one backend with no subprocess of its own: `run_lua` itself decides how\n    // `inputs` reaches the running chunk, by injecting either a pre-populated global or a\n    // per-call function, and the emitted source commits to one of those two shapes purely from\n    // `tir::fusion`, independent of whether a fixture or the real command line supplied the\n    // bytes. So unlike `live_inputs` above, this also has to hold for a fixture-fed test, or\n    // the host would inject the global while the fused source calls the function.\n    let lua_fused = matches!(backend, Backend::Lua) && streams_inputs(&program);\n\n    // The input is checked against the declared type once, here, rather than by each backend.\n    // What a backend receives has already been parsed, so no backend re-decides what is valid.\n    let value = match (&program.input, stdin) {\n        (Some(ty), Some(text)) => {\n            let value: serde_json::Value = serde_json::from_str(text)?;\n            input::validate(&program.enums, &value, ty, \"input\").map_err(anyhow::Error::msg)?;\n            Some(value)\n        }\n        (Some(ty), None) => anyhow::bail!(\"this program reads input, of type {ty}\"),\n        (None, _) => None,\n    };\n\n    // `inputs` is eager like `input`, not incremental like `lines`, so it normally goes through\n    // the same validate-then-re-serialize step: every backend receives canonical bytes, one\n    // compact JSON value per line, rather than whatever formatting the original text happened to\n    // use. `live_inputs` and `lua_fused` are the exceptions -- nothing is precomputed for them,\n    // because the whole point of a fused loop is not reading the whole stream before the first\n    // record is handled. Validation still happens record-by-record on the host: `run_lua`'s\n    // `next_input` function does it for the Lua backend it is embedded in, and `Feed::LiveInputs`\n    // below does it for every other, subprocess-based backend, since generated code cannot be\n    // trusted to reimplement `input::validate` correctly in six different target languages.\n    let inputs_values = if live_inputs || lua_fused {\n        None\n    } else {\n        match (&program.inputs, stdin) {\n            (Some(elem_ty), Some(text)) => {\n                let mut values = Vec::new();\n                for line in text.lines().filter(|l| !l.trim().is_empty()) {\n                    let value: serde_json::Value = serde_json::from_str(line)?;\n                    input::validate(&program.enums, &value, elem_ty, \"inputs\")\n                        .map_err(anyhow::Error::msg)?;\n                    values.push(value);\n                }\n                Some(values)\n            }\n            (Some(elem_ty), None) => {\n                anyhow::bail!(\"this program reads inputs, of type Vec<{elem_ty}>\");\n            }\n            (None, _) => None,\n        }\n    };\n\n    // What a backend's stdin should be connected to. `input` always has known bytes: the\n    // re-serialized, already-validated value. `lines` has known bytes only when a caller (a\n    // corpus fixture, most often) supplied them directly -- and when it does, they are piped in\n    // verbatim rather than pre-split, so a backend's own splitting genuinely runs against them\n    // rather than being bypassed by the test that is supposed to exercise it. Only when nothing\n    // was supplied, which is the real command-line case, does `lines` connect the real stdin\n    // straight through, with no Rust-side buffering in between -- the one thing that would make\n    // its streaming indistinguishable from having read everything first. A live, fused `inputs`\n    // program has no type to lean on the way `lines` does (raw text is always valid `Str`), so it\n    // cannot connect the real stdin straight through: `Feed::LiveInputs` reads and validates one\n    // record at a time itself and forwards only what passes, real streaming with no read-it-all-\n    // first buffering, but through Rust rather than direct fd inheritance. The Lua backend is the\n    // one exception -- it validates through its own `next_input` function instead, direct from\n    // `Feed::Live`, since it runs embedded rather than as a subprocess with a pipe to mediate.\n    // A fused Lua program run against a fixture is not live, but still needs the raw bytes\n    // verbatim rather than the eager re-serialized form, since `run_lua`'s injected function does\n    // its own splitting the same way `lines` always has.\n    let feed = if program.uses_lines {\n        match stdin {\n            Some(text) => Feed::Text(text.to_string()),\n            None => Feed::Live,\n        }\n    } else if live_inputs {\n        live_inputs_feed(&program, backend)\n    } else if lua_fused {\n        Feed::Text(stdin.map(str::to_string).unwrap_or_default())\n    } else if let Some(values) = &inputs_values {\n        Feed::Text(\n            values\n                .iter()\n                .map(|v| v.to_string())\n                .collect::<Vec<_>>()\n                .join(\"\\n\"),\n        )\n    } else {\n        Feed::Text(value.as_ref().map(|v| v.to_string()).unwrap_or_default())\n    };\n\n    match backend {\n        Backend::Lua => run_lua(\n            &emit_lua::emit(&program),\n            &program.enums,\n            value.as_ref(),\n            inputs_values.as_ref(),\n            program.inputs.as_ref(),\n            &feed,\n        ),\n        Backend::Js => run_node(&emit_js::emit(&program), &feed),\n        Backend::Jq => run_jq(\n            &emit_jq::emit(&program).map_err(anyhow::Error::msg)?,\n            JqInvocation {\n                has_value: value.is_some(),\n                raw: program.body.ty == ty::Type::Str,\n                uses_lines: program.uses_lines,\n            },\n            &feed,\n        ),\n        Backend::Go => run_go(&emit_go::emit(&program), &feed),\n        Backend::Py => run_py(&emit_py::emit(&program), &feed),\n        Backend::Native => {\n            let dir = tempfile::tempdir()?;\n            let exe = dir.path().join(\"program\");\n            link(&program, &exe)?;\n            run_binary(&exe, &feed)\n        }\n        Backend::Rust => {\n            let dir = tempfile::tempdir()?;\n            let exe = dir.path().join(\"program\");\n            link_rust(&emit_rs::emit(&program), &exe)?;\n            run_binary(&exe, &feed)\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n/// The feed for a live, fused `inputs` program: real stdin, but validated. Lua is the exception,\n/// since `run_lua`'s own `next_input` function validates directly off `Feed::Live` rather than\n/// needing `Feed::LiveInputs` to mediate a pipe it has no subprocess to write into.\nfn live_inputs_feed(program: &Program, backend: Backend) -> Feed {\n    if matches!(backend, Backend::Lua) {\n        Feed::Live\n    } else {\n        Feed::LiveInputs(\n            program\n                .inputs\n                .clone()\n                .expect(\"live_inputs implies an inputs type\"),\n            program.enums.clone(),\n        )\n    }\n}\n\n/// What a subprocess backend's stdin is connected to.\nenum Feed {\n    /// Known bytes, written through a pipe: `input`'s re-serialized value, a `lines` program's\n    /// fixture text verbatim, or nothing for a program that reads no stdin at all.\n    Text(String),\n    /// The real process stdin, inherited rather than piped. Reachable only for a `lines`\n    /// program run with no fixture supplied.\n    Live,\n    /// The real process stdin, read and validated one record at a time against the element type,\n    /// then forwarded through a pipe -- real streaming, but mediated by the host rather than\n    /// inherited directly, which is what lets `input::validate` see every record before a\n    /// subprocess backend's own, untrusted parse of it does. Reachable only for a live, fused\n    /// `inputs` program on a backend other than Lua (see `write_to`).\n    LiveInputs(ty::Type, ty::Enums),\n}\n\nimpl Feed {\n    fn stdio(&self) -> std::process::Stdio {\n        match self {\n            Feed::Text(_) | Feed::LiveInputs(..) => std::process::Stdio::piped(),\n            Feed::Live => std::process::Stdio::inherit(),\n        }\n    }\n\n    fn write_to(&self, child: &mut std::process::Child) -> Result<()> {\n        match self {\n            Feed::Text(text) => {\n                child\n                    .stdin\n                    .take()\n                    .expect(\"piped\")\n                    .write_all(text.as_bytes())?;\n            }\n            Feed::Live => {}\n            Feed::LiveInputs(elem_ty, enums) => {\n                let mut stdin = child.stdin.take().expect(\"piped\");\n                for line in std::io::stdin().lock().lines() {\n                    let line = line?;\n                    if line.trim().is_empty() {\n                        continue;\n                    }\n                    let value: serde_json::Value = serde_json::from_str(&line)?;\n                    if let Err(msg) = input::validate(enums, &value, elem_ty, \"inputs\") {\n                        // Close the pipe so the child sees EOF and can finish printing whatever\n                        // it already validly received, then wait for it before reporting the\n                        // refusal -- otherwise this leaves an unreaped child behind.\n                        drop(stdin);\n                        child.wait()?;\n                        return Err(anyhow::Error::msg(msg));\n                    }\n                    writeln!(stdin, \"{line}\")?;\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        Ok(())\n    }\n}\n\n/// Compile to a native executable at `out`.\n///\n/// LLVM produces an object file, which is not a program, so this shells out to `cc` for the\n/// link. That is a toolchain requirement the Lua backend does not have, since mlua vendors its\n/// interpreter.\npub fn link(program: &Program, out: &std::path::Path) -> Result<()> {\n    let dir = tempfile::tempdir()?;\n    let object = dir.path().join(\"program.o\");\n    emit_llvm::compile_to_object(program, &object).map_err(anyhow::Error::msg)?;\n\n    // The runtime is compiled alongside rather than shipped as a library, which keeps the build\n    // to one `cc` call and means there is nothing to install.\n    let runtime = dir.path().join(\"toylang.c\");\n    std::fs::write(&runtime, emit_llvm::RUNTIME_C)?;\n\n    let status = std::process::Command::new(\"cc\")\n        .arg(&object)\n        .arg(&runtime)\n        .arg(\"-o\")\n        .arg(out)\n        .status()\n        .context(\"could not run `cc`\")?;\n    if !status.success() {\n        anyhow::bail!(\"cc failed to link: {status}\");\n    }\n    Ok(())\n}\n\n/// Compile Rust source to an executable at `out`. One `rustc` call on one self-contained file,\n/// no external crate and no Cargo project, the same reason `link` above is one `cc` call: the\n/// generated source depends on nothing this compiler did not already assume was installed.\n/// Public for the same reason as `link`: `tests/backend_rust.rs` calls it directly, separately\n/// from running the result, so a genuine codegen gap (`rustc` rejects the source) is told apart\n/// from a legitimate runtime refusal (`rustc` succeeds, the binary exits non-zero).\npub fn link_rust(source: &str, out: &std::path::Path) -> Result<()> {\n    let dir = tempfile::tempdir()?;\n    let src = dir.path().join(\"program.rs\");\n    std::fs::write(&src, source)?;\n\n    let result = std::process::Command::new(\"rustc\")\n        .arg(\"--edition\")\n        .arg(\"2021\")\n        .arg(&src)\n        .arg(\"-o\")\n        .arg(out)\n        .output()\n        .context(\"could not run `rustc`\")?;\n    if !result.status.success() {\n        anyhow::bail!(\n            \"rustc failed to compile: {}\",\n            String::from_utf8_lossy(&result.stderr)\n        );\n    }\n    Ok(())\n}\n\n/// Runs `cmd` (already given whatever args the caller needs) connected to `feed`, and returns\n/// what it printed.\n///\n/// When `feed` is `Feed::Live` or `Feed::LiveInputs` there is no test harness reading the result\n/// back: stdout and stderr are inherited straight through to the real terminal or pipe instead of\n/// being captured, which is what lets a program that streams actually show output as it produces\n/// it rather than all at once when it exits. `Ok(String::new())` reflects that nothing is left to\n/// hand back -- it already went to the real stdout directly. `feed.write_to` reports a validation\n/// refusal for `Feed::LiveInputs` as an `Err` before this function's own `child.wait()` runs, so\n/// that path never reaches here at all.\nfn run_subprocess(mut cmd: std::process::Command, label: &str, feed: &Feed) -> Result<String> {\n    let live = matches!(feed, Feed::Live | Feed::LiveInputs(..));\n    let mut child = cmd\n        .stdin(feed.stdio())\n        .stdout(if live {\n            std::process::Stdio::inherit()\n        } else {\n            std::process::Stdio::piped()\n        })\n        .stderr(if live {\n            std::process::Stdio::inherit()\n        } else {\n            std::process::Stdio::piped()\n        })\n        .spawn()\n        .with_context(|| format!(\"could not run `{label}`\"))?;\n\n    feed.write_to(&mut child)?;\n\n    if live {\n        let status = child.wait()?;\n        if !status.success() {\n            anyhow::bail!(\"{label} failed\");\n        }\n        return Ok(String::new());\n    }\n\n    let out = child.wait_with_output()?;\n    if !out.status.success() {\n        anyhow::bail!(\"{label} failed: {}\", String::from_utf8_lossy(&out.stderr));\n    }\n    Ok(String::from_utf8(out.stdout)?)\n}\n\nfn run_binary(exe: &std::path::Path, feed: &Feed) -> Result<String> {\n    run_subprocess(\n        std::process::Command::new(exe),\n        \"the compiled program\",\n        feed,\n    )\n}\n\n/// jq puts stdin in `.`, so a program that reads no input runs with `-n` rather than waiting on\n/// a terminal. `has_value` rather than the `Feed` itself, since a `lines` program run live\n/// (`Feed::Live`) still has real stdin coming and must not get `-n` on that account.\n/// The three facts about a program that shape a jq invocation's flags. Each is derived from\n/// the compiled program, not a caller identity -- the fn-params-excessive-bools finding on the\n/// old three-bool signature was escalated, and the settled answer was this struct: the facts\n/// travel under their names.\nstruct JqInvocation {\n    has_value: bool,\n    raw: bool,\n    uses_lines: bool,\n}\n\nfn run_jq(source: &str, inv: JqInvocation, feed: &Feed) -> Result<String> {\n    let JqInvocation {\n        has_value,\n        raw,\n        uses_lines,\n    } = inv;\n    let mut cmd = std::process::Command::new(\"jq\");\n    // jq's stdout is fully buffered rather than line-buffered whenever it is not a terminal, the\n    // same as any other libc stdio program, so a filter over `inputs` piped into another process\n    // would otherwise sit on every result until the run ends. `run_subprocess` only inherits\n    // stdout instead of capturing it for a live run, so this is still what makes that case show a\n    // result as soon as jq produces one rather than at exit.\n    cmd.arg(\"--unbuffered\");\n    cmd.arg(\"-c\");\n    // `-r` decides from the runtime value, so it would print a present Opt<Str> raw and an\n    // absent one as the word null. The rule here is the type's, as on every other backend.\n    if raw {\n        cmd.arg(\"-r\");\n    }\n    if !has_value && !uses_lines {\n        cmd.arg(\"-n\");\n    }\n    // Raw-input mode, needed for `[ inputs ]` to read lines rather than JSON, and forced by the\n    // checker to be the only way `lines` is used in this invocation: mixing it with `input` was\n    // rejected there, because `-R` changes what the whole invocation means, not just one call.\n    // `-n` here is not \"no input coming\"; it is what raw-input mode needs regardless, since\n    // `[ inputs ]` reads for itself rather than through the implicit `.` a normal run would use.\n    if uses_lines {\n        cmd.arg(\"-R\").arg(\"-n\");\n    }\n    cmd.arg(source);\n    run_subprocess(cmd, \"jq\", feed)\n}\n\nfn run_lua(\n    source: &str,\n    enums: &ty::Enums,\n    value: Option<&serde_json::Value>,\n    inputs_values: Option<&Vec<serde_json::Value>>,\n    inputs_elem_ty: Option<&ty::Type>,\n    feed: &Feed,\n) -> Result<String> {\n    let lua = mlua::Lua::new();\n    if let Some(value) = value {\n        lua.globals()\n            .set(emit_lua::INPUT, input::to_lua(&lua, value)?)?;\n    }\n    if let Some(values) = inputs_values {\n        let array = serde_json::Value::Array(values.clone());\n        lua.globals()\n            .set(emit_lua::INPUTS, input::to_lua(&lua, &array)?)?;\n    } else if let Some(elem_ty) = inputs_elem_ty {\n        // `inputs_values` is `None` here for one of two reasons: the program does not read\n        // `inputs` at all (then `inputs_elem_ty` is also `None`, and neither branch runs), or\n        // `run_on` recognized this as a fused program and skipped precomputing the whole Vec on\n        // purpose (`live_inputs` or `lua_fused` there). mlua is embedded in this same process, so\n        // there is no separate JSON parser to write in Lua source the way Rust's own is -- the\n        // generated source just calls this function for the next already-validated, already-\n        // converted record. Reading from `feed` rather than always the real stdin is what keeps\n        // this correct for a fixture-tested fused program too: `feed` is `Feed::Text` there, the\n        // same raw bytes a subprocess backend would have been piped.\n        let elem_ty = elem_ty.clone();\n        let enums = enums.clone();\n        let reader: RefCell<Box<dyn std::io::BufRead>> = RefCell::new(match feed {\n            Feed::Live => Box::new(std::io::BufReader::new(std::io::stdin())),\n            Feed::Text(text) => Box::new(std::io::Cursor::new(text.clone().into_bytes())),\n            // `run_on` never builds this feed for the Lua backend: `live_inputs` there stays\n            // `Feed::Live`, since this function's own reader validates each record itself.\n            Feed::LiveInputs(..) => unreachable!(\"run_on keeps the Lua backend on Feed::Live\"),\n        });\n        let next_input = lua.create_function(move |lua, ()| {\n            use std::io::BufRead;\n            loop {\n                let mut line = String::new();\n                let n = reader\n                    .borrow_mut()\n                    .read_line(&mut line)\n                    .map_err(mlua::Error::external)?;\n                if n == 0 {\n                    return Ok(mlua::Value::Nil);\n                }\n                let line = line.strip_suffix('\\n').unwrap_or(&line);\n                if line.trim().is_empty() {\n                    continue;\n                }\n                let value: serde_json::Value =\n                    serde_json::from_str(line).map_err(mlua::Error::external)?;\n                input::validate(&enums, &value, &elem_ty, \"inputs\")\n                    .map_err(mlua::Error::RuntimeError)?;\n                return input::to_lua(lua, &value);\n            }\n        })?;\n        lua.globals().set(emit_lua::NEXT_INPUT, next_input)?;\n    }\n    // mlua is embedded in this same process, so `io.lines()` in the emitted source reads the\n    // real stdin directly -- verified against a live pipe, and exactly what a `lines` program\n    // run for real should do. A fixture is different: cargo test runs many tests concurrently\n    // in one process, so redirecting the process's own fd 0 would race with all of them, and is\n    // not done. Instead the fixture is written to a file of its own and `io.lines` is pointed at\n    // it, which keeps Lua's own real line-splitting in the loop rather than reimplementing it in\n    // Rust and only testing that reimplementation.\n    let _fixture_dir;\n    if let Feed::Text(text) = feed {\n        let dir = tempfile::tempdir()?;\n        let path = dir.path().join(\"stdin.txt\");\n        std::fs::write(&path, text)?;\n        let path = path\n            .to_str()\n            .expect(\"a temp path is valid UTF-8\")\n            .to_string();\n        let io: mlua::Table = lua.globals().get(\"io\")?;\n        let real_lines: mlua::Function = io.get(\"lines\")?;\n        let fixture =\n            lua.create_function(move |_, ()| real_lines.call::<mlua::Value>(path.clone()))?;\n        io.set(\"lines\", fixture)?;\n        _fixture_dir = Some(dir);\n    }\n\n    // A live run has no test harness reading the result back, the same distinction\n    // `run_subprocess` makes for the OS-process backends: `print` writes straight to the real\n    // stdout and flushes per call instead of buffering into a string only handed back once the\n    // whole chunk finishes.\n    let live = matches!(feed, Feed::Live);\n    let captured = Rc::new(RefCell::new(String::new()));\n    let sink = Rc::clone(&captured);\n    let print = lua.create_function(move |_, s: String| {\n        if live {\n            let mut stdout = std::io::stdout();\n            writeln!(stdout, \"{s}\").map_err(mlua::Error::external)?;\n            stdout.flush().map_err(mlua::Error::external)?;\n        } else {\n            sink.borrow_mut().push_str(&s);\n            sink.borrow_mut().push('\\n');\n        }\n        Ok(())\n    })?;\n    lua.globals().set(\"print\", print)?;\n\n    lua.load(source).exec()?;\n\n    if live {\n        return Ok(String::new());\n    }\n    let out = captured.borrow().clone();\n    Ok(out)\n}\n\n/// Runs through `python3`. Like `node`, this is an interpreter that has to be on the machine\n/// rather than one vendored into the build.\nfn run_py(source: &str, feed: &Feed) -> Result<String> {\n    let dir = tempfile::tempdir()?;\n    let path = dir.path().join(\"program.py\");\n    std::fs::write(&path, source)?;\n\n    let mut cmd = std::process::Command::new(\"python3\");\n    cmd.arg(&path);\n    run_subprocess(cmd, \"python3\", feed)\n}\n\n/// Runs through `go run`, which compiles and executes in one step. Go has no interpreter, so\n/// this is the second backend that needs a real toolchain; like `node` and `cc`, a missing one\n/// is an error rather than a skipped backend.\nfn run_go(source: &str, feed: &Feed) -> Result<String> {\n    let dir = tempfile::tempdir()?;\n    let path = dir.path().join(\"main.go\");\n    std::fs::write(&path, source)?;\n\n    let mut cmd = std::process::Command::new(\"go\");\n    cmd.arg(\"run\").arg(&path);\n    run_subprocess(cmd, \"go\", feed)\n}\n\n/// Runs through `node`, which must be present. A missing toolchain is an error rather than a\n/// quietly skipped backend: a report that says two backends agreed when only one ran is worse\n/// than no report.\nfn run_node(source: &str, feed: &Feed) -> Result<String> {\n    let dir = tempfile::tempdir()?;\n    let path = dir.path().join(\"program.js\");\n    std::fs::write(&path, source)?;\n\n    let mut cmd = std::process::Command::new(\"node\");\n    cmd.arg(&path);\n    run_subprocess(cmd, \"node\", feed)\n}\n\nimpl std::error::Error for Error {}","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":634,"totalLines":634,"truncated":false}},"title":"src/lib.rs","time":{"start":1788297852270,"end":1788297852277}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Key insight: the corpus harness uses `toylang::run_on(program, input, backend)` and expects all backends to produce the same output. So a pipe_through corpus case requires all backends to implement subprocess spawning.\n\nBut jq can't spawn subprocesses... Actually, wait. Let me reconsider - maybe jq CAN. jq can run external commands via... no. jq has no syscall. Actually jq does not support spawning subprocesses.\n\n Actually - there's `jq`'s `input`/`inputs` which read JSON from stdin. There's no subprocess. But wait, maybe the harness's `run_on` runs jq as a subprocess itself... Let me check how run_on works and whether jq could shell out somehow.\n\n Let me look at the backends list. Let me check Backend::ALL and run_on in lib.rs.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05edbb569001DiYygpVzmmAQcR","sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","messageID":"msg_05edb921f00134YxObwjBGXEC0"}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1788297852295,"sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","part":{"id":"prt_05edbb584001tC0bZBItZBqBZs","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"5a1012ab7eb7388a8de2a55a732be7fdbf823735","messageID":"msg_05edb921f00134YxObwjBGXEC0","sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":48602,"input":2269,"output":67,"reasoning":186,"cache":{"write":0,"read":46080}},"cost":0.000930305}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788297853996,"sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","part":{"id":"prt_05edbbc24001UWR0dJHSSKK4h7","messageID":"msg_05edbb5970013kkAUQwSMKbJPM","sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","snapshot":"5a1012ab7eb7388a8de2a55a732be7fdbf823735","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788297862662,"sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_9e6c49cc0a57479c834f3439","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/src/emit_jq.rs"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/src/emit_jq.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: //! The jq backend.\n2: //!\n3: //! The other three targets are imperative and this one is not, so it is the only backend where\n4: //! the compiler has to say what it means in stream terms. That makes it a check on the\n5: //! relationship to jq rather than another way to run a program: everything the design diverges\n6: //! on has to be bridged here explicitly.\n7: //!\n8: //! Two mappings are worth naming. A dimension spec becomes `.[]` plus a reification, since\n9: //! keeping a dimension in a stream language means iterating and collecting. And `Opt` carries\n10: //! its enum's own runtime shape even here -- `{\"some\": v}` present, `\"none\"` absent -- tagged\n11: //! in memory like every backend now, with `null` appearing only when the printer flattens the\n12: //! tags away. One consequence is load-bearing: no in-memory value is ever JSON null, so jq's\n13: //! own null (an out-of-range `.[i]`) still unambiguously means \"was not there\".\n14: \n15: use crate::ast::BinOp;\n16: use crate::tir::{self, Builtin, Kind, LocalId, Program, Tir};\n17: use crate::ty::{self, Enums, Type};\n18: \n19: /// The binding stdin is read into. jq puts the input in `.`, which this backend needs for the\n20: /// subject, so it is bound away before the program starts.\n21: const INPUT: &str = \"$t_input\";\n22: const INPUTS: &str = \"$t_inputs\";\n23: \n24: /// jq has no integer type, so 32-bit wrapping is arithmetic on doubles. Addition and\n25: /// subtraction stay exact because the result never passes 2^33; multiplication does not, since\n26: /// the true product needs 62 bits, so it goes through 16-bit halves whose partial products each\n27: /// stay under 2^53. Verified against C and Math.imul, including -2147483648 * -1.\n28: const ARITH_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_i32: . as $r\n29:   | ((($r % 4294967296) + 4294967296) % 4294967296)\n30:   | if . >= 2147483648 then . - 4294967296 else . end;\n31: def tl_mul($a; $b):\n32:   ($a | tl_i32) as $x | ($b | tl_i32) as $y\n33:   | (if $x < 0 then $x + 4294967296 else $x end) as $ua\n34:   | (if $y < 0 then $y + 4294967296 else $y end) as $ub\n35:   | ($ua / 65536 | floor) as $ah | ($ua % 65536) as $al\n36:   | ($ub / 65536 | floor) as $bh | ($ub % 65536) as $bl\n37:   | (($al * $bl) + ((($ah * $bl) + ($al * $bh)) % 65536) * 65536) | tl_i32;\n38: def tl_div($a; $b):\n39:   if $b == 0 then error(\"toylang: divided by zero\")\n40:   else ($a / $b | trunc) | tl_i32 end;\n41: def tl_rem($a; $b):\n42:   if $b == 0 then error(\"toylang: divided by zero\") else ($a % $b) | tl_i32 end;\n43: \"#;\n44: \n45: /// Int64 arithmetic on doubles, exact only within +/-2^53 (kantord/toylang#83). That boundary\n46: /// is documented rather than papered over: 32-bit wrapping could be emulated exactly through\n47: /// 16-bit halves because every partial product fit a double, and no such split reassembles a\n48: /// 64-bit product or a mod-2^64 wrap from pieces a double can hold. So `+`, `-` and `*` are\n49: /// jq's own, `%` casts through C's 64-bit integers inside jq itself, and a program whose\n50: /// Int64 values stay within 2^53 -- every corpus case does -- agrees with the other six\n51: /// backends; one that leaves it diverges here, and docs/reference/types/int64.md says so.\n52: const ARITH64_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_div64($a; $b):\n53:   if $b == 0 then error(\"toylang: divided by zero\") else ($a / $b | trunc) end;\n54: def tl_rem64($a; $b):\n55:   if $b == 0 then error(\"toylang: divided by zero\") else ($a % $b) end;\n56: \"#;\n57: \n58: pub fn emit(program: &Program) -> Result<String, String> {\n59:     let enums = &program.enums;\n60:     let mut out = String::new();\n61:     let (arith, arith64) = uses_arith(program);\n62:     if arith {\n63:         out.push_str(ARITH_HELPER);\n64:     }\n65:     if arith64 {\n66:         out.push_str(ARITH64_HELPER);\n67:     }\n68: \n69:     out.push_str(&printers(program)?);\n70: \n71:     // jq resolves a `def` only against what is already defined, so definitions have to come out\n72:     // callee-first. The checker collects every signature before checking any body and therefore\n73:     // accepts a call to a function defined further down, which is a rule this target does not\n74:     // share. Lua needed forward declarations for the same reason; jq has no way to write one.\n75:     for f in ordered(program)? {\n76:         // A unary function's argument arrives as `.` and is bound before the body runs; a\n77:         // nullary one ignores `.` entirely, since it has nothing to bind.\n78:         out.push_str(&match &f.param {\n79:             Some(param) => format!(\n80:                 \"def {}: . as ${} | {};\\n\",\n81:                 user(&f.name),\n82:                 user(param),\n83:                 expr(enums, &f.body)\n84:             ),\n85:             None => format!(\"def {}: {};\\n\", user(&f.name), expr(enums, &f.body)),\n86:         });\n87:     }\n88: \n89:     if let Some(fusion) = tir::fusion(program) {\n90:         // jq's own `inputs` is already lazy; the eager path below only becomes eager by wrapping\n91:         // it in `[...]`. Skipping that wrapper and running the whole `map`/`select` chain as one\n92:         // filter over the `inputs` generator is what makes jq print each record as it arrives\n93:         // rather than after the last one, the same as running the equivalent `jq` program by\n94:         // hand would.\n95:         out.push_str(\"inputs\");\n96:         for stage in &fusion.stages {\n97:             match stage {\n98:                 tir::Stage::Map { param, body } => {\n99:                     out.push_str(&format!(\n100:                         \" | . as {} | {}\",\n101:                         local(*param),\n102:                         expr(enums, body)\n103:                     ));\n104:                 }\n105:                 tir::Stage::Select { param, pred } => {\n106:                     out.push_str(&format!(\n107:                         \" | . as {} | select({})\",\n108:                         local(*param),\n109:                         expr(enums, pred)\n110:                     ));\n111:                 }\n112:             }\n113:         }\n114:         let Kind::Builtin { arg, .. } = &program.body.kind else {\n115:             unreachable!(\"fusion only matches a jsonlines body\")\n116:         };\n117:         let elem = tir::runtime_elem(&arg.ty).expect(\"jsonlines's argument has an element\");\n118:         out.push_str(&format!(\" | ({} | tojson)\\n\", canonical(enums, elem, \".\")));\n119:         return Ok(out);\n120:     }\n121: \n122:     if program.input.is_some() {\n123:         out.push_str(&format!(\". as {INPUT} | \"));\n124:     }\n125:     // `-n` (added whenever the invocation has no value already in `.`, run_jq's job) is what\n126:     // makes `inputs` mean \"every value on stdin\" rather than \"every value after the first.\"\n127:     if program.inputs.is_some() {\n128:         out.push_str(&format!(\"[inputs] as {INPUTS} | \"));\n129:     }\n130:     // Records are rebuilt in the type's field order, because jq preserves insertion order and\n131:     // an object read from input carries whatever order the input had.\n132:     out.push_str(&canonical(\n133:         enums,\n134:         &program.body.ty,\n135:         &expr(enums, &program.body),\n136:     ));\n137:     out.push('\\n');\n138:     Ok(out)\n139: }\n140: \n141: /// Reconstruct a value with keys in the type's order, so the printed form matches the other\n142: /// backends rather than the input's key order.\n143: fn canonical(enums: &Enums, ty: &Type, value: &str) -> String {\n144:     match ty {\n145:         Type::Param(_) => unreachable!(\"params are substituted before emit\"),\n146:         // The checker refuses a program whose result contains a stream, since there is nothing to\n147:         // print: a stream has no value, only a promise that collect can redeem.\n148:         Type::Stream(_) => unreachable!(\"a stream cannot reach the printer\"),\n149:         Type::Char => unreachable!(\"Char cannot reach the printer, refused by the checker\"),\n150:         Type::Str | Type::Int | Type::Int64 | Type::Bool => value.to_string(),\n151:         Type::Vec(elem) => format!(\"[ {value}[] | {} ]\", canonical(enums, elem, \".\")),\n152:         Type::Enum { .. } if ty.as_opt().is_some() => {\n153:             let inner = ty.as_opt().expect(\"guarded\");\n154:             format!(\n155:                 \"({value} | if . == \\\"none\\\" then null else (.some | {}) end)\",\n156:                 canonical(enums, inner, \".\")\n157:             )\n158:         }\n159:         // A recursive enum is rebuilt by a filter of its own (`printers`), because expanding one\n160:         // here has no bottom: its payload leads back to the same type.\n161:         Type::Enum { .. } if ty::is_recursive(enums, ty) => {\n162:             format!(\"({value} | {})\", ty.show_fn())\n163:         }\n164:         Type::Enum { .. } => canonical_enum(enums, ty, value),\n165:         Type::Record(fields) => {\n166:             let parts: Vec<String> = fields\n167:                 .iter()\n168:                 .map(|(name, fty)| {\n169:                     format!(\n170:                         \"{}: {}\",\n171:                         jq_string(name),\n172:                         canonical(enums, fty, &field_of(\".\", name))\n173:                     )\n174:                 })\n175:                 .collect();\n176:             format!(\"({value} | {{{}}})\", parts.join(\", \"))\n177:         }\n178:     }\n179: }\n180: \n181: /// Every function name a `Kind::Call` inside `t` names, in the order encountered, duplicates and\n182: /// all -- `ordered` only ever asks whether a name is present, and a cycle's error message reads\n183: /// better walking a real call rather than a deduplicated set.\n184: fn callees(t: &Tir, out: &mut Vec<String>) {\n185:     match &t.kind {\n186:         Kind::Str(_)\n187:         | Kind::Int(_)\n188:         | Kind::Var(_)\n189:         | Kind::Local(_)\n190:         | Kind::Input\n191:         | Kind::Inputs\n192:         | Kind::Lines => {}\n193:         Kind::VecLit(items) => items.iter().for_each(|i| callees(i, out)),\n194:         Kind::RecordLit { fields } => {\n195:             fields.iter().for_each(|(_, v)| callees(v, out));\n196:         }\n197:         Kind::EnumLit { payload, .. } => {\n198:             if let Some(p) = payload {\n199:                 callees(p, out);\n200:             }\n201:         }\n202:         Kind::Call { func, arg } => {\n203:             out.push(func.clone());\n204:             if let Some(a) = arg {\n205:                 callees(a, out);\n206:             }\n207:         }\n208:         Kind::Concat(l, r)\n209:         | Kind::Compare { lhs: l, rhs: r, .. }\n210:         | Kind::Logic { lhs: l, rhs: r, .. } => {\n211:             callees(l, out);\n212:             callees(r, out);\n213:         }\n214:         Kind::Bind { value, body, .. } => {\n215:             callees(value, out);\n216:             callees(body, out);\n217:         }\n218:         Kind::Select { source, pred, .. } => {\n219:             callees(source, out);\n220:             callees(pred, out);\n221:         }\n222:         Kind::Map { source, body, .. } | Kind::OptMap { source, body, .. } => {\n223:             callees(source, out);\n224:             callees(body, out);\n225:         }\n226:         Kind::Builtin { arg, .. } => callees(arg, out),\n227:         Kind::Cond {\n228:             cond,\n229:             then,\n230:             otherwise,\n231:         } => {\n232:             callees(cond, out);\n233:             callees(then, out);\n234:             callees(otherwise, out);\n235:         }\n236:         Kind::Arith { lhs, rhs, .. } => {\n237:             callees(lhs, out);\n238:             callees(rhs, out);\n239:         }\n240:         Kind::Field { base, .. } | Kind::Unwrap { base } | Kind::Not(base) => callees(base, out),\n241:         Kind::Index { base, index, .. } => {\n242:             callees(base, out);\n243:             callees(index, out);\n244:         }\n245:         Kind::Match { subject, arms, .. } => {\n246:             callees(subject, out);\n247:             for a in arms {\n248:                 if let Some(g) = &a.guard {\n249:                     callees(g, out);\n250:                 }\n251:                 callees(&a.body, out);\n252:             }\n253:         }\n254:     }\n255: }\n256: \n257: /// Definitions in callee-before-caller order, or the cycle blocking one: jq's `def` sees only\n258: /// itself and whatever is already defined above it, with no forward declaration to bridge a\n259: /// real cycle between two or more named functions (kantord/toylang#79). Self-recursion never\n260: /// gets stuck here -- a function calling only itself is always immediately ready -- so reaching\n261: /// the stuck state below means the remaining functions have a genuine cycle among them.\n262: fn ordered(program: &Program) -> Result<Vec<&tir::Func>, String> {\n263:     topsort(\n264:         program.funcs.iter().collect(),\n265:         |f| f.name.clone(),\n266:         |f| {\n267:             let mut calls = Vec::new();\n268:             callees(&f.body, &mut calls);\n269:             calls\n270:         },\n271:         \"named functions\",\n272:     )\n273: }\n274: \n275: /// Callee-before-caller order over a list of named things, or the cycle blocking one. `name`\n276: /// yields each item's `def` name and `deps` the names it calls; `kind` names what `remaining`\n277: /// holds, for the refusal message alone. Both the named functions (`ordered`) and the\n278: /// recursive-enum printers (`printers`) go through here, because jq's `def` has the same reach on\n279: /// each: only itself and what is already defined above it, with no forward declaration\n280: /// (kantord/toylang#79, kantord/toylang#116).\n281: fn topsort<T>(\n282:     mut remaining: Vec<T>,\n283:     name: impl Fn(&T) -> String,\n284:     deps: impl Fn(&T) -> Vec<String>,\n285:     kind: &str,\n286: ) -> Result<Vec<T>, String> {\n287:     let mut done: Vec<T> = Vec::new();\n288:     let mut placed: Vec<String> = Vec::new();\n289:     while !remaining.is_empty() {\n290:         let ready: Vec<usize> = remaining\n291:             .iter()\n292:             .enumerate()\n293:             .filter(|(_, item)| {\n294:                 let n = name(item);\n295:                 deps(item).iter().all(|c| c == &n || placed.contains(c))\n296:             })\n297:             .map(|(i, _)| i)\n298:             .collect();\n299:         if ready.is_empty() {\n300:             return Err(cycle_message(&remaining, &name, &deps, kind));\n301:         }\n302:         for i in ready.into_iter().rev() {\n303:             let item = remaining.remove(i);\n304:             placed.push(name(&item));\n305:             done.push(item);\n306:         }\n307:     }\n308:     Ok(done)\n309: }\n310: \n311: /// A concrete cycle among `remaining`'s items, walked by following one unresolved call from\n312: /// each to the next: every item still in `remaining` has at least one such call (otherwise it\n313: /// would have been ready), so the walk is finite and must revisit a name. `kind` is `topsort`'s\n314: /// own, unchanged, naming `remaining`'s contents in the message.\n315: fn cycle_message<T>(\n316:     remaining: &[T],\n317:     name: &impl Fn(&T) -> String,\n318:     deps: &impl Fn(&T) -> Vec<String>,\n319:     kind: &str,\n320: ) -> String {\n321:     let names: Vec<String> = remaining.iter().map(name).collect();\n322:     let mut path: Vec<String> = Vec::new();\n323:     let mut current = name(&remaining[0]);\n324:     loop {\n325:         if let Some(at) = path.iter().position(|n| *n == current) {\n326:             let cycle = &path[at..];\n327:             let chain: Vec<String> = cycle.iter().map(|n| format!(\"`{n}`\")).collect();\n328:             return format!(\n329:                 \"jq cannot compile this: {} -> `{}` is a cycle between {kind}, and jq's `def` \\\n330:                  has no forward declaration -- only self-recursion and a call to something \\\n331:                  already defined above it are representable here\",\n332:                 chain.join(\" -> \"),\n333:                 cycle[0],\n334:             );\n335:         }\n336:         path.push(current.clone());\n337:         let item = remaining\n338:             .iter()\n339:             .find(|f| name(f) == current)\n340:             .expect(\"current is a name drawn from remaining\");\n341:         current = deps(item)\n342:             .into_iter()\n343:             .find(|c| c != &name(item) && names.contains(c))\n344:             .expect(\"a stuck item has at least one unresolved call within remaining\");\n345:     }\n346: }\n347: \n348: /// Whether the program does 32-bit arithmetic, 64-bit arithmetic, or both: the node's type\n349: /// says which width each `Arith` is, so the two helper blocks are included independently.\n350: fn uses_arith(program: &Program) -> (bool, bool) {\n351:     fn walk(t: &Tir, found: &mut (bool, bool)) {\n352:         match &t.kind {\n353:             Kind::Arith { lhs, rhs, .. } => {\n354:                 if t.ty == Type::Int64 {\n355:                     found.1 = true;\n356:                 } else {\n357:                     found.0 = true;\n358:                 }\n359:                 walk(lhs, found);\n360:                 walk(rhs, found);\n361:             }\n362:             Kind::Cond {\n363:                 cond,\n364:                 then,\n365:                 otherwise,\n366:             } => {\n367:                 walk(cond, found);\n368:                 walk(then, found);\n369:                 walk(otherwise, found);\n370:             }\n371:             Kind::Str(_)\n372:             | Kind::Int(_)\n373:             | Kind::Var(_)\n374:             | Kind::Local(_)\n375:             | Kind::Input\n376:             | Kind::Inputs\n377:             | Kind::Lines => {}\n378:             Kind::VecLit(items) => items.iter().for_each(|i| walk(i, found)),\n379:             Kind::RecordLit { fields } => fields.iter().for_each(|(_, v)| walk(v, found)),\n380:             Kind::EnumLit { payload, .. } => {\n381:                 if let Some(p) = payload {\n382:                     walk(p, found);\n383:                 }\n384:             }\n385:             Kind::Call { arg, .. } => {\n386:                 if let Some(a) = arg {\n387:                     walk(a, found);\n388:                 }\n389:             }\n390:             Kind::Builtin { which, arg } => {\n391:                 // `sum` over Int narrows through `tl_i32` in its fold, so the helper that\n392:                 // defines it has to be present alongside.\n393:                 if *which == Builtin::Sum && tir::runtime_elem(&arg.ty) == Some(&Type::Int) {\n394:                     found.0 = true;\n395:                 }\n396:                 walk(arg, found);\n397:             }\n398:             Kind::Concat(l, r)\n399:             | Kind::Compare { lhs: l, rhs: r, .. }\n400:             | Kind::Logic { lhs: l, rhs: r, .. } => {\n401:                 walk(l, found);\n402:                 walk(r, found);\n403:             }\n404:             Kind::Bind { value, body, .. }\n405:             | Kind::Map {\n406:                 source: value,\n407:                 body,\n408:                 ..\n409:             }\n410:             | Kind::OptMap {\n411:                 source: value,\n412:                 body,\n413:                 ..\n414:             } => {\n415:                 walk(value, found);\n416:                 walk(body, found);\n417:             }\n418:             Kind::Select { source, pred, .. } => {\n419:                 walk(source, found);\n420:                 walk(pred, found);\n421:             }\n422:             Kind::Field { base, .. } | Kind::Unwrap { base } | Kind::Not(base) => walk(base, found),\n423:             Kind::Index { base, index, .. } => {\n424:                 walk(base, found);\n425:                 walk(index, found);\n426:             }\n427:             Kind::Match { subject, arms, .. } => {\n428:                 walk(subject, found);\n429:                 for a in arms {\n430:                     if let Some(g) = &a.guard {\n431:                         walk(g, found);\n432:                     }\n433:                     walk(&a.body, found);\n434:                 }\n435:             }\n436:         }\n437:     }\n438:     let mut found = (false, false);\n439:     program.funcs.iter().for_each(|f| walk(&f.body, &mut found));\n440:     walk(&program.body, &mut found);\n441:     found\n442: }\n443: \n444: fn user(name: &str) -> String {\n445:     format!(\"v_{name}\")\n446: }\n447: \n448: fn local(id: LocalId) -> String {\n449:     format!(\"$t_{id}\")\n450: }\n451: \n452: fn field_of(base: &str, name: &str) -> String {\n453:     format!(\"{base}[{}]\", jq_string(name))\n454: }\n455: \n456: /// Apply `inner` one dimension down, `depth` times. Keeping a dimension in a stream language is\n457: /// iterate-and-collect, which is why every level costs a `[ .[] | ... ]`.\n458: fn distribute(inner: &str, depth: usize) -> String {\n459:     if depth == 0 {\n460:         inner.to_string()\n461:     } else {\n462:         format!(\"[ .[] | {} ]\", distribute(inner, depth - 1))\n463:     }\n464: }\n465: \n466: fn expr(enums: &Enums, t: &Tir) -> String {\n467:     match &t.kind {\n468:         Kind::Str(s) => jq_string(s),\n469:         Kind::Int(n) => n.to_string(),\n470:         Kind::Var(name) => format!(\"${}\", user(name)),\n471:         Kind::Local(id) => local(*id),\n472:         Kind::Input => INPUT.to_string(),\n473:         Kind::Inputs => INPUTS.to_string(),\n474:         // The stream, materialized eagerly: `[ inputs ]` in raw-input mode is every line of\n475:         // stdin as an array of strings. `-n -R` on the invocation is what makes this mode\n476:         // available; see the checker rule against mixing `input` and `lines` in one program.\n477:         Kind::Lines => \"[ inputs ]\".to_string(),\n478:         // Each value is parenthesised: everything in jq is a filter, so an unbracketed `|`\n479:         // or `,` inside one would be read as part of the object rather than as its value.\n480:         Kind::RecordLit { fields } => {\n481:             let parts: Vec<String> = fields\n482:                 .iter()\n483:                 .map(|(name, value)| format!(\"{}: ({})\", jq_string(name), expr(enums, value)))\n484:                 .collect();\n485:             format!(\"{{{}}}\", parts.join(\", \"))\n486:         }\n487: \n488:         // The value is its JSON shape, which jq spells directly.\n489:         Kind::EnumLit { variant, payload } => match payload {\n490:             None => jq_string(variant),\n491:             Some(p) => format!(\"{{{}: ({})}}\", jq_string(variant), expr(enums, p)),\n492:         },\n493: \n494:         Kind::VecLit(items) => {\n495:             let parts: Vec<String> = items.iter().map(|i| expr(enums, i)).collect();\n496:             format!(\"[{}]\", parts.join(\", \"))\n497:         }\n498:         // A nullary function ignores `.`, so its call is just its name, run against whatever\n499:         // is already flowing through the surrounding pipeline.\n500:         Kind::Call { func, arg } => match arg {\n501:             Some(arg) => format!(\"({} | {})\", expr(enums, arg), user(func)),\n502:             None => format!(\"({})\", user(func)),\n503:         },\n504:         // jq's own `+` already joins two arrays the way it joins two strings, so a Vec needs\n505:         // no different spelling here than Str does.\n506:         Kind::Concat(l, r) => format!(\"({} + {})\", expr(enums, l), expr(enums, r)),\n507:         Kind::Arith { op, lhs, rhs } => arith(&t.ty, *op, expr(enums, lhs), expr(enums, rhs)),\n508:         Kind::Cond {\n509:             cond,\n510:             then,\n511:             otherwise,\n512:         } => format!(\n513:             \"(if {} then {} else {} end)\",\n514:             expr(enums, cond),\n515:             expr(enums, then),\n516:             expr(enums, otherwise)\n517:         ),\n518:         Kind::Builtin { which, arg } => match which {\n519:             Builtin::IntToStr => format!(\"({} | tostring)\", expr(enums, arg)),\n520:             // jq has one number type at every width, so the bridge has nothing to do.\n521:             Builtin::IntToI64 => format!(\"({})\", expr(enums, arg)),\n522:             Builtin::Range => format!(\"[ range(0; {}) ]\", expr(enums, arg)),\n523:             // `explode` already decodes jq's UTF-8 string by codepoint, not by byte, so there\n524:             // is no decoding to get right here.\n525:             Builtin::Chars => format!(\"({} | explode)\", expr(enums, arg)),\n526:             // `canonical` reorders a record's keys but leaves the *value*, not text; `tojson`\n527:             // is what turns each element into the same compact JSON string `-c` would print for\n528:             // it, matching every other backend's per-element encoding.\n529:             Builtin::JsonLines => {\n530:                 let elem = tir::runtime_elem(&arg.ty).expect(\"checked to be a Vec or a stream\");\n531:                 format!(\n532:                     \"({} | [.[] | ({} | tojson)] | join(\\\"\\\\n\\\"))\",\n533:                     expr(enums, arg),\n534:                     canonical(enums, elem, \".\")\n535:                 )\n536:             }\n537:             // The source already materialized, so the exit has nothing left to do.\n538:             Builtin::Collect => expr(enums, arg),\n539:             Builtin::Length => format!(\"({} | length)\", expr(enums, arg)),\n540:             // jq's own `.[1:]` on an empty array is `[]`, not null; toylang's tail needs the\n541:             // tagged Opt shape instead, so both cases are spelled out rather than borrowed.\n542:             Builtin::Tail => {\n543:                 format!(\n544:                     \"({} | if length == 0 then \\\"none\\\" else {{some: .[1:]}} end)\",\n545:                     expr(enums, arg)\n546:                 )\n547:             }\n548:             // Not jq's own `add`, which is `null` on an empty list rather than `[]` -- a reduce\n549:             // starting from `[]` gives the right answer in both cases.\n550:             Builtin::Flatten => format!(\"({} | reduce .[] as $x ([]; . + $x))\", expr(enums, arg)),\n551:             // jq's own `sort`/`reverse` already are this, restricted the same way the checker\n552:             // restricts `sort` to Int, Int64, Str, and Char.\n553:             Builtin::Sort => format!(\"({} | sort)\", expr(enums, arg)),\n554:             Builtin::Reverse => format!(\"({} | reverse)\", expr(enums, arg)),\n555:             // `reduce` starting from 0 is `add`'s empty-is-0 shape with the wrap `+` applies to\n556:             // an Int spelled explicitly; Int64's fold is plain `+`, exact within the 2^53 the\n557:             // module comment already owns.\n558:             Builtin::Sum => {\n559:                 if tir::runtime_elem(&arg.ty) == Some(&Type::Int) {\n560:                     format!(\n561:                         \"({} | reduce .[] as $x (0; ((. + $x) | tl_i32)))\",\n562:                         expr(enums, arg)\n563:                     )\n564:                 } else {\n565:                     format!(\"({} | reduce .[] as $x (0; . + $x))\", expr(enums, arg))\n566:                 }\n567:             }\n568:             // jq's own `max` on an empty list errors, and on a non-empty one is exactly this,\n569:             // so only the empty case is spelled: the absent Opt, tagged the way `Tail` tags.\n570:             Builtin::Max => format!(\n571:                 \"({} | if length == 0 then \\\"none\\\" else {{some: max}} end)\",\n572:                 expr(enums, arg)\n573:             ),\n574:             // The names come from the checked type, not the object value, so `arg` runs only to\n575:             // become the `.` a literal array then ignores -- the same discard the pipe already\n576:             // gives every other builtin here.\n577:             Builtin::Fields => {\n578:                 let Type::Record(fields) = &arg.ty else {\n579:                     unreachable!(\"checked to be a record\")\n580:                 };\n581:                 let names: Vec<String> = fields.iter().map(|(n, _)| jq_string(n)).collect();\n582:                 format!(\"({} | [{}])\", expr(enums, arg), names.join(\", \"))\n583:             }\n584:         },\n585:         Kind::Compare { op, lhs, rhs } => {\n586:             format!(\"({} {} {})\", expr(enums, lhs), jq_op(*op), expr(enums, rhs))\n587:         }\n588:         // jq spells them the same way toylang does, and short-circuits them the same way too.\n589:         Kind::Logic { op, lhs, rhs } => format!(\"({} {op} {})\", expr(enums, lhs), expr(enums, rhs)),\n590:         Kind::Not(base) => format!(\"({} | not)\", expr(enums, base)),\n591:         Kind::Bind {\n592:             local: id,\n593:             value,\n594:             body,\n595:         } => {\n596:             format!(\n597:                 \"({} as {} | {})\",\n598:                 expr(enums, value),\n599:                 local(*id),\n600:                 expr(enums, body)\n601:             )\n602:         }\n603:         // The one operator that is derived in jq and primitive here: `map(f)` is `[ .[] | f ]`\n604:         // there, and neither half of that exists in a language with no effect layer.\n605:         Kind::Map {\n606:             source,\n607:             param,\n608:             body,\n609:         } => format!(\n610:             \"[ {}[] | . as {} | {} ]\",\n611:             expr(enums, source),\n612:             local(*param),\n613:             expr(enums, body)\n614:         ),\n615:         Kind::Select {\n616:             source,\n617:             param,\n618:             pred,\n619:         } => format!(\n620:             \"[ {}[] | . as {} | select({}) ]\",\n621:             expr(enums, source),\n622:             local(*param),\n623:             expr(enums, pred)\n624:         ),\n625:         Kind::Field { base, name } => {\n626:             let depth = tir::vec_depth(&base.ty);\n627:             format!(\n628:                 \"({} | {})\",\n629:                 expr(enums, base),\n630:                 distribute(&field_of(\".\", name), depth)\n631:             )\n632:         }\n633:         Kind::Unwrap { base } => {\n634:             let check = format!(\n635:                 \"if . == \\\"none\\\" then error({}) else .some end\",\n636:                 jq_string(\"toylang: unwrapped a value that is not there\")\n637:             );\n638:             format!(\n639:                 \"({} | {})\",\n640:                 expr(enums, base),\n641:                 distribute(&check, tir::vec_depth(&base.ty))\n642:             )\n643:         }\n644:         // Opt's reorder pass (kantord/toylang#66): the same `== \"none\"`/`.some` shape the\n645:         // printer and Match already read, generalised to rebuild the object instead.\n646:         Kind::OptMap {\n647:             source,\n648:             param,\n649:             body,\n650:         } => format!(\n651:             \"({} | if . == \\\"none\\\" then \\\"none\\\" else (.some as {} | {{some: {}}}) end)\",\n652:             expr(enums, source),\n653:             local(*param),\n654:             expr(enums, body)\n655:         ),\n656:         // Out of range is null in jq, and no in-memory toylang value is ever null (the\n657:         // module comment above), so the null test is exactly the was-not-there test; what\n658:         // comes out is the tagged Opt either way.\n659:         Kind::Index {\n660:             base, index, depth, ..\n661:         } => {\n662:             let at = format!(\n663:                 \"(.[{}] as $e | if $e == null then \\\"none\\\" else {{some: $e}} end)\",\n664:                 expr(enums, index)\n665:             );\n666:             format!(\"({} | {})\", expr(enums, base), distribute(&at, *depth))\n667:         }\n668:         // Tests over the subject: equality for a unit variant, `type`-guarded `has` for a\n669:         // payload one, since `has` on a string is an error rather than false, and the guard's\n670:         // own Bool for a guard arm. A total chain's last arm is the `else`, the checker having\n671:         // proved nothing else can reach it; a partial chain tags every present arm and its\n672:         // `else` is the absent Opt.\n673:         Kind::Match {\n674:             subject,\n675:             arms,\n676:             partial,\n677:         } => {\n678:             let subj = expr(enums, subject);\n679:             let run = |arm: &tir::MatchArm| match arm.payload {\n680:                 Some(pid) => {\n681:                     let variant = arm\n682:                         .variant\n683:                         .as_ref()\n684:                         .expect(\"only a variant arm has a payload\");\n685:                     format!(\n686:                         \"({subj}[{}] as {} | {})\",\n687:                         jq_string(variant),\n688:                         local(pid),\n689:                         expr(enums, &arm.body)\n690:                     )\n691:                 }\n692:                 None => expr(enums, &arm.body),\n693:             };\n694:             let run = |arm: &tir::MatchArm| {\n695:                 if *partial {\n696:                     // A partial chain's yield is an Opt, so a present arm is tagged.\n697:                     format!(\"{{some: {}}}\", run(arm))\n698:                 } else {\n699:                     run(arm)\n700:                 }\n701:             };\n702:             if !*partial && arms.len() == 1 {\n703:                 return format!(\"({})\", run(&arms[0]));\n704:             }\n705:             let mut out = String::from(\"(\");\n706:             for (i, arm) in arms.iter().enumerate() {\n707:                 let test = match (&arm.variant, &arm.guard) {\n708:                     (Some(v), _) if arm.payload.is_some() => Some(format!(\n709:                         \"({subj} | type == \\\"object\\\" and has({}))\",\n710:                         jq_string(v)\n711:                     )),\n712:                     (Some(v), _) => Some(format!(\"{subj} == {}\", jq_string(v))),\n713:                     (None, Some(g)) => Some(expr(enums, g)),\n714:                     (None, None) => None,\n715:                 };\n716:                 match test {\n717:                     Some(test) if *partial || i + 1 < arms.len() => {\n718:                         let word = if i == 0 { \"if\" } else { \"elif\" };\n719:                         out.push_str(&format!(\"{word} {test} then {} \", run(arm)));\n720:                     }\n721:                     _ => out.push_str(&format!(\"else {} end\", run(arm))),\n722:                 }\n723:             }\n724:             if *partial {\n725:                 out.push_str(\"else \\\"none\\\" end\");\n726:             }\n727:             out.push(')');\n728:             out\n729:         }\n730:     }\n731: }\n732: \n733: /// One arithmetic expression at the width the node's type names. The 64-bit side stays in\n734: /// jq's doubles, exact within +/-2^53 and honestly not past it (the ARITH64_HELPER comment);\n735: /// nothing wraps there, since no double could carry the wrapped value back.\n736: fn arith(ty: &Type, op: BinOp, l: String, r: String) -> String {\n737:     if *ty == Type::Int64 {\n738:         match op {\n739:             BinOp::Div => format!(\"tl_div64({l}; {r})\"),\n740:             BinOp::Rem => format!(\"tl_rem64({l}; {r})\"),\n741:             BinOp::Mul => format!(\"({l} * {r})\"),\n742:             BinOp::Add => format!(\"({l} + {r})\"),\n743:             BinOp::Sub => format!(\"({l} - {r})\"),\n744:             other => unreachable!(\"{other} is not arithmetic\"),\n745:         }\n746:     } else {\n747:         match op {\n748:             BinOp::Div => format!(\"tl_div({l}; {r})\"),\n749:             BinOp::Rem => format!(\"tl_rem({l}; {r})\"),\n750:             BinOp::Mul => format!(\"tl_mul({l}; {r})\"),\n751:             BinOp::Add => format!(\"(({l} + {r}) | tl_i32)\"),\n752:             BinOp::Sub => format!(\"(({l} - {r}) | tl_i32)\"),\n753:             other => unreachable!(\"{other} is not arithmetic\"),\n754:         }\n755:     }\n756: }\n757: \n758: fn jq_op(op: BinOp) -> &'static str {\n759:     match op {\n760:         BinOp::Eq => \"==\",\n761:         BinOp::Ne => \"!=\",\n762:         BinOp::Lt => \"<\",\n763:         BinOp::Le => \"<=\",\n764:         BinOp::Gt => \">\",\n765:         BinOp::Ge => \">=\",\n766:         other => unreachable!(\"{other} is not a comparison\"),\n767:     }\n768: }\n769: \n770: /// One enum rebuilt in place. One type, two runtime shapes (ADR 0009): a unit variant is already\n771: /// canonical, being a bare string; a payload wrapper is rebuilt so the payload's own keys come\n772: /// out in the type's order, the same reason records are rebuilt.\n773: fn canonical_enum(enums: &Enums, ty: &Type, value: &str) -> String {\n774:     let variants = ty::variants(enums, ty);\n775:     let payloads: Vec<(&String, &Type)> = variants\n776:         .iter()\n777:         .filter_map(|(n, p)| p.as_ref().map(|p| (n, p)))\n778:         .collect();\n779:     if payloads.is_empty() {\n780:         return value.to_string();\n781:     }\n782:     let wrap = |vname: &String, pty: &Type| {\n783:         format!(\n784:             \"{{{}: {}}}\",\n785:             jq_string(vname),\n786:             canonical(enums, pty, &field_of(\".\", vname))\n787:         )\n788:     };\n789:     // The conditions cover everything but the last shape, which needs no test: the type\n790:     // says nothing else is left. A unit variant, if any exists, is the string shape.\n791:     let mut tests: Vec<String> = Vec::new();\n792:     if payloads.len() < variants.len() {\n793:         tests.push(\"if type == \\\"string\\\" then .\".to_string());\n794:     }\n795:     for (vname, pty) in &payloads[..payloads.len() - 1] {\n796:         let word = if tests.is_empty() { \"if\" } else { \"elif\" };\n797:         tests.push(format!(\n798:             \"{word} has({}) then {}\",\n799:             jq_string(vname),\n800:             wrap(vname, pty)\n801:         ));\n802:     }\n803:     let (last_name, last_ty) = payloads[payloads.len() - 1];\n804:     let last = wrap(last_name, last_ty);\n805:     if tests.is_empty() {\n806:         return format!(\"({value} | {last})\");\n807:     }\n808:     format!(\"({value} | {} else {last} end)\", tests.join(\" \"))\n809: }\n810: \n811: /// A named filter for every recursive enum the program prints, in callee-before-caller order.\n812: /// The call in `canonical` above is what a nested occurrence rebuilds through, so the recursion\n813: /// in the type becomes recursion in the emitted filter rather than in this compiler\n814: /// (kantord/toylang#94). jq's `def` has no forward declaration, so the same topsort `ordered`\n815: /// uses applies here: one printer can call another only when the callee is already defined above\n816: /// it. Two enums that reach each other through `Vec` typecheck legally, but their printers form a\n817: /// genuine cycle jq cannot take, so it is refused rather than emitting source that fails with\n818: /// jq's own raw error (kantord/toylang#116).\n819: fn printers(program: &Program) -> Result<String, String> {\n820:     let ordered = topsort(\n821:         tir::printed_recursive_enums(program),\n822:         |ty| ty.show_fn(),\n823:         |ty| printer_deps(&program.enums, ty),\n824:         \"recursive-enum printers\",\n825:     )?;\n826:     let mut out = String::new();\n827:     for ty in ordered {\n828:         out.push_str(&format!(\n829:             \"def {}: {};\\n\",\n830:             ty.show_fn(),\n831:             canonical_enum(&program.enums, &ty, \".\")\n832:         ));\n833:     }\n834:     Ok(out)\n835: }\n836: \n837: /// The `tl_show_*` names a printer for `ty` calls: every recursive enum nested inside it, `ty`\n838: /// itself excluded, since self-recursion is the one back-edge jq's `def` can express. `seen`\n839: /// stops a recursive type from being walked forever; the first time each enum is met it is\n840: /// recorded and its payloads descended, so every distinct reachable enum shows up exactly once.\n841: fn printer_deps(enums: &Enums, ty: &Type) -> Vec<String> {\n842:     fn collect(\n843:         enums: &Enums,\n844:         ty: &Type,\n845:         root: &Type,\n846:         seen: &mut Vec<Type>,\n847:         deps: &mut Vec<String>,\n848:     ) {\n849:         match ty {\n850:             Type::Vec(e) | Type::Stream(e) => collect(enums, e, root, seen, deps),\n851:             Type::Record(fields) => {\n852:                 for (_, f) in fields {\n853:                     collect(enums, f, root, seen, deps);\n854:                 }\n855:             }\n856:             Type::Enum { name, args, .. } => {\n857:                 if seen.contains(ty) {\n858:                     return;\n859:                 }\n860:                 seen.push(ty.clone());\n861:                 if ty != root && crate::ty::is_recursive(enums, ty) {\n862:                     deps.push(ty.show_fn());\n863:                 }\n864:                 for arg in args {\n865:                     collect(enums, arg, root, seen, deps);\n866:                 }\n867:                 for (_, payload) in ty::variants_of(enums, name, args) {\n868:                     if let Some(p) = payload {\n869:                         collect(enums, &p, root, seen, deps);\n870:                     }\n871:                 }\n872:             }\n873:             _ => {}\n874:         }\n875:     }\n876:     let mut deps = Vec::new();\n877:     collect(enums, ty, ty, &mut Vec::new(), &mut deps);\n878:     deps\n879: }\n880: \n881: fn jq_string(s: &str) -> String {\n882:     let mut out = String::with_capacity(s.len() + 2);\n883:     out.push('\"');\n884:     for c in s.chars() {\n885:         match c {\n886:             '\"' => out.push_str(\"\\\\\\\"\"),\n887:             '\\\\' => out.push_str(\"\\\\\\\\\"),\n888:             '\\n' => out.push_str(\"\\\\n\"),\n889:             '\\r' => out.push_str(\"\\\\r\"),\n890:             '\\t' => out.push_str(\"\\\\t\"),\n891:             c if (c as u32) < 0x20 || c == '\\u{7f}' => {\n892:                 out.push_str(&format!(\"\\\\u{:04x}\", c as u32));\n893:             }\n894:             c => out.push(c),\n895:         }\n896:     }\n897:     out.push('\"');\n898:     out\n899: }\n\n(End of file - total 899 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"//! The jq backend.\n//!\n//! The other three targets are imperative and this one is not, so it is the only backend where\n//! the compiler has to say what it means in stream terms. That makes it a check on the\n//! relationship to jq rather than another way to run a program: everything the design diverges\n//! on has to be bridged here explicitly.\n//!\n//! Two mappings are worth naming. A dimension spec becomes `.[]` plus a reification, since\n//! keeping a dimension in a stream language means iterating and collecting. And `Opt` carries\n//! its enum's own runtime shape even here -- `{\"some\": v}` present, `\"none\"` absent -- tagged\n//! in memory like every backend now, with `null` appearing only when the printer flattens the\n//! tags away. One consequence is load-bearing: no in-memory value is ever JSON null, so jq's\n//! own null (an out-of-range `.[i]`) still unambiguously means \"was not there\".\n\nuse crate::ast::BinOp;\nuse crate::tir::{self, Builtin, Kind, LocalId, Program, Tir};\nuse crate::ty::{self, Enums, Type};\n\n/// The binding stdin is read into. jq puts the input in `.`, which this backend needs for the\n/// subject, so it is bound away before the program starts.","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/src/emit_jq.rs","text":"//! The jq backend.\n//!\n//! The other three targets are imperative and this one is not, so it is the only backend where\n//! the compiler has to say what it means in stream terms. That makes it a check on the\n//! relationship to jq rather than another way to run a program: everything the design diverges\n//! on has to be bridged here explicitly.\n//!\n//! Two mappings are worth naming. A dimension spec becomes `.[]` plus a reification, since\n//! keeping a dimension in a stream language means iterating and collecting. And `Opt` carries\n//! its enum's own runtime shape even here -- `{\"some\": v}` present, `\"none\"` absent -- tagged\n//! in memory like every backend now, with `null` appearing only when the printer flattens the\n//! tags away. One consequence is load-bearing: no in-memory value is ever JSON null, so jq's\n//! own null (an out-of-range `.[i]`) still unambiguously means \"was not there\".\n\nuse crate::ast::BinOp;\nuse crate::tir::{self, Builtin, Kind, LocalId, Program, Tir};\nuse crate::ty::{self, Enums, Type};\n\n/// The binding stdin is read into. jq puts the input in `.`, which this backend needs for the\n/// subject, so it is bound away before the program starts.\nconst INPUT: &str = \"$t_input\";\nconst INPUTS: &str = \"$t_inputs\";\n\n/// jq has no integer type, so 32-bit wrapping is arithmetic on doubles. Addition and\n/// subtraction stay exact because the result never passes 2^33; multiplication does not, since\n/// the true product needs 62 bits, so it goes through 16-bit halves whose partial products each\n/// stay under 2^53. Verified against C and Math.imul, including -2147483648 * -1.\nconst ARITH_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_i32: . as $r\n  | ((($r % 4294967296) + 4294967296) % 4294967296)\n  | if . >= 2147483648 then . - 4294967296 else . end;\ndef tl_mul($a; $b):\n  ($a | tl_i32) as $x | ($b | tl_i32) as $y\n  | (if $x < 0 then $x + 4294967296 else $x end) as $ua\n  | (if $y < 0 then $y + 4294967296 else $y end) as $ub\n  | ($ua / 65536 | floor) as $ah | ($ua % 65536) as $al\n  | ($ub / 65536 | floor) as $bh | ($ub % 65536) as $bl\n  | (($al * $bl) + ((($ah * $bl) + ($al * $bh)) % 65536) * 65536) | tl_i32;\ndef tl_div($a; $b):\n  if $b == 0 then error(\"toylang: divided by zero\")\n  else ($a / $b | trunc) | tl_i32 end;\ndef tl_rem($a; $b):\n  if $b == 0 then error(\"toylang: divided by zero\") else ($a % $b) | tl_i32 end;\n\"#;\n\n/// Int64 arithmetic on doubles, exact only within +/-2^53 (kantord/toylang#83). That boundary\n/// is documented rather than papered over: 32-bit wrapping could be emulated exactly through\n/// 16-bit halves because every partial product fit a double, and no such split reassembles a\n/// 64-bit product or a mod-2^64 wrap from pieces a double can hold. So `+`, `-` and `*` are\n/// jq's own, `%` casts through C's 64-bit integers inside jq itself, and a program whose\n/// Int64 values stay within 2^53 -- every corpus case does -- agrees with the other six\n/// backends; one that leaves it diverges here, and docs/reference/types/int64.md says so.\nconst ARITH64_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_div64($a; $b):\n  if $b == 0 then error(\"toylang: divided by zero\") else ($a / $b | trunc) end;\ndef tl_rem64($a; $b):\n  if $b == 0 then error(\"toylang: divided by zero\") else ($a % $b) end;\n\"#;\n\npub fn emit(program: &Program) -> Result<String, String> {\n    let enums = &program.enums;\n    let mut out = String::new();\n    let (arith, arith64) = uses_arith(program);\n    if arith {\n        out.push_str(ARITH_HELPER);\n    }\n    if arith64 {\n        out.push_str(ARITH64_HELPER);\n    }\n\n    out.push_str(&printers(program)?);\n\n    // jq resolves a `def` only against what is already defined, so definitions have to come out\n    // callee-first. The checker collects every signature before checking any body and therefore\n    // accepts a call to a function defined further down, which is a rule this target does not\n    // share. Lua needed forward declarations for the same reason; jq has no way to write one.\n    for f in ordered(program)? {\n        // A unary function's argument arrives as `.` and is bound before the body runs; a\n        // nullary one ignores `.` entirely, since it has nothing to bind.\n        out.push_str(&match &f.param {\n            Some(param) => format!(\n                \"def {}: . as ${} | {};\\n\",\n                user(&f.name),\n                user(param),\n                expr(enums, &f.body)\n            ),\n            None => format!(\"def {}: {};\\n\", user(&f.name), expr(enums, &f.body)),\n        });\n    }\n\n    if let Some(fusion) = tir::fusion(program) {\n        // jq's own `inputs` is already lazy; the eager path below only becomes eager by wrapping\n        // it in `[...]`. Skipping that wrapper and running the whole `map`/`select` chain as one\n        // filter over the `inputs` generator is what makes jq print each record as it arrives\n        // rather than after the last one, the same as running the equivalent `jq` program by\n        // hand would.\n        out.push_str(\"inputs\");\n        for stage in &fusion.stages {\n            match stage {\n                tir::Stage::Map { param, body } => {\n                    out.push_str(&format!(\n                        \" | . as {} | {}\",\n                        local(*param),\n                        expr(enums, body)\n                    ));\n                }\n                tir::Stage::Select { param, pred } => {\n                    out.push_str(&format!(\n                        \" | . as {} | select({})\",\n                        local(*param),\n                        expr(enums, pred)\n                    ));\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        let Kind::Builtin { arg, .. } = &program.body.kind else {\n            unreachable!(\"fusion only matches a jsonlines body\")\n        };\n        let elem = tir::runtime_elem(&arg.ty).expect(\"jsonlines's argument has an element\");\n        out.push_str(&format!(\" | ({} | tojson)\\n\", canonical(enums, elem, \".\")));\n        return Ok(out);\n    }\n\n    if program.input.is_some() {\n        out.push_str(&format!(\". as {INPUT} | \"));\n    }\n    // `-n` (added whenever the invocation has no value already in `.`, run_jq's job) is what\n    // makes `inputs` mean \"every value on stdin\" rather than \"every value after the first.\"\n    if program.inputs.is_some() {\n        out.push_str(&format!(\"[inputs] as {INPUTS} | \"));\n    }\n    // Records are rebuilt in the type's field order, because jq preserves insertion order and\n    // an object read from input carries whatever order the input had.\n    out.push_str(&canonical(\n        enums,\n        &program.body.ty,\n        &expr(enums, &program.body),\n    ));\n    out.push('\\n');\n    Ok(out)\n}\n\n/// Reconstruct a value with keys in the type's order, so the printed form matches the other\n/// backends rather than the input's key order.\nfn canonical(enums: &Enums, ty: &Type, value: &str) -> String {\n    match ty {\n        Type::Param(_) => unreachable!(\"params are substituted before emit\"),\n        // The checker refuses a program whose result contains a stream, since there is nothing to\n        // print: a stream has no value, only a promise that collect can redeem.\n        Type::Stream(_) => unreachable!(\"a stream cannot reach the printer\"),\n        Type::Char => unreachable!(\"Char cannot reach the printer, refused by the checker\"),\n        Type::Str | Type::Int | Type::Int64 | Type::Bool => value.to_string(),\n        Type::Vec(elem) => format!(\"[ {value}[] | {} ]\", canonical(enums, elem, \".\")),\n        Type::Enum { .. } if ty.as_opt().is_some() => {\n            let inner = ty.as_opt().expect(\"guarded\");\n            format!(\n                \"({value} | if . == \\\"none\\\" then null else (.some | {}) end)\",\n                canonical(enums, inner, \".\")\n            )\n        }\n        // A recursive enum is rebuilt by a filter of its own (`printers`), because expanding one\n        // here has no bottom: its payload leads back to the same type.\n        Type::Enum { .. } if ty::is_recursive(enums, ty) => {\n            format!(\"({value} | {})\", ty.show_fn())\n        }\n        Type::Enum { .. } => canonical_enum(enums, ty, value),\n        Type::Record(fields) => {\n            let parts: Vec<String> = fields\n                .iter()\n                .map(|(name, fty)| {\n                    format!(\n                        \"{}: {}\",\n                        jq_string(name),\n                        canonical(enums, fty, &field_of(\".\", name))\n                    )\n                })\n                .collect();\n            format!(\"({value} | {{{}}})\", parts.join(\", \"))\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n/// Every function name a `Kind::Call` inside `t` names, in the order encountered, duplicates and\n/// all -- `ordered` only ever asks whether a name is present, and a cycle's error message reads\n/// better walking a real call rather than a deduplicated set.\nfn callees(t: &Tir, out: &mut Vec<String>) {\n    match &t.kind {\n        Kind::Str(_)\n        | Kind::Int(_)\n        | Kind::Var(_)\n        | Kind::Local(_)\n        | Kind::Input\n        | Kind::Inputs\n        | Kind::Lines => {}\n        Kind::VecLit(items) => items.iter().for_each(|i| callees(i, out)),\n        Kind::RecordLit { fields } => {\n            fields.iter().for_each(|(_, v)| callees(v, out));\n        }\n        Kind::EnumLit { payload, .. } => {\n            if let Some(p) = payload {\n                callees(p, out);\n            }\n        }\n        Kind::Call { func, arg } => {\n            out.push(func.clone());\n            if let Some(a) = arg {\n                callees(a, out);\n            }\n        }\n        Kind::Concat(l, r)\n        | Kind::Compare { lhs: l, rhs: r, .. }\n        | Kind::Logic { lhs: l, rhs: r, .. } => {\n            callees(l, out);\n            callees(r, out);\n        }\n        Kind::Bind { value, body, .. } => {\n            callees(value, out);\n            callees(body, out);\n        }\n        Kind::Select { source, pred, .. } => {\n            callees(source, out);\n            callees(pred, out);\n        }\n        Kind::Map { source, body, .. } | Kind::OptMap { source, body, .. } => {\n            callees(source, out);\n            callees(body, out);\n        }\n        Kind::Builtin { arg, .. } => callees(arg, out),\n        Kind::Cond {\n            cond,\n            then,\n            otherwise,\n        } => {\n            callees(cond, out);\n            callees(then, out);\n            callees(otherwise, out);\n        }\n        Kind::Arith { lhs, rhs, .. } => {\n            callees(lhs, out);\n            callees(rhs, out);\n        }\n        Kind::Field { base, .. } | Kind::Unwrap { base } | Kind::Not(base) => callees(base, out),\n        Kind::Index { base, index, .. } => {\n            callees(base, out);\n            callees(index, out);\n        }\n        Kind::Match { subject, arms, .. } => {\n            callees(subject, out);\n            for a in arms {\n                if let Some(g) = &a.guard {\n                    callees(g, out);\n                }\n                callees(&a.body, out);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n/// Definitions in callee-before-caller order, or the cycle blocking one: jq's `def` sees only\n/// itself and whatever is already defined above it, with no forward declaration to bridge a\n/// real cycle between two or more named functions (kantord/toylang#79). Self-recursion never\n/// gets stuck here -- a function calling only itself is always immediately ready -- so reaching\n/// the stuck state below means the remaining functions have a genuine cycle among them.\nfn ordered(program: &Program) -> Result<Vec<&tir::Func>, String> {\n    topsort(\n        program.funcs.iter().collect(),\n        |f| f.name.clone(),\n        |f| {\n            let mut calls = Vec::new();\n            callees(&f.body, &mut calls);\n            calls\n        },\n        \"named functions\",\n    )\n}\n\n/// Callee-before-caller order over a list of named things, or the cycle blocking one. `name`\n/// yields each item's `def` name and `deps` the names it calls; `kind` names what `remaining`\n/// holds, for the refusal message alone. Both the named functions (`ordered`) and the\n/// recursive-enum printers (`printers`) go through here, because jq's `def` has the same reach on\n/// each: only itself and what is already defined above it, with no forward declaration\n/// (kantord/toylang#79, kantord/toylang#116).\nfn topsort<T>(\n    mut remaining: Vec<T>,\n    name: impl Fn(&T) -> String,\n    deps: impl Fn(&T) -> Vec<String>,\n    kind: &str,\n) -> Result<Vec<T>, String> {\n    let mut done: Vec<T> = Vec::new();\n    let mut placed: Vec<String> = Vec::new();\n    while !remaining.is_empty() {\n        let ready: Vec<usize> = remaining\n            .iter()\n            .enumerate()\n            .filter(|(_, item)| {\n                let n = name(item);\n                deps(item).iter().all(|c| c == &n || placed.contains(c))\n            })\n            .map(|(i, _)| i)\n            .collect();\n        if ready.is_empty() {\n            return Err(cycle_message(&remaining, &name, &deps, kind));\n        }\n        for i in ready.into_iter().rev() {\n            let item = remaining.remove(i);\n            placed.push(name(&item));\n            done.push(item);\n        }\n    }\n    Ok(done)\n}\n\n/// A concrete cycle among `remaining`'s items, walked by following one unresolved call from\n/// each to the next: every item still in `remaining` has at least one such call (otherwise it\n/// would have been ready), so the walk is finite and must revisit a name. `kind` is `topsort`'s\n/// own, unchanged, naming `remaining`'s contents in the message.\nfn cycle_message<T>(\n    remaining: &[T],\n    name: &impl Fn(&T) -> String,\n    deps: &impl Fn(&T) -> Vec<String>,\n    kind: &str,\n) -> String {\n    let names: Vec<String> = remaining.iter().map(name).collect();\n    let mut path: Vec<String> = Vec::new();\n    let mut current = name(&remaining[0]);\n    loop {\n        if let Some(at) = path.iter().position(|n| *n == current) {\n            let cycle = &path[at..];\n            let chain: Vec<String> = cycle.iter().map(|n| format!(\"`{n}`\")).collect();\n            return format!(\n                \"jq cannot compile this: {} -> `{}` is a cycle between {kind}, and jq's `def` \\\n                 has no forward declaration -- only self-recursion and a call to something \\\n                 already defined above it are representable here\",\n                chain.join(\" -> \"),\n                cycle[0],\n            );\n        }\n        path.push(current.clone());\n        let item = remaining\n            .iter()\n            .find(|f| name(f) == current)\n            .expect(\"current is a name drawn from remaining\");\n        current = deps(item)\n            .into_iter()\n            .find(|c| c != &name(item) && names.contains(c))\n            .expect(\"a stuck item has at least one unresolved call within remaining\");\n    }\n}\n\n/// Whether the program does 32-bit arithmetic, 64-bit arithmetic, or both: the node's type\n/// says which width each `Arith` is, so the two helper blocks are included independently.\nfn uses_arith(program: &Program) -> (bool, bool) {\n    fn walk(t: &Tir, found: &mut (bool, bool)) {\n        match &t.kind {\n            Kind::Arith { lhs, rhs, .. } => {\n                if t.ty == Type::Int64 {\n                    found.1 = true;\n                } else {\n                    found.0 = true;\n                }\n                walk(lhs, found);\n                walk(rhs, found);\n            }\n            Kind::Cond {\n                cond,\n                then,\n                otherwise,\n            } => {\n                walk(cond, found);\n                walk(then, found);\n                walk(otherwise, found);\n            }\n            Kind::Str(_)\n            | Kind::Int(_)\n            | Kind::Var(_)\n            | Kind::Local(_)\n            | Kind::Input\n            | Kind::Inputs\n            | Kind::Lines => {}\n            Kind::VecLit(items) => items.iter().for_each(|i| walk(i, found)),\n            Kind::RecordLit { fields } => fields.iter().for_each(|(_, v)| walk(v, found)),\n            Kind::EnumLit { payload, .. } => {\n                if let Some(p) = payload {\n                    walk(p, found);\n                }\n            }\n            Kind::Call { arg, .. } => {\n                if let Some(a) = arg {\n                    walk(a, found);\n                }\n            }\n            Kind::Builtin { which, arg } => {\n                // `sum` over Int narrows through `tl_i32` in its fold, so the helper that\n                // defines it has to be present alongside.\n                if *which == Builtin::Sum && tir::runtime_elem(&arg.ty) == Some(&Type::Int) {\n                    found.0 = true;\n                }\n                walk(arg, found);\n            }\n            Kind::Concat(l, r)\n            | Kind::Compare { lhs: l, rhs: r, .. }\n            | Kind::Logic { lhs: l, rhs: r, .. } => {\n                walk(l, found);\n                walk(r, found);\n            }\n            Kind::Bind { value, body, .. }\n            | Kind::Map {\n                source: value,\n                body,\n                ..\n            }\n            | Kind::OptMap {\n                source: value,\n                body,\n                ..\n            } => {\n                walk(value, found);\n                walk(body, found);\n            }\n            Kind::Select { source, pred, .. } => {\n                walk(source, found);\n                walk(pred, found);\n            }\n            Kind::Field { base, .. } | Kind::Unwrap { base } | Kind::Not(base) => walk(base, found),\n            Kind::Index { base, index, .. } => {\n                walk(base, found);\n                walk(index, found);\n            }\n            Kind::Match { subject, arms, .. } => {\n                walk(subject, found);\n                for a in arms {\n                    if let Some(g) = &a.guard {\n                        walk(g, found);\n                    }\n                    walk(&a.body, found);\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    let mut found = (false, false);\n    program.funcs.iter().for_each(|f| walk(&f.body, &mut found));\n    walk(&program.body, &mut found);\n    found\n}\n\nfn user(name: &str) -> String {\n    format!(\"v_{name}\")\n}\n\nfn local(id: LocalId) -> String {\n    format!(\"$t_{id}\")\n}\n\nfn field_of(base: &str, name: &str) -> String {\n    format!(\"{base}[{}]\", jq_string(name))\n}\n\n/// Apply `inner` one dimension down, `depth` times. Keeping a dimension in a stream language is\n/// iterate-and-collect, which is why every level costs a `[ .[] | ... ]`.\nfn distribute(inner: &str, depth: usize) -> String {\n    if depth == 0 {\n        inner.to_string()\n    } else {\n        format!(\"[ .[] | {} ]\", distribute(inner, depth - 1))\n    }\n}\n\nfn expr(enums: &Enums, t: &Tir) -> String {\n    match &t.kind {\n        Kind::Str(s) => jq_string(s),\n        Kind::Int(n) => n.to_string(),\n        Kind::Var(name) => format!(\"${}\", user(name)),\n        Kind::Local(id) => local(*id),\n        Kind::Input => INPUT.to_string(),\n        Kind::Inputs => INPUTS.to_string(),\n        // The stream, materialized eagerly: `[ inputs ]` in raw-input mode is every line of\n        // stdin as an array of strings. `-n -R` on the invocation is what makes this mode\n        // available; see the checker rule against mixing `input` and `lines` in one program.\n        Kind::Lines => \"[ inputs ]\".to_string(),\n        // Each value is parenthesised: everything in jq is a filter, so an unbracketed `|`\n        // or `,` inside one would be read as part of the object rather than as its value.\n        Kind::RecordLit { fields } => {\n            let parts: Vec<String> = fields\n                .iter()\n                .map(|(name, value)| format!(\"{}: ({})\", jq_string(name), expr(enums, value)))\n                .collect();\n            format!(\"{{{}}}\", parts.join(\", \"))\n        }\n\n        // The value is its JSON shape, which jq spells directly.\n        Kind::EnumLit { variant, payload } => match payload {\n            None => jq_string(variant),\n            Some(p) => format!(\"{{{}: ({})}}\", jq_string(variant), expr(enums, p)),\n        },\n\n        Kind::VecLit(items) => {\n            let parts: Vec<String> = items.iter().map(|i| expr(enums, i)).collect();\n            format!(\"[{}]\", parts.join(\", \"))\n        }\n        // A nullary function ignores `.`, so its call is just its name, run against whatever\n        // is already flowing through the surrounding pipeline.\n        Kind::Call { func, arg } => match arg {\n            Some(arg) => format!(\"({} | {})\", expr(enums, arg), user(func)),\n            None => format!(\"({})\", user(func)),\n        },\n        // jq's own `+` already joins two arrays the way it joins two strings, so a Vec needs\n        // no different spelling here than Str does.\n        Kind::Concat(l, r) => format!(\"({} + {})\", expr(enums, l), expr(enums, r)),\n        Kind::Arith { op, lhs, rhs } => arith(&t.ty, *op, expr(enums, lhs), expr(enums, rhs)),\n        Kind::Cond {\n            cond,\n            then,\n            otherwise,\n        } => format!(\n            \"(if {} then {} else {} end)\",\n            expr(enums, cond),\n            expr(enums, then),\n            expr(enums, otherwise)\n        ),\n        Kind::Builtin { which, arg } => match which {\n            Builtin::IntToStr => format!(\"({} | tostring)\", expr(enums, arg)),\n            // jq has one number type at every width, so the bridge has nothing to do.\n            Builtin::IntToI64 => format!(\"({})\", expr(enums, arg)),\n            Builtin::Range => format!(\"[ range(0; {}) ]\", expr(enums, arg)),\n            // `explode` already decodes jq's UTF-8 string by codepoint, not by byte, so there\n            // is no decoding to get right here.\n            Builtin::Chars => format!(\"({} | explode)\", expr(enums, arg)),\n            // `canonical` reorders a record's keys but leaves the *value*, not text; `tojson`\n            // is what turns each element into the same compact JSON string `-c` would print for\n            // it, matching every other backend's per-element encoding.\n            Builtin::JsonLines => {\n                let elem = tir::runtime_elem(&arg.ty).expect(\"checked to be a Vec or a stream\");\n                format!(\n                    \"({} | [.[] | ({} | tojson)] | join(\\\"\\\\n\\\"))\",\n                    expr(enums, arg),\n                    canonical(enums, elem, \".\")\n                )\n            }\n            // The source already materialized, so the exit has nothing left to do.\n            Builtin::Collect => expr(enums, arg),\n            Builtin::Length => format!(\"({} | length)\", expr(enums, arg)),\n            // jq's own `.[1:]` on an empty array is `[]`, not null; toylang's tail needs the\n            // tagged Opt shape instead, so both cases are spelled out rather than borrowed.\n            Builtin::Tail => {\n                format!(\n                    \"({} | if length == 0 then \\\"none\\\" else {{some: .[1:]}} end)\",\n                    expr(enums, arg)\n                )\n            }\n            // Not jq's own `add`, which is `null` on an empty list rather than `[]` -- a reduce\n            // starting from `[]` gives the right answer in both cases.\n            Builtin::Flatten => format!(\"({} | reduce .[] as $x ([]; . + $x))\", expr(enums, arg)),\n            // jq's own `sort`/`reverse` already are this, restricted the same way the checker\n            // restricts `sort` to Int, Int64, Str, and Char.\n            Builtin::Sort => format!(\"({} | sort)\", expr(enums, arg)),\n            Builtin::Reverse => format!(\"({} | reverse)\", expr(enums, arg)),\n            // `reduce` starting from 0 is `add`'s empty-is-0 shape with the wrap `+` applies to\n            // an Int spelled explicitly; Int64's fold is plain `+`, exact within the 2^53 the\n            // module comment already owns.\n            Builtin::Sum => {\n                if tir::runtime_elem(&arg.ty) == Some(&Type::Int) {\n                    format!(\n                        \"({} | reduce .[] as $x (0; ((. + $x) | tl_i32)))\",\n                        expr(enums, arg)\n                    )\n                } else {\n                    format!(\"({} | reduce .[] as $x (0; . + $x))\", expr(enums, arg))\n                }\n            }\n            // jq's own `max` on an empty list errors, and on a non-empty one is exactly this,\n            // so only the empty case is spelled: the absent Opt, tagged the way `Tail` tags.\n            Builtin::Max => format!(\n                \"({} | if length == 0 then \\\"none\\\" else {{some: max}} end)\",\n                expr(enums, arg)\n            ),\n            // The names come from the checked type, not the object value, so `arg` runs only to\n            // become the `.` a literal array then ignores -- the same discard the pipe already\n            // gives every other builtin here.\n            Builtin::Fields => {\n                let Type::Record(fields) = &arg.ty else {\n                    unreachable!(\"checked to be a record\")\n                };\n                let names: Vec<String> = fields.iter().map(|(n, _)| jq_string(n)).collect();\n                format!(\"({} | [{}])\", expr(enums, arg), names.join(\", \"))\n            }\n        },\n        Kind::Compare { op, lhs, rhs } => {\n            format!(\"({} {} {})\", expr(enums, lhs), jq_op(*op), expr(enums, rhs))\n        }\n        // jq spells them the same way toylang does, and short-circuits them the same way too.\n        Kind::Logic { op, lhs, rhs } => format!(\"({} {op} {})\", expr(enums, lhs), expr(enums, rhs)),\n        Kind::Not(base) => format!(\"({} | not)\", expr(enums, base)),\n        Kind::Bind {\n            local: id,\n            value,\n            body,\n        } => {\n            format!(\n                \"({} as {} | {})\",\n                expr(enums, value),\n                local(*id),\n                expr(enums, body)\n            )\n        }\n        // The one operator that is derived in jq and primitive here: `map(f)` is `[ .[] | f ]`\n        // there, and neither half of that exists in a language with no effect layer.\n        Kind::Map {\n            source,\n            param,\n            body,\n        } => format!(\n            \"[ {}[] | . as {} | {} ]\",\n            expr(enums, source),\n            local(*param),\n            expr(enums, body)\n        ),\n        Kind::Select {\n            source,\n            param,\n            pred,\n        } => format!(\n            \"[ {}[] | . as {} | select({}) ]\",\n            expr(enums, source),\n            local(*param),\n            expr(enums, pred)\n        ),\n        Kind::Field { base, name } => {\n            let depth = tir::vec_depth(&base.ty);\n            format!(\n                \"({} | {})\",\n                expr(enums, base),\n                distribute(&field_of(\".\", name), depth)\n            )\n        }\n        Kind::Unwrap { base } => {\n            let check = format!(\n                \"if . == \\\"none\\\" then error({}) else .some end\",\n                jq_string(\"toylang: unwrapped a value that is not there\")\n            );\n            format!(\n                \"({} | {})\",\n                expr(enums, base),\n                distribute(&check, tir::vec_depth(&base.ty))\n            )\n        }\n        // Opt's reorder pass (kantord/toylang#66): the same `== \"none\"`/`.some` shape the\n        // printer and Match already read, generalised to rebuild the object instead.\n        Kind::OptMap {\n            source,\n            param,\n            body,\n        } => format!(\n            \"({} | if . == \\\"none\\\" then \\\"none\\\" else (.some as {} | {{some: {}}}) end)\",\n            expr(enums, source),\n            local(*param),\n            expr(enums, body)\n        ),\n        // Out of range is null in jq, and no in-memory toylang value is ever null (the\n        // module comment above), so the null test is exactly the was-not-there test; what\n        // comes out is the tagged Opt either way.\n        Kind::Index {\n            base, index, depth, ..\n        } => {\n            let at = format!(\n                \"(.[{}] as $e | if $e == null then \\\"none\\\" else {{some: $e}} end)\",\n                expr(enums, index)\n            );\n            format!(\"({} | {})\", expr(enums, base), distribute(&at, *depth))\n        }\n        // Tests over the subject: equality for a unit variant, `type`-guarded `has` for a\n        // payload one, since `has` on a string is an error rather than false, and the guard's\n        // own Bool for a guard arm. A total chain's last arm is the `else`, the checker having\n        // proved nothing else can reach it; a partial chain tags every present arm and its\n        // `else` is the absent Opt.\n        Kind::Match {\n            subject,\n            arms,\n            partial,\n        } => {\n            let subj = expr(enums, subject);\n            let run = |arm: &tir::MatchArm| match arm.payload {\n                Some(pid) => {\n                    let variant = arm\n                        .variant\n                        .as_ref()\n                        .expect(\"only a variant arm has a payload\");\n                    format!(\n                        \"({subj}[{}] as {} | {})\",\n                        jq_string(variant),\n                        local(pid),\n                        expr(enums, &arm.body)\n                    )\n                }\n                None => expr(enums, &arm.body),\n            };\n            let run = |arm: &tir::MatchArm| {\n                if *partial {\n                    // A partial chain's yield is an Opt, so a present arm is tagged.\n                    format!(\"{{some: {}}}\", run(arm))\n                } else {\n                    run(arm)\n                }\n            };\n            if !*partial && arms.len() == 1 {\n                return format!(\"({})\", run(&arms[0]));\n            }\n            let mut out = String::from(\"(\");\n            for (i, arm) in arms.iter().enumerate() {\n                let test = match (&arm.variant, &arm.guard) {\n                    (Some(v), _) if arm.payload.is_some() => Some(format!(\n                        \"({subj} | type == \\\"object\\\" and has({}))\",\n                        jq_string(v)\n                    )),\n                    (Some(v), _) => Some(format!(\"{subj} == {}\", jq_string(v))),\n                    (None, Some(g)) => Some(expr(enums, g)),\n                    (None, None) => None,\n                };\n                match test {\n                    Some(test) if *partial || i + 1 < arms.len() => {\n                        let word = if i == 0 { \"if\" } else { \"elif\" };\n                        out.push_str(&format!(\"{word} {test} then {} \", run(arm)));\n                    }\n                    _ => out.push_str(&format!(\"else {} end\", run(arm))),\n                }\n            }\n            if *partial {\n                out.push_str(\"else \\\"none\\\" end\");\n            }\n            out.push(')');\n            out\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n/// One arithmetic expression at the width the node's type names. The 64-bit side stays in\n/// jq's doubles, exact within +/-2^53 and honestly not past it (the ARITH64_HELPER comment);\n/// nothing wraps there, since no double could carry the wrapped value back.\nfn arith(ty: &Type, op: BinOp, l: String, r: String) -> String {\n    if *ty == Type::Int64 {\n        match op {\n            BinOp::Div => format!(\"tl_div64({l}; {r})\"),\n            BinOp::Rem => format!(\"tl_rem64({l}; {r})\"),\n            BinOp::Mul => format!(\"({l} * {r})\"),\n            BinOp::Add => format!(\"({l} + {r})\"),\n            BinOp::Sub => format!(\"({l} - {r})\"),\n            other => unreachable!(\"{other} is not arithmetic\"),\n        }\n    } else {\n        match op {\n            BinOp::Div => format!(\"tl_div({l}; {r})\"),\n            BinOp::Rem => format!(\"tl_rem({l}; {r})\"),\n            BinOp::Mul => format!(\"tl_mul({l}; {r})\"),\n            BinOp::Add => format!(\"(({l} + {r}) | tl_i32)\"),\n            BinOp::Sub => format!(\"(({l} - {r}) | tl_i32)\"),\n            other => unreachable!(\"{other} is not arithmetic\"),\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfn jq_op(op: BinOp) -> &'static str {\n    match op {\n        BinOp::Eq => \"==\",\n        BinOp::Ne => \"!=\",\n        BinOp::Lt => \"<\",\n        BinOp::Le => \"<=\",\n        BinOp::Gt => \">\",\n        BinOp::Ge => \">=\",\n        other => unreachable!(\"{other} is not a comparison\"),\n    }\n}\n\n/// One enum rebuilt in place. One type, two runtime shapes (ADR 0009): a unit variant is already\n/// canonical, being a bare string; a payload wrapper is rebuilt so the payload's own keys come\n/// out in the type's order, the same reason records are rebuilt.\nfn canonical_enum(enums: &Enums, ty: &Type, value: &str) -> String {\n    let variants = ty::variants(enums, ty);\n    let payloads: Vec<(&String, &Type)> = variants\n        .iter()\n        .filter_map(|(n, p)| p.as_ref().map(|p| (n, p)))\n        .collect();\n    if payloads.is_empty() {\n        return value.to_string();\n    }\n    let wrap = |vname: &String, pty: &Type| {\n        format!(\n            \"{{{}: {}}}\",\n            jq_string(vname),\n            canonical(enums, pty, &field_of(\".\", vname))\n        )\n    };\n    // The conditions cover everything but the last shape, which needs no test: the type\n    // says nothing else is left. A unit variant, if any exists, is the string shape.\n    let mut tests: Vec<String> = Vec::new();\n    if payloads.len() < variants.len() {\n        tests.push(\"if type == \\\"string\\\" then .\".to_string());\n    }\n    for (vname, pty) in &payloads[..payloads.len() - 1] {\n        let word = if tests.is_empty() { \"if\" } else { \"elif\" };\n        tests.push(format!(\n            \"{word} has({}) then {}\",\n            jq_string(vname),\n            wrap(vname, pty)\n        ));\n    }\n    let (last_name, last_ty) = payloads[payloads.len() - 1];\n    let last = wrap(last_name, last_ty);\n    if tests.is_empty() {\n        return format!(\"({value} | {last})\");\n    }\n    format!(\"({value} | {} else {last} end)\", tests.join(\" \"))\n}\n\n/// A named filter for every recursive enum the program prints, in callee-before-caller order.\n/// The call in `canonical` above is what a nested occurrence rebuilds through, so the recursion\n/// in the type becomes recursion in the emitted filter rather than in this compiler\n/// (kantord/toylang#94). jq's `def` has no forward declaration, so the same topsort `ordered`\n/// uses applies here: one printer can call another only when the callee is already defined above\n/// it. Two enums that reach each other through `Vec` typecheck legally, but their printers form a\n/// genuine cycle jq cannot take, so it is refused rather than emitting source that fails with\n/// jq's own raw error (kantord/toylang#116).\nfn printers(program: &Program) -> Result<String, String> {\n    let ordered = topsort(\n        tir::printed_recursive_enums(program),\n        |ty| ty.show_fn(),\n        |ty| printer_deps(&program.enums, ty),\n        \"recursive-enum printers\",\n    )?;\n    let mut out = String::new();\n    for ty in ordered {\n        out.push_str(&format!(\n            \"def {}: {};\\n\",\n            ty.show_fn(),\n            canonical_enum(&program.enums, &ty, \".\")\n        ));\n    }\n    Ok(out)\n}\n\n/// The `tl_show_*` names a printer for `ty` calls: every recursive enum nested inside it, `ty`\n/// itself excluded, since self-recursion is the one back-edge jq's `def` can express. `seen`\n/// stops a recursive type from being walked forever; the first time each enum is met it is\n/// recorded and its payloads descended, so every distinct reachable enum shows up exactly once.\nfn printer_deps(enums: &Enums, ty: &Type) -> Vec<String> {\n    fn collect(\n        enums: &Enums,\n        ty: &Type,\n        root: &Type,\n        seen: &mut Vec<Type>,\n        deps: &mut Vec<String>,\n    ) {\n        match ty {\n            Type::Vec(e) | Type::Stream(e) => collect(enums, e, root, seen, deps),\n            Type::Record(fields) => {\n                for (_, f) in fields {\n                    collect(enums, f, root, seen, deps);\n                }\n            }\n            Type::Enum { name, args, .. } => {\n                if seen.contains(ty) {\n                    return;\n                }\n                seen.push(ty.clone());\n                if ty != root && crate::ty::is_recursive(enums, ty) {\n                    deps.push(ty.show_fn());\n                }\n                for arg in args {\n                    collect(enums, arg, root, seen, deps);\n                }\n                for (_, payload) in ty::variants_of(enums, name, args) {\n                    if let Some(p) = payload {\n                        collect(enums, &p, root, seen, deps);\n                    }\n                }\n            }\n            _ => {}\n        }\n    }\n    let mut deps = Vec::new();\n    collect(enums, ty, ty, &mut Vec::new(), &mut deps);\n    deps\n}\n\nfn jq_string(s: &str) -> String {\n    let mut out = String::with_capacity(s.len() + 2);\n    out.push('\"');\n    for c in s.chars() {\n        match c {\n            '\"' => out.push_str(\"\\\\\\\"\"),\n            '\\\\' => out.push_str(\"\\\\\\\\\"),\n            '\\n' => out.push_str(\"\\\\n\"),\n            '\\r' => out.push_str(\"\\\\r\"),\n            '\\t' => out.push_str(\"\\\\t\"),\n            c if (c as u32) < 0x20 || c == '\\u{7f}' => {\n                out.push_str(&format!(\"\\\\u{:04x}\", c as u32));\n            }\n            c => out.push(c),\n        }\n    }\n    out.push('\"');\n    out\n}","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":899,"totalLines":899,"truncated":false}},"title":"src/emit_jq.rs","time":{"start":1788297862656,"end":1788297862659}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"This is a large task. The key question: how do we implement `pipe_through` on all 7 backends? jq can't spawn subprocesses. Hmm.\n\nWait, let me reconsider. Let me look at the issue more carefully:\n\n\"Build the streaming pipe-to-subprocess primitive per the shell-out-design ruling: lines | pipe_through(\\\"grep\\\", [args]) | collect.\"\n\nThe issue also says:\n\n\"This explicitly inherits the open Q35 dependency (plans/questions.md#q35 -- what stdout/stderr are, whether a program writes or returns) since streaming a subprocess's output progressively requires that effect model to exist first. Q35 should land, or at least have a concrete enough shape, before this is implementable.\"\n\nHmm interesting. So the issue says Q35 should land before this is implementable. But the task brief says to go straight to implementing. So presumably Q35 has landed in the current state of the repo (the issue was created at some point, and the repo has advanced). Let me check the current state of plans/questions.md Q35 and whether the design has been resolved.\n\nActually, I read Q35 above. It's still open. But the task brief explicitly says: \"Build the streaming pipe-to-subprocess primitive per the shell-out-design ruling... go straight to implementing.\"\n\nSo I should implement it. The corpus case: `lines | pipe_through(\"grep\", [\"foo\"]) | collect`. For a corpus case, all backends must produce identical output. That means jq must also implement pipe_through.\n\n\n\nLet me think about how jq could do this. jq has `input` and... Hmm. Actually, is there a way in jq to spawn subprocesses? jq is Turing-complete-ish but cannot shell out...\n\nWait, maybe the answer is that the corpus case has `refuses: true`? No - then no backend runs it, and nothing gets tested. But wait - maybe there's a per-backend mechanism I'm missing. Let me re-read the corpus spec in AGENTS.md:\n\n\"Every case runs on every backend. `output` means they all print it and agree; `refuses: true` means they all decline to run it.\"\n\nSo no per-backend. Either all run it or all refuse.\n\n\n\nHmm. But wait, maybe jq CAN shell out. jq can invoke... Actually jq has no syscall or exec. It's a pure filter language. It cannot spawn grep.\n\n\n\nUnless... hmm, wait. Actually, jq DOES have a way to call external programs? Let me think... No, jq is a pure JSON processor. There's no subprocess spawn in jq. There is `$ENV` and... no exec.\n\n\n\nSo implementing pipe_through for jq seems impossible... unless the corpus harness runs jq differently, or pipe_through is somehow implemented at the harness level for jq?\n\nActually wait. Let me reconsider. Maybe the streaming pipeline doesn't have to be implemented in jq as a subprocess; maybe for jq it could... no, jq literally cannot spawn subprocesses. There's no way.\n\n\n\nHmm. Let me look at what tools exist. Let me look at the corpus cases that have `refuses: true` - to see examples of cases where no backend can do something. Let me look at jsonlines_of_lines.yaml - it runs on all backends. Fine.\n\n\n\nLet me reconsider the problem. Maybe the intended approach is that the corpus case is `refuses: true` for pipe_through when the subprocess feature isn't implementable? No, that makes no sense either because then we wouldn't build anything.\n\n\n\n\n\nActually, let me re-read the task brief once more:\n\n\"Build the streaming pipe-to-subprocess primitive per the shell-out-design ruling: lines | pipe_through(\\\"grep\\\", [args]) | collect. Add the corpus case FIRST (tests/corpus/, program/input/output per AGENTS.md), then implement parser/checker/backends until just check is green.\"\n\nSo the brief explicitly says add the corpus case with program/input/output (i.e., `output`, not `refuses`) and make all backends pass.\n\n\n\n\n\nOK so all seven backends must implement `pipe_through`. jq must too. How?\n\nLet me think about jq differently. jq is run as a subprocess by the harness. The program passed to jq is a jq filter. If the toylang program does `lines | pipe_through(\"grep\", [\"foo\"]) | collect`... For jq, could the emitted jq filter somehow cause a grep subprocess to run? No. jq cannot exec.\n\n\n\nUnless... the harness (run_on) could handle pipe_through specially for jq? But the whole point is each backend's emitted code does the work...\n\nHmm wait, actually, let me reconsider. Maybe pipe_through in jq is implemented at the *harness* level? No...\n\nLet me look at how jq handles `lines`. The corpus case lines_cat.yaml (join_lines collect lines) works on jq. How? `lines` maps to `[inputs]` with `-R` flag (raw input mode), reading lines from stdin. Then collect materializes them.\n\n collect on jq... Let me look at emit_jq.rs to see how lines/collect/jsonlines are emitted.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05edbddfe001YqfMA3Ae0gjQTE","sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","messageID":"msg_05edbb5970013kkAUQwSMKbJPM"}}
