{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788297867524,"sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_4661de791ee5402dad310720","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"grep -rln \"refuses: true\" tests/corpus | head -20; echo \"=====\"; grep -rln \"refuses\" tests/corpus | wc -l"},"output":"tests/corpus/div_by_zero.yaml\ntests/corpus/enum_inputs_reject.yaml\ntests/corpus/enum_scalar_input_reject.yaml\ntests/corpus/field_key_case_is_significant.yaml\ntests/corpus/max_empty_unwrap_refuses.yaml\ntests/corpus/opt_unwrap_of_none_refuses.yaml\ntests/corpus/rem_by_zero.yaml\ntests/corpus/tail_of_empty_refuses.yaml\ntests/corpus/unpaired_surrogate_input.yaml\ntests/corpus/unwrap_absent.yaml\n=====\n12\n","metadata":{"output":"tests/corpus/div_by_zero.yaml\ntests/corpus/enum_inputs_reject.yaml\ntests/corpus/enum_scalar_input_reject.yaml\ntests/corpus/field_key_case_is_significant.yaml\ntests/corpus/max_empty_unwrap_refuses.yaml\ntests/corpus/opt_unwrap_of_none_refuses.yaml\ntests/corpus/rem_by_zero.yaml\ntests/corpus/tail_of_empty_refuses.yaml\ntests/corpus/unpaired_surrogate_input.yaml\ntests/corpus/unwrap_absent.yaml\n=====\n12\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"grep -rln \"refuses: true\" tests/corpus | head -20; echo \"=====\"; grep -rln \"refuses\" tests/corpus | wc -l","time":{"start":1788297867520,"end":1788297867522}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"So for jq, the corpus case for `pipe_through` cannot be implemented in jq's own language. jq cannot spawn subprocesses.\n\n\n\nHmm. But wait - maybe I'm wrong. Let me think about what `lines | pipe_through(\"grep\", [\"foo\"]) | collect` means in jq terms. It's: read lines from stdin, run them through the external command grep foo, collect the output lines.\n\n\n\njq CANNOT do this. There's no subprocess spawn in jq.\n\n\n\nSo how does the corpus harness pass for jq? Options:\n\n1. The corpus case for pipe_through has `refuses: true` - all backends refuse to run it. But then why build the feature at all? Because the feature could still be implemented in the other backends and the checker, and the corpus case would pin that all backends refuse... no. Hmm, `refuses: true` means they ALL decline. But the feature is meant to work on real backends...\n\n2. Maybe jq CAN refuse, and the corpus case... no, per-case, not per-backend.\n\n\n\nWait, let me reconsider. Is there a mechanism for per-backend refusals? Let me re-read the AGENTS.md section:\n\n\"Every case runs on every backend. `output` means they all print it and agree; `refuses: true` means they all decline to run it, and what each says while refusing is its own business.\"\n\nSo it's all-or-nothing per case. Hmm.\n\n\n\nLet me reconsider the problem. Maybe `pipe_through` for jq is implemented as... jq has no external command execution. Really, jq is a pure filter. It cannot.\n\n\n\nOK, so let me think about this differently. Maybe the answer is that the corpus case is meant to test... hmm.\n\n\n\nWait. Let me look at whether there is an existing \"refuses\" case that involves a backend-specific limitation... Let me look at the corpus files with refuses: true.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05edbf0e1001UT13Jw4vBJPKmg","sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","messageID":"msg_05edbde170013y8D0Immymppst"}}
