{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788297880461,"sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","part":{"id":"prt_05edc238b001zyodPY4e1N62R6","messageID":"msg_05edc2065001ja7sWQXt1ZHaK8","sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","snapshot":"5a1012ab7eb7388a8de2a55a732be7fdbf823735","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788297889105,"sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_2c33fcb84c3b4bfd847bd82d","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"which jq && jq --version; echo \"=====\"; jq -n 'env | keys[]' 2>&1 | head -5"},"output":"/usr/bin/jq\njq-1.8.2\n=====\n\"AGENT\"\n\"AI_AGENT\"\n\"ATUIN_HISTORY_ID\"\n\"ATUIN_SESSION\"\n\"BUN_INSTALL\"\n","metadata":{"output":"/usr/bin/jq\njq-1.8.2\n=====\n\"AGENT\"\n\"AI_AGENT\"\n\"ATUIN_HISTORY_ID\"\n\"ATUIN_SESSION\"\n\"BUN_INSTALL\"\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"which jq && jq --version; echo \"=====\"; jq -n 'env | keys[]' 2>&1 | head -5","time":{"start":1788297889096,"end":1788297889098}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The shell-out-design board is still `todo`, but the issue #158 has a \"Ruling\" that the maintainer picked full streaming pipe-to-subprocess. So the design was decided in the grill round and this issue is the build of that decision.\n\nNow the crucial question remains: how do we make jq (a pure filter language that cannot exec) implement pipe_through?\n\nLet me reconsider. Maybe the answer is that pipe_through is NOT required to work in jq, and the corpus case handles that differently. But per the harness, all 7 backends must agree...\n\nHold on. Let me look at this from a totally different angle. What if pipe_through is implemented at a *different level* for jq? What if the jq emitted code... jq actually does have a subprocess-like feature? Let me think hard about jq...\n\njq builtins: `input`, `inputs`, `range`, `env`, `$ENV`... There's `input_filename`... no. jq cannot spawn subprocesses. Period.\n\n\n\nUnless jq 1.7+ has something new? Let me check. Actually I recall jq has no external process spawning. It is famously pure.\n\n\n\nOK so if jq cannot do it, then the corpus case cannot demand it. So the corpus case would need to be... `refuses: true`? But then what would we be building?\n\n\n\nWait wait wait. Let me re-read the brief once more: \"Add the corpus case FIRST (tests/corpus/, program/input/output per AGENTS.md).\"\n\nIt says program/input/output. So output. Hmm.\n\n\n\nUnless... maybe the intent is that the corpus case has `output` and the harness somehow skips jq? Let me check if there's a way to mark a case as not-jq... No - deny_unknown_fields, and snapshot lists backends for snapshotting, not for running.\n\n\n\n\n\nOK here's another thought: maybe pipe_through for jq IS possible because... jq can read from files and run... no.\n\n\n\n\n\nHmm, let me look at whether there is a per-backend skip in the agreement harness... No, I read it - it iterates Backend::ALL. Every backend must run and agree, or it's reported as BROKEN.\n\n\n\nSo for a pipe_through corpus case with `output`, all 7 backends must implement it, including jq. jq cannot spawn subprocesses. Contradiction.\n\n\n\nUnless... hmm, wait. What if the jq backend refuses at emit time? Then `run_on` would return an Err, and the corpus harness would report \"BROKEN jq: ...could not run\". So that fails.\n\n\n\nWhat if pipe_through in jq is implemented by having the jq emitted code... no way.\n\n\n\nLet me reconsider the whole thing. Maybe the answer is that jq simply cannot implement this and therefore the corpus case is `refuses: true`... but that contradicts the brief's \"program/input/output\".\n\n\n\nOR maybe the brief expects me to write an ESCALATION.md about this fundamental blocker. The brief says: \"If something needs deciding that this brief does not settle, write ESCALATION.md at the worktree root -- the question, the alternatives, their costs -- COMMIT it on this branch, take the most conservative continuation, and keep going.\"\n\nHmm. But before escalating, let me check: maybe jq actually CAN run subprocesses in recent versions? Let me verify with the actual jq binary on this machine.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05edc4534001glm8sYTBy9rM0c","sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","messageID":"msg_05edc2065001ja7sWQXt1ZHaK8"}}
