harness.md\nmerging-passes-turns-redundant-traversals-into-bugs.md\nnamed-functions-kept-an-open-question-open.md\none-invariant-three-independent-construction-sites.md\nremoving-the-effect-layer-makes-map-primitive.md\nreorder-found-a-route-around-the-layout-it-did-not-reconcile.md\nsoa-is-cheap-until-something-wants-a-whole-element.md\nstreaming-input-is-pull-verified-against-jq-itself.md\nthe-backend-language-has-rules-the-checker-does-not.md\nthe-lowering-needs-types-the-checker-already-computed.md\nthe-widths-a-backend-cannot-fake-become-the-types-contract.md\ntrack-an-incomplete-backend-as-a-shrinking-list.md\nunescapable-control-bytes-are-the-crack-in-the-reserialization-gate.md\nvec-of-enum-falls-into-the-boxed-default-nobody-chose.md\nwinnow-replaced-the-tokenizer-not-the-grammar.md\n=====\nresearch-log/a-backend-that-finds-nothing-is-evidence-only-if-it-is-different.md\nresearch-log/a-fourth-backend-found-two-rules-three-could-not.md\nresearch-log/a-pure-value-layer-dissolves-jqs-iteration-operators.md\nresearch-log/a-recursive-type-is-where-inline-codegen-needs-a-name.md\nresearch-log/a-sixth-backend-rule-the-checker-did-not-know.md\nresearch-log/a-statically-typed-target-asks-for-the-types-the-checker-already-has.md\nresearch-log/a-test-that-cannot-fail-is-worse-than-no-test.md\nresearch-log/backends-can-agree-and-still-be-wrong.md\nresearch-log/borrowing-the-hosts-null-borrowed-its-conflations.md\nresearch-log/codepoint-order-is-not-utf-16-order.md\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"ls research-log; echo \"=====\"; grep -rln \"jq\" research-log | head","time":{"start":1788297905665,"end":1788297905666}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Confirmed: jq has no subprocess capability. `system` is not defined.\n\nSo a corpus case for pipe_through with `output` on all 7 backends is impossible unless jq implements pipe_through somehow.\n\nWait - actually, maybe the design is that pipe_through is a *primitive* that each backend lowers to whatever IT has. For jq, what could pipe_through lower to? Nothing that spawns a process. But maybe jq could... hmm.\n\n\n\nOK, let me step back and reconsider whether the corpus really must run pipe_through on jq. Let me re-read the AGENTS.md test section:\n\n\"Every case runs on every backend. `output` means they all print it and agree; `refuses: true` means they all decline to run it, and what each says while refusing is its own business.\"\n\n\n\nAnd the corpus.rs harness: for a case with output, every backend must produce output or it's \"BROKEN\". \n\n\n\nSo the ONLY way to have a pipe_through corpus case pass is if all 7 backends (incl. jq) can run it. jq can't spawn grep. \n\n\n\nUnless... we make pipe_through for jq work WITHOUT spawning a subprocess. What would that even mean? The semantics: `lines | pipe_through(\"grep\", [\"foo\"]) | collect` = read stdin lines, write them to grep's stdin, read grep's stdout lines, collect them. For jq, there's no grep. The output depends on the actual `grep foo` behavior.\n\n\n\nCould jq reimplement grep? No - arbitrary external commands can't be reimplemented.\n\n\n\nSo the fundamental issue: pipe_through to an arbitrary external command cannot work on jq. UNLESS the jq backend is allowed to refuse. \n\n\n\nHmm, wait. Let me re-read the harness failure reporting. For an `Output` case, if `run_on` returns Err for jq, it reports \"BROKEN\". So jq must run it. If it can't, the case fails.\n\n no per-backend exception.\n\n.\n\n\n\nSo what's the right move per the brief? The brief is a continuation brief from a coordinator. It says: \"Build the streaming pipe-to-subprocess primitive per the shell-out-design ruling... Add the corpus case FIRST... then implement parser/checker/backends until just check is green.\"\n\n\n\nMaybe the coordinator assumed this was feasible. But it's not - for jq. So this is exactly a \"needs deciding\" situation per the brief: \"If something needs deciding that this brief does not settle, write ESCALATION.md at the worktree root -- the question, the alternatives, their costs -- COMMIT it on this branch, take the most conservative continuation, and keep going; never stop to wait for a human.\"\n\n\n\nBut wait - before escalating, let me look at how the project has historically handled a backend that can't do something that others can. Are there corpus cases where ONE backend is incapable but the case still has output? Let me search for how jq's limitations are handled. For example, jq can't do... hmm. What about `input`? jq CAN read input. What about streaming - jq handles it. What about Char? jq has explode. \n\n\n\nLet me look at whether there's any precedent for a backend-specific \"refuse at emit\" that doesn't break the corpus... For instance, emit_jq returns Err in some cases (cycle errors). And `Backend::emit` for Jq returns Result<String, String>. So jq can refuse to emit. But then the corpus harness's `run_on` would Err → \"BROKEN\".\n\n\n\nHmm wait, unless... let me look at how the docs harness or fmt handles backends... \n\n\n\nActually, let me reconsider. Maybe the resolution is simpler than I think. What if pipe_through does NOT go through jq as a subprocess spawn, but rather the *corpus program* that exercises pipe_through is designed so that jq can compute the same answer WITHOUT spawning? No - the corpus program IS `lines | pipe_through(\"grep\", [\"foo\"]) | collect`; jq has to run grep.\n\n\n\nOK here's yet another angle. What if jq's implementation of pipe_through spawns grep via... jq has `import`? jq can import other jq modules from files. No exec.\n\n\n\nWhat about jq's `input`/`inputs` reading from stdin... the toylang program reads stdin lines then pipes them through grep. For jq, could we make the *harness* (run_on for jq) do the subprocess spawn? I.e., emit_jq produces a placeholder/filter, and run_jq detects pipe_through and... no, that's absurd and not how the backends work. The emitted code is supposed to be self-contained jq.\n\n\n\nAlright. Let me look at the issue comments once more. The issue says: \"This explicitly inherits the open Q35 dependency... Q35 should land, or at least have a concrete enough shape, before this is implementable.\"\n\n\n\nSo the issue ITSELF says it's not implementable until Q35 lands. And Q35 is still OPEN (I read it - it says \"Everything in the bullet list above remains open\"). So per the issue's own text, this issue is NOT ready to implement.\n\n \n\nBut the task brief (the continuation brief from a previous coordinator run) says to go straight to implementing. Hmm. There's a conflict between the brief and the issue's stated dependency.\n\n\n\n\n\nWait, maybe Q35 has a \"concrete enough shape\" already. Let me re-read Q35:\n\n\"What exists is one answer by default. A program is an expression, its value is rendered by the type-driven printer...\"\n\nAnd: \"The fused `jsonlines(f(inputs))` loop has since made write-as-it-goes real at the backend level, and [the streams decision](../draft.md#decided-stream-is-the-effect-layer-typed) gave the effect layer a type -- so the blockage above is gone, and the question is sharpened rather than answered. What was decided there about output is deliberately minimal: `jsonlines` is a sink, legal only as the program's outermost expression, with no result type.\"\n\n\n\nSo Q35 is still open. The issue #158 inherits it. Per the issue, this shouldn't be implementable yet.\n\n\n\nHmm, but the brief is explicit: \"go straight to implementing\" and \"a previous run verified baseline green (311 tests) and then expired while still reading code -- do NOT re-explore\". \n\n\n\nMaybe the coordinator decided the design shape is \"concrete enough\": the shape `lines | pipe_through(\"grep\", [\"args\"]) | collect` is ratified. The Q35 questions (stdout/stderr semantics) are about details like: does output have a type? Is stderr in the language? Those don't block building the primitive for the corpus case. The corpus case just needs the output to be the collected lines from grep's stdout. So we can implement pipe_through as: lines → write to subprocess stdin → read subprocess stdout lines → stream → collect.\n\n \n\nThe Q35 stuff about stderr could be: stderr passes through, not captured. And whether a program writes or returns: for pipe_through, the intermediate result is a stream (lazy-ish), and collect is the exit.\n\n\n\nOK so let's assume we implement it. Now the jq problem remains.\n\n\n\nLet me look at this from the maintainer's design perspective. The shell-out-design ruling picks \"full streaming pipe-to-subprocess\". The example: `lines | pipe_through(\"grep\", [\"foo\"]) | collect`. \n\n\n\nHow would jq handle this? It CANNOT. So perhaps the design intent is that jq (and maybe others) are exempt because... hmm.\n\n\n\nWait, let me look at whether jq has been dropped from some backends' set for certain features. Is there a concept of a backend \"refusing a program\" per-case? Let me look at how `emit_jq` returns Err - for cycle errors. And how that surfaces in tests. For instance tests/step_*.rs pin error messages for programs that don't compile. But a corpus case must compile (compile happens before emit for jq - actually emit_jq returns Result, and compile is separate). \n\n\n\nHmm, but here's a thought: what if the corpus case for pipe_through compiles fine, but jq's `run_on` errors because emit_jq refuses (cycle-style), and then we'd get \"BROKEN\" in the corpus. So that's not acceptable for a `output` case.\n\n\n\nOK let me look at how OTHER similar language-design issues handled a backend limitation. Let me search research-log for \"jq\" limitations and \"refus\".","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05edc85e600172v5GCTJTq903z","sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","messageID":"msg_05edc4569001bZkhF1qk85hZUr"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788297905670,"sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_7fc5da0ed0884ad2b455b9c4","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"grep -rn \"refus\\|cannot\\|unable\" research-log/*.md | grep -i jq | head -20"},"output":"research-log/a-pure-value-layer-dissolves-jqs-iteration-operators.md:27:**`|` cannot be elementwise.** The step 4 plan said the load-bearing assumption was that `|`\nresearch-log/a-pure-value-layer-dissolves-jqs-iteration-operators.md:52:[a test that cannot fail is worse than no test](a-test-that-cannot-fail-is-worse-than-no-test.md).\nresearch-log/a-statically-typed-target-asks-for-the-types-the-checker-already-has.md:17:**Distribution cannot be depth-polymorphic.** Lua, JavaScript and jq each carry one\nresearch-log/index.md:38:  -- jq derives map from reflect-apply-reify, so a language without the layer cannot, and the\nresearch-log/jqs-item-wise-access-is-the-effect-layer-wearing-brackets.md:6:description: Running the same edge cases through jq and NumPy shows that jq's brackets do not make access item-wise, the stream does, so a value-layer language cannot borrow the operator without the layer.\nresearch-log/jqs-item-wise-access-is-the-effect-layer-wearing-brackets.md:25:| field, no `[]` | `.users.name` | **error: cannot index array with string** | -- | -- |\nresearch-log/jqs-item-wise-access-is-the-effect-layer-wearing-brackets.md:53:jq's `[]` cannot be borrowed without borrowing the effect layer. Take the layer away, as\nresearch-log/removing-the-effect-layer-makes-map-primitive.md:6:description: jq defines map as sugar for reflect-apply-reify, so a language without an effect layer cannot derive it, and the operator that was free in the inspiration becomes a builtin here.\nresearch-log/the-widths-a-backend-cannot-fake-become-the-types-contract.md:31:  The alternative -- refusing Int64 programs on jq -- would have made every Int64 corpus case\n","metadata":{"output":"research-log/a-pure-value-layer-dissolves-jqs-iteration-operators.md:27:**`|` cannot be elementwise.** The step 4 plan said the load-bearing assumption was that `|`\nresearch-log/a-pure-value-layer-dissolves-jqs-iteration-operators.md:52:[a test that cannot fail is worse than no test](a-test-that-cannot-fail-is-worse-than-no-test.md).\nresearch-log/a-statically-typed-target-asks-for-the-types-the-checker-already-has.md:17:**Distribution cannot be depth-polymorphic.** Lua, JavaScript and jq each carry one\nresearch-log/index.md:38:  -- jq derives map from reflect-apply-reify, so a language without the layer cannot, and the\nresearch-log/jqs-item-wise-access-is-the-effect-layer-wearing-brackets.md:6:description: Running the same edge cases through jq and NumPy shows that jq's brackets do not make access item-wise, the stream does, so a value-layer language cannot borrow the operator without the layer.\nresearch-log/jqs-item-wise-access-is-the-effect-layer-wearing-brackets.md:25:| field, no `[]` | `.users.name` | **error: cannot index array with string** | -- | -- |\nresearch-log/jqs-item-wise-access-is-the-effect-layer-wearing-brackets.md:53:jq's `[]` cannot be borrowed without borrowing the effect layer. Take the layer away, as\nresearch-log/removing-the-effect-layer-makes-map-primitive.md:6:description: jq defines map as sugar for reflect-apply-reify, so a language without an effect layer cannot derive it, and the operator that was free in the inspiration becomes a builtin here.\nresearch-log/the-widths-a-backend-cannot-fake-become-the-types-contract.md:31:  The alternative -- refusing Int64 programs on jq -- would have made every Int64 corpus case\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"grep -rn \"refus\\|cannot\\|unable\" research-log/*.md | grep -i jq | head -20","time":{"start":1788297905667,"end":1788297905668}},"id":"prt_05edc85f4001XtJiGa9eP8Y4UN","sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","messageID":"msg_05edc4569001bZkhF1qk85hZUr"}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1788297905676,"sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","part":{"id":"prt_05edc860a001l0GbXzdvY3odAB","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"5a1012ab7eb7388a8de2a55a732be7fdbf823735","messageID":"msg_05edc4569001bZkhF1qk85hZUr","sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":75502,"input":1065,"output":6,"reasoning":1983,"cache":{"write":0,"read":72448}},"cost":0.001586413}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788297906615,"sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","part":{"id":"prt_05edc89b4001xaAbWCcvF28mpy","messageID":"msg_05edc8613001RVlsKSMyg0r5Rx","sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","snapshot":"5a1012ab7eb7388a8de2a55a732be7fdbf823735","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1788297907291,"sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","part":{"id":"prt_05edc8b17001oPXZ9y3Y9iruq8","messageID":"msg_05edc8613001RVlsKSMyg0r5Rx","sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","type":"text","text":"\n\n","time":{"start":1788297906967,"end":1788297907289}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788297907294,"sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_9ceaa8d7bc9a4dc1bcd985a9","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/research-log/the-widths-a-backend-cannot-fake-become-the-types-contract.md"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/research-log/the-widths-a-backend-cannot-fake-become-the-types-contract.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: ---\n2: type: Lesson\n3: calendar:\n4:   - 2026-08-30\n5: title: The widths a backend cannot fake become the type's contract\n6: description: Int64 landed with three different backend stories -- native width, a second representation, and an honest precision boundary -- and the boundary had to move into the documented contract because no emulation trick could reassemble 64-bit wrapping from doubles.\n7: tags:\n8:   - backends\n9:   - int64\n10:   - arithmetic\n11:   - jq\n12: timestamp: 2026-08-30T00:00:00Z\n13: ---\n14: \n15: 32-bit wrapping was emulable on every backend, which let ADR 0006 make one promise everywhere:\n16: jq's worst case, multiplication, split into 16-bit halves whose partial products each fit a\n17: double, and the harness could pin `-2147483648 * -1` across all seven targets. Int64\n18: (kantord/toylang#83) is the width where that stops being available. There is no split that\n19: reassembles a 64-bit product -- or a mod-2^64 wrap -- from pieces a double can hold.\n20: \n21: So the backends divide into three honest stories rather than one emulation:\n22: \n23: - **Native width.** Lua, Go, Rust, LLVM, and Python (whose unbounded integers make the wrap one\n24:   modulo). Lua is the surprise: its integers are 64-bit already, so Int64's `+`, `-`, `*` are\n25:   bare operators while Int's need `tl_i32` fixups -- the wider type is the cheaper one there.\n26: - **A second representation.** JavaScript carries Int64 as BigInt behind `BigInt.asIntN(64, ...)`,\n27:   which is exactly the cost draft.md's carrying-width correction predicted: 53 bits was the\n28:   ceiling for staying on doubles, and going past it means two numeric representations in one\n29:   emitted program.\n30: - **A documented boundary.** jq computes in doubles, exact within +/-2^53 and wrong past it.\n31:   The alternative -- refusing Int64 programs on jq -- would have made every Int64 corpus case\n32:   unwritable, since a case must either run on all seven backends or be refused by all seven.\n33: \n34: The consequence for testing is structural, not incidental: the corpus can only pin behaviour\n35: all seven backends share, so the 2^63 wrapping edges (`MAX + 1`, `MIN / -1`) moved to\n36: tests/int64.rs, pinned across six backends with jq's divergent answer asserted beside them\n37: rather than skipped. The boundary is part of the type's reference page for the same reason.\n38: A promise the implementation cannot keep everywhere has to surface in the contract, or the\n39: agreement harness quietly stops meaning what it says.\n40: \n41: One knock-on defect worth remembering: the Rust backend generated a JSON parser for every\n42: record type the program mentions, not just the ones reachable from `input`. Harmless while\n43: every type was parseable; the first deliberately unparseable type (Int64, whose wire codec is\n44: undecided) made those spurious parsers panic. The fix scopes parser generation to the types\n45: stdin can actually deliver -- the general shape being that \"generate for everything, it's\n46: harmless\" holds only until some type is unsupported on purpose.\n\n(End of file - total 46 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"---\ntype: Lesson\ncalendar:\n  - 2026-08-30\ntitle: The widths a backend cannot fake become the type's contract\ndescription: Int64 landed with three different backend stories -- native width, a second representation, and an honest precision boundary -- and the boundary had to move into the documented contract because no emulation trick could reassemble 64-bit wrapping from doubles.\ntags:\n  - backends\n  - int64\n  - arithmetic\n  - jq\ntimestamp: 2026-08-30T00:00:00Z\n---\n\n32-bit wrapping was emulable on every backend, which let ADR 0006 make one promise everywhere:\njq's worst case, multiplication, split into 16-bit halves whose partial products each fit a\ndouble, and the harness could pin `-2147483648 * -1` across all seven targets. Int64\n(kantord/toylang#83) is the width where that stops being available. There is no split that\nreassembles a 64-bit product -- or a mod-2^64 wrap -- from pieces a double can hold.\n","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/research-log/the-widths-a-backend-cannot-fake-become-the-types-contract.md","text":"---\ntype: Lesson\ncalendar:\n  - 2026-08-30\ntitle: The widths a backend cannot fake become the type's contract\ndescription: Int64 landed with three different backend stories -- native width, a second representation, and an honest precision boundary -- and the boundary had to move into the documented contract because no emulation trick could reassemble 64-bit wrapping from doubles.\ntags:\n  - backends\n  - int64\n  - arithmetic\n  - jq\ntimestamp: 2026-08-30T00:00:00Z\n---\n\n32-bit wrapping was emulable on every backend, which let ADR 0006 make one promise everywhere:\njq's worst case, multiplication, split into 16-bit halves whose partial products each fit a\ndouble, and the harness could pin `-2147483648 * -1` across all seven targets. Int64\n(kantord/toylang#83) is the width where that stops being available. There is no split that\nreassembles a 64-bit product -- or a mod-2^64 wrap -- from pieces a double can hold.\n\nSo the backends divide into three honest stories rather than one emulation:\n\n- **Native width.** Lua, Go, Rust, LLVM, and Python (whose unbounded integers make the wrap one\n  modulo). Lua is the surprise: its integers are 64-bit already, so Int64's `+`, `-`, `*` are\n  bare operators while Int's need `tl_i32` fixups -- the wider type is the cheaper one there.\n- **A second representation.** JavaScript carries Int64 as BigInt behind `BigInt.asIntN(64, ...)`,\n  which is exactly the cost draft.md's carrying-width correction predicted: 53 bits was the\n  ceiling for staying on doubles, and going past it means two numeric representations in one\n  emitted program.\n- **A documented boundary.** jq computes in doubles, exact within +/-2^53 and wrong past it.\n  The alternative -- refusing Int64 programs on jq -- would have made every Int64 corpus case\n  unwritable, since a case must either run on all seven backends or be refused by all seven.\n\nThe consequence for testing is structural, not incidental: the corpus can only pin behaviour\nall seven backends share, so the 2^63 wrapping edges (`MAX + 1`, `MIN / -1`) moved to\ntests/int64.rs, pinned across six backends with jq's divergent answer asserted beside them\nrather than skipped. The boundary is part of the type's reference page for the same reason.\nA promise the implementation cannot keep everywhere has to surface in the contract, or the\nagreement harness quietly stops meaning what it says.\n\nOne knock-on defect worth remembering: the Rust backend generated a JSON parser for every\nrecord type the program mentions, not just the ones reachable from `input`. Harmless while\nevery type was parseable; the first deliberately unparseable type (Int64, whose wire codec is\nundecided) made those spurious parsers panic. The fix scopes parser generation to the types\nstdin can actually deliver -- the general shape being that \"generate for everything, it's\nharmless\" holds only until some type is unsupported on purpose.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":46,"totalLines":46,"truncated":false}},"title":"research-log/the-widths-a-backend-cannot-fake-become-the-types-contract.md","time":{"start":1788297907285,"end":1788297907293}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Let me read \"track-an-incomplete-backend-as-a-shrinking-list.md\" and \"the-widths-a-backend-cannot-fake-become-the-types-contract.md\" - the second one is about how a backend that can't do something leads to either refusing the program on that backend or implementing it. Let me read it.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05edc8c4f001Ift1eYm1Ijy2eK","sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","messageID":"msg_05edc8613001RVlsKSMyg0r5Rx"}}
