{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788297926295,"sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_8df41bbd090342568bef672c","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"grep -rn \"refus\" tests/backend_jq.rs | head; echo \"=====\"; wc -l tests/backend_jq.rs tests/int64.rs"},"output":"72:/// function's callees are already in scope, so it refuses rather than emitting jq source that\n76:fn a_genuine_cycle_between_named_functions_is_refused_cleanly() {\n97:/// The printer-side counterpart to `a_genuine_cycle_between_named_functions_is_refused_cleanly`:\n100:/// name instead of toylang's clean refusal.\n102:fn a_genuine_cycle_between_printers_is_refused_cleanly() {\n=====\n 121 tests/backend_jq.rs\n 145 tests/int64.rs\n 266 total\n","metadata":{"output":"72:/// function's callees are already in scope, so it refuses rather than emitting jq source that\n76:fn a_genuine_cycle_between_named_functions_is_refused_cleanly() {\n97:/// The printer-side counterpart to `a_genuine_cycle_between_named_functions_is_refused_cleanly`:\n100:/// name instead of toylang's clean refusal.\n102:fn a_genuine_cycle_between_printers_is_refused_cleanly() {\n=====\n 121 tests/backend_jq.rs\n 145 tests/int64.rs\n 266 total\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"grep -rn \"refus\" tests/backend_jq.rs | head; echo \"=====\"; wc -l tests/backend_jq.rs tests/int64.rs","time":{"start":1788297926289,"end":1788297926291}},"id":"prt_05edcd68b001AX16CUWVR1Q4J4","sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","messageID":"msg_05edc8c6f001EtzXUvx008B4ue"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788297926295,"sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_c314dc66ec694dad8fec7668","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"grep -rln \"jq\" tests/corpus | head -20; echo \"=====\"; grep -rln \"snapshot:\" tests/corpus | wc -l"},"output":"tests/corpus/enum_match.yaml\ntests/corpus/enum_recursive_walk.yaml\ntests/corpus/field_key_case_is_significant.yaml\ntests/corpus/jq_tutorial.yaml\ntests/corpus/jq_tutorial_step4.yaml\ntests/corpus/lines_preserve_crlf.yaml\ntests/corpus/lines_unterminated_final_line.yaml\ntests/corpus/max_int64s.yaml\ntests/corpus/sum_int64s.yaml\ntests/corpus/tail_recursion_deep.yaml\n=====\n6\n","metadata":{"output":"tests/corpus/enum_match.yaml\ntests/corpus/enum_recursive_walk.yaml\ntests/corpus/field_key_case_is_significant.yaml\ntests/corpus/jq_tutorial.yaml\ntests/corpus/jq_tutorial_step4.yaml\ntests/corpus/lines_preserve_crlf.yaml\ntests/corpus/lines_unterminated_final_line.yaml\ntests/corpus/max_int64s.yaml\ntests/corpus/sum_int64s.yaml\ntests/corpus/tail_recursion_deep.yaml\n=====\n6\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"grep -rln \"jq\" tests/corpus | head -20; echo \"=====\"; grep -rln \"snapshot:\" tests/corpus | wc -l","time":{"start":1788297926291,"end":1788297926294}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"This confirms it. \"A case must either run on all seven backends or be refused by all seven.\" And Int64's jq divergence was handled by: the corpus cases only pin shared behavior; the jq-divergent edges moved to a non-corpus test (tests/int64.rs), pinned across six backends with jq's divergent answer asserted beside them.\n\n\n\nSo for pipe_through, if jq cannot run it, then either:\n- (a) all seven backends refuse pipe_through programs (`refuses: true` corpus case), which means we don't actually build anything... OR\n- (b) the corpus case stays out of the corpus (like int64's edge cases), and pipe_through programs are tested per-backend in a non-corpus test. But then the corpus still needs... hmm, wait. If ALL pipe_through corpus cases are impossible, there'd be no corpus case for it at all, and the corpus case brief says to add one.\n\n \n\n\n\nWait, hold on. Actually let me re-read: \"A case must either run on all seven backends or be refused by all seven.\" So for pipe_through, either:\n(a) it runs on all 7, or\n(b) it's refused by all 7.\n\n\n\nFor (a), jq can't spawn subprocesses. So it can't run on all 7.\n\n\n\nHmm, unless... wait, let me reconsider whether jq CAN really not. Let me think once more. jq 1.8... there's no exec. Right.\n\n\n\nSo (b): all seven refuse. That means `pipe_through` programs refuse to run on ALL backends. That's not a buildable feature - it would mean the checker or backends refuse any program using pipe_through.\n\n\n\nThat can't be the intent of an issue titled \"Design and build a streaming pipe-to-subprocess primitive\". \n\n\n\nSo there's a real conflict here. The issue is blocked on Q35 (open), and the corpus harness's all-or-nothing rule means a pipe_through case can't be output unless jq can exec (it can't) or unless we change the harness (can't - deny_unknown_fields / all 7).\n\n\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider option (a) once more. What if jq's pipe_through implementation is... at the harness level? I.e., the jq backend's \"pipe_through\" emits jq code that doesn't spawn grep, but the toylang compile/lower step pre-processes: since the toylang program's source knows `pipe_through(\"grep\", [\"foo\"])` is the middle of a pipeline from `lines` to `collect`, maybe the emitted jq program is... no, the emitted program must compute the actual grep output, which only grep can compute.\n\n\n\nThere's genuinely no way around it: jq cannot compute grep's output.\n\n\n\nUnless the harness itself runs grep for jq... which would be per-backend behavior divergence in the harness. Actually wait - what does \"the same program\" mean? The corpus program runs grep. On jq, grep runs as a subprocess OF jq... impossible. On jq, the harness (run_jq) could... hmm. The harness runs jq as a subprocess, pipes stdin/out. It could theoretically pre-process the stream through grep... but then jq's emitted code wouldn't be doing it, and jq wouldn't be \"the backend\" that ran the program. Also the emitted code snapshot would be wrong.\n\n\n\nThis is genuinely a design blocker. But wait - maybe I'm wrong that jq can't. Let me actually verify by checking jq docs or trying a known trick. Some jq versions support `$ENV` and... there's `import`, `include`... Those load jq modules. No subprocess.\n\n\n\nActually hold on - jq DOES have one external-command-ish feature: `jq -e`... no. What about the `INVOCATION`... no.\n\nLet me check if there's an `@sh` or system in jq 1.8. I already tested `system` - not defined. Let me also check `debug`, `stderr`, `input_filename`... `debug` writes to stderr. No subprocess.\n\n\n\nOK. So jq can't. Definitively.\n\n\n\nSo what's the most conservative continuation per the brief?\n\nThe brief: \"If something needs deciding that this brief does not settle, write ESCALATION.md at the worktree root -- the question, the alternatives, their costs -- COMMIT it on this branch, take the most conservative continuation, and keep going; never stop to wait for a human.\"\n\n\n\nSo I should write ESCALATION.md with the question: jq cannot spawn subprocesses; the corpus demands all-or-nothing; how should pipe_through be tested? Alternatives:\n\nA. Make the corpus case `refuses: true` (all seven refuse,) and don't implement the actual behavior at all - effectively shelving the feature. Cost: nothing built.\n\n\n\nB. Implement pipe_through on the six backends that can spawn subprocesses, and add a corpus case... but that fails the harness (jq BROKEN).. Cost: harness red.\n\n\n\nC. Change the agreement harness to allow per-backend refusals ( a new YAML key like `refuses: [jq]` or backend-specific expectations). This is a harness feature that touches the all-or-nothing rule; needs maintainer decision. Cost: harness churn.\n\n\n\nD. Put the pipe_through cases in a non-corpus test (like tests/int64.rs does for jq-divergent edges: pinned across six backends with jq's refusal asserted beside them), and keep the corpus without a pipe_through case... but then the \"corpus case FIRST\" brief instruction can't be honored. Cost: no corpus case; testing via a dedicated test file.\n\n\n\nHmm. But actually wait. Let me reconsider whether maybe the intended answer is more subtle. Let me look at how `lines` itself works on jq: `Kind::Lines => \"[ inputs ]\"` with `-R`. So `lines | pipe_through(...) | collect` on jq would be `[inputs] | ...`. \n\n\n\nWhat if jq's implementation of pipe_through is to emit the subprocess invocation via... the *toylang compiler* pre-lowering? Like: pipe_through is implemented as a *macro-ish* rewrite at the tir level: the compiler lowers `lines | pipe_through(cmd, args) | collect` into code that writes the whole input to a temp file, runs cmd on it, reads the output? But that would still need to spawn cmd in jq's emitted program - no.\n\n\n\n\n\nOK here's yet another thought. What if pipe_through is *not* meant to run grep per se on jq, but rather jq's version uses a different mechanism: since jq is a *filter*, maybe pipe_through is only ever used with the stream ending in `collect` (per the ratified shape), and... no, doesn't help; still need grep's output.\n\n\n\nOK. I'm now confident: this is a genuine blocker needing escalation. But before escalating, let me double check the brief's \"Definition of done\" and whether maybe the coordinator anticipated this: \"Build the streaming pipe-to-subprocess primitive per the shell-out-design ruling.\"\n\n\n\nAnd also: \"Commit the moment any coherent piece works\". \n\n\n\nGiven the escalation protocol, I should:\n1. Write ESCALATION.md with the question, alternatives, costs.\n2. Commit it.\n3. \"take the most conservative continuation, and keep going\".\n\n\n\nWhat's \"the most conservative continuation\"? It means: do something that makes progress without making a decision the brief didn't authorize. Options B (harness red) is not acceptable. Option A (refuses: true, build nothing) makes no progress. Option D (non-corpus per-backend test, implement on 6 backends) is \"conservative\" in that it doesn't touch the all-or-nothing harness, and it does build the feature. Option C (harness change) needs a maintainer.\n\n \n\nHmm, but wait. Let me reconsider. Maybe I'm overcomplicating this. Let me look at whether the project actually *does* have per-backend handling in the corpus somehow... Let me grep for \"jq\" in tests/corpus and see if any corpus case mentions jq specially.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05edcd686001gE4sTdMPgAL28x","sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","messageID":"msg_05edc8c6f001EtzXUvx008B4ue"}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1788297926304,"sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","part":{"id":"prt_05edcd69d00126ZXVl3PAhNCdZ","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"5a1012ab7eb7388a8de2a55a732be7fdbf823735","messageID":"msg_05edc8c6f001EtzXUvx008B4ue","sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":79634,"input":1150,"output":100,"reasoning":1840,"cache":{"write":0,"read":76544}},"cost":0.001648654}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788297930216,"sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","part":{"id":"prt_05edce5e2001o2t8u353oE4Ili","messageID":"msg_05edcd6a8001gwcLk67bNVbKr1","sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","snapshot":"5a1012ab7eb7388a8de2a55a732be7fdbf823735","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788297978527,"sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-80fdd0928a8a4a5e","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"grep -n \"pipe_through\\|pipe-through\\|pipe through\\|shell\\|external\\|subprocess\\|spawn\\|jq\" draft.md | head -50"},"output":"28:jq's defining feature is that every expression produces a *stream* of 0..n values. The insight\n149:lens.** This works, and it is honest: these are two different operations that jq conflates only\n150:because jq has neither lenses nor cardinality tracking. But it puts the *default* on the wrong\n278:That connects to a decision already implied elsewhere. jq values are immutable and acyclic, so\n317:A consequence worth noting: jq needs `;` to separate filter arguments because `,` is already\n418:jq conflates \"missing\" with \"type error\": `null.a.b.c` yields `null` but `1 | .a` raises. Those\n783:jq's `reduce .[] as $x (0; . + $x)` is a left fold with an explicit accumulator. It is\n1173:In jq, `=` is not assignment. Its right-hand side is an ordinary expression, so if it yields\n1184:Compounding it, jq's `=` and `|=` disagree about cardinality and say nothing about it:\n1193:**A. Keep `=`.** Familiar to anyone arriving from jq, with zero migration cost. But it\n1203:(\"red\",\"blue\") as $c | db.color = $c    # explicit; jq already supports this and it reads better\n1223:jq's multi-output semantics produce hazards that are all the same shape, namely the right\n1224:feature in the wrong position. Measured against a jq implementation:\n1256:name with no `[]` anywhere, and `[]` had nothing left to do. Running the same cases through jq\n1258:[jq's item-wise access is the effect layer wearing brackets](research-log/jqs-item-wise-access-is-the-effect-layer-wearing-brackets.md). `[.[][1]]` applies its\n1260:separates them is that a stream came first. jq's `[]` is not the item-wise operator; it is the\n1290:ragged data is perfectly meaningful, and jq does it: `[.[][1]]` on `[[1,2],[3]]` yields\n1305:known only to the program. Collapsing them is what forces jq to treat an object as a struct and a\n1308:**Five deliberate divergences from jq**, all measured rather than assumed:\n1310:| | jq | here |\n1320:**One bracket per dimension**, left to right: `v[3][]`, not `v[3, ]`. This is already jq's\n1327:Built, and the ragged case comes out identical to jq: `[[1,2],[3]][][1]` is `[2,null]` on all\n1332:error. jq is incoherent on this, allowing `\"abc\"[0:2]` while rejecting `\"abc\"[0]`; a string is\n1368:which keeps every value inside the Smi range where V8 is fast. On jq it is emulated exactly and\n1378:64 bits is worse again: addition needs an overflow intrinsic rather than a compare, jq cannot\n1383:Doubles do not wrap, so on node and jq a wrap costs a fixup that a check does not. But trapping\n1388:Verified rather than assumed: wrapping 32-bit multiplication has no direct spelling in jq, since\n1404:integer exactly, jq included. Five of the six do, and jq is indeed one of them, since it\n1416:a real 64-bit integer that wraps -- JavaScript pays the BigInt representation, and jq, which\n1480:`lua`, `js`, `llvm`, `jq`, `go` and `py`.\n1498:32-bit rule is one modulo, and truncated division is a sign fixup over `//`. jq needed a split\n1523:jq's `if ... then ... else ... end` was the alternative and was rejected. The objections to\n1531:The deciding point is that this language has no statements. jq's form is an expression too, but\n1563:`range(n)` is zero-based, matching jq, Python, and this language's own indices, which is why the\n1568:\"b\"` does not typecheck, where jq would run both branches and hand back two answers. Both arms\n1602:What it cost is that three of jq's defining operators came out trivial. `.[]` is the identity on\n1609:language in which `.[]` does nothing is still recognisably in the jq family. Written up in\n1610:[a pure value layer dissolves jq's iteration operators](research-log/a-pure-value-layer-dissolves-jqs-iteration-operators.md).\n1738:`{name}` for `{name: .name}` is jq's most-used shorthand and is not being adopted, for a reason\n1761:What decided it is the call site rather than the cost. `join(\", \")` in jq says nothing about\n1811:flowing mode, is push and is Node's own documented backpressure footgun), and jq itself --\n1823:not pre-reading stdin into Rust before a subprocess backend runs, which this cut does -- an\n1840:any backend starts) is untouched, and a program may not use both: forced by jq specifically,\n1843:in one run -- verified against real jq before the rule was written.\n1864:alone as ordinary content, matching `jq -R` and Python's own raw stdin iteration, neither of\n1877:Two thirds of the six backends are separate OS processes Rust spawns (native, jq, Go, Python,\n1882:For the five subprocesses, genuine streaming meant stopping Rust from reading all of stdin into a\n1931:jq's own `inputs` -- every remaining JSON value on stdin, one per line -- was the obvious next\n1957:principle, and jq needs a different invocation flag for raw-line mode (`-R -n`) than for\n1979:## DECIDED: `jsonlines`, and the jq tutorial reproduces in full\n","metadata":{"output":"28:jq's defining feature is that every expression produces a *stream* of 0..n values. The insight\n149:lens.** This works, and it is honest: these are two different operations that jq conflates only\n150:because jq has neither lenses nor cardinality tracking. But it puts the *default* on the wrong\n278:That connects to a decision already implied elsewhere. jq values are immutable and acyclic, so\n317:A consequence worth noting: jq needs `;` to separate filter arguments because `,` is already\n418:jq conflates \"missing\" with \"type error\": `null.a.b.c` yields `null` but `1 | .a` raises. Those\n783:jq's `reduce .[] as $x (0; . + $x)` is a left fold with an explicit accumulator. It is\n1173:In jq, `=` is not assignment. Its right-hand side is an ordinary expression, so if it yields\n1184:Compounding it, jq's `=` and `|=` disagree about cardinality and say nothing about it:\n1193:**A. Keep `=`.** Familiar to anyone arriving from jq, with zero migration cost. But it\n1203:(\"red\",\"blue\") as $c | db.color = $c    # explicit; jq already supports this and it reads better\n1223:jq's multi-output semantics produce hazards that are all the same shape, namely the right\n1224:feature in the wrong position. Measured against a jq implementation:\n1256:name with no `[]` anywhere, and `[]` had nothing left to do. Running the same cases through jq\n1258:[jq's item-wise access is the effect layer wearing brackets](research-log/jqs-item-wise-access-is-the-effect-layer-wearing-brackets.md). `[.[][1]]` applies its\n1260:separates them is that a stream came first. jq's `[]` is not the item-wise operator; it is the\n1290:ragged data is perfectly meaningful, and jq does it: `[.[][1]]` on `[[1,2],[3]]` yields\n1305:known only to the program. Collapsing them is what forces jq to treat an object as a struct and a\n1308:**Five deliberate divergences from jq**, all measured rather than assumed:\n1310:| | jq | here |\n1320:**One bracket per dimension**, left to right: `v[3][]`, not `v[3, ]`. This is already jq's\n1327:Built, and the ragged case comes out identical to jq: `[[1,2],[3]][][1]` is `[2,null]` on all\n1332:error. jq is incoherent on this, allowing `\"abc\"[0:2]` while rejecting `\"abc\"[0]`; a string is\n1368:which keeps every value inside the Smi range where V8 is fast. On jq it is emulated exactly and\n1378:64 bits is worse again: addition needs an overflow intrinsic rather than a compare, jq cannot\n1383:Doubles do not wrap, so on node and jq a wrap costs a fixup that a check does not. But trapping\n1388:Verified rather than assumed: wrapping 32-bit multiplication has no direct spelling in jq, since\n1404:integer exactly, jq included. Five of the six do, and jq is indeed one of them, since it\n1416:a real 64-bit integer that wraps -- JavaScript pays the BigInt representation, and jq, which\n1480:`lua`, `js`, `llvm`, `jq`, `go` and `py`.\n1498:32-bit rule is one modulo, and truncated division is a sign fixup over `//`. jq needed a split\n1523:jq's `if ... then ... else ... end` was the alternative and was rejected. The objections to\n1531:The deciding point is that this language has no statements. jq's form is an expression too, but\n1563:`range(n)` is zero-based, matching jq, Python, and this language's own indices, which is why the\n1568:\"b\"` does not typecheck, where jq would run both branches and hand back two answers. Both arms\n1602:What it cost is that three of jq's defining operators came out trivial. `.[]` is the identity on\n1609:language in which `.[]` does nothing is still recognisably in the jq family. Written up in\n1610:[a pure value layer dissolves jq's iteration operators](research-log/a-pure-value-layer-dissolves-jqs-iteration-operators.md).\n1738:`{name}` for `{name: .name}` is jq's most-used shorthand and is not being adopted, for a reason\n1761:What decided it is the call site rather than the cost. `join(\", \")` in jq says nothing about\n1811:flowing mode, is push and is Node's own documented backpressure footgun), and jq itself --\n1823:not pre-reading stdin into Rust before a subprocess backend runs, which this cut does -- an\n1840:any backend starts) is untouched, and a program may not use both: forced by jq specifically,\n1843:in one run -- verified against real jq before the rule was written.\n1864:alone as ordinary content, matching `jq -R` and Python's own raw stdin iteration, neither of\n1877:Two thirds of the six backends are separate OS processes Rust spawns (native, jq, Go, Python,\n1882:For the five subprocesses, genuine streaming meant stopping Rust from reading all of stdin into a\n1931:jq's own `inputs` -- every remaining JSON value on stdin, one per line -- was the obvious next\n1957:principle, and jq needs a different invocation flag for raw-line mode (`-R -n`) than for\n1979:## DECIDED: `jsonlines`, and the jq tutorial reproduces in full\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"grep -n \"pipe_through\\|pipe-through\\|pipe through\\|shell\\|external\\|subprocess\\|spawn\\|jq\" draft.md | head -50","time":{"start":1788297978521,"end":1788297978525}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"So the pattern for backend-specific limitations (like jq's Int64 divergence) is to keep those in tests/int64.rs (non-corpus), not in the corpus. The corpus only contains behavior all seven backends share.\n\nNow, for pipe_through: the corpus case can't be in the corpus (jq can't run it). So the \"corpus case FIRST\" instruction can't be satisfied for an `output` case. \n\nBut wait - maybe the corpus case CAN be there as a `refuses: true` case? Let me think about what that would pin. If the corpus case is `pipe_through` with `refuses: true`, then all seven backends must refuse to run it. That means pipe_through would be unimplemented (all refuse). That's not building the feature.\n\nHmm, unless... the design is that pipe_through programs are REFUSED by all seven because it's not implementable? No.\n\nOK let me step back and think really carefully about what a sensible implementation looks like, and whether there's a clever way to make all seven agree.\n\nActually... wait. Let me reconsider the whole premise. Maybe jq is NOT in scope because the shape `lines | pipe_through(...) | collect` — maybe for jq, pipe_through gets lowered to something using jq's ability... no.\n\nLet me reconsider: could the jq backend refuse pipe_through at emit time (like the cycle refusal), and we accept that the corpus case must then be `refuses: true`? That means ALL backends refuse. But the whole point is the feature works on some backends...\n\nI think the resolution really is one of two:\n1. Implement on 6 backends, test in a dedicated non-corpus test file (like tests/streaming.rs style or tests/int64.rs style), and NOT have a pipe_through corpus case. This means the \"corpus case FIRST\" instruction cannot be literally followed, and I should escalate.\n\n2. Change the harness to allow per-backend handling. Bigger decision.\n\nGiven the brief's escalation instruction, and the fact that this is genuinely a decision the brief doesn't settle (how to test a backend-gap feature when the harness is all-or-nothing), I should write ESCALATION.md.\n\nBut \"take the most conservative continuation, and keep going\" - the most conservative is to build the feature for the backends that CAN do it (6 backends), test it outside the corpus, and escalate the harness/corpus question.\n\nHmm, but actually, wait. Let me reconsider once more. Let me re-read the exact corpus agreement harness for a `refuses` case: it only checks that ALL backends refuse. For an output case, all must run.\n\nWhat if I make the corpus case `refuses: true`? Then all 7 must refuse. For jq, pipe_through refuses (emit error). For the other 6, they'd have to refuse too - meaning I DON'T implement the behavior, I make them refuse. That builds nothing. Not useful.\n\nSo the only way to actually build & test the feature is outside the corpus (per-backend tests), plus possibly a corpus case only if all seven can do it (impossible for jq).\n\n\n\nWait, actually, hold on. Let me reconsider whether jq really can't, because this is THE crux. Let me search online knowledge: jq is a lightweight and flexible command-line JSON processor. It has no subprocess invocation builtin. Correct. There's no `system`, no `exec`, no `run`. Confirmed by my earlier test: `system` is not defined in jq 1.8.2.\n\nSo jq cannot run an external command. Definitive.\n\nTherefore: pipe_through cannot be a corpus `output` case. It must be either refused-by-all (no build) or tested per-backend (outside corpus).\n\nGiven the feature is real and wanted, the natural continuation is: implement on the 6 capable backends + checker/parser, test via a dedicated test file, and escalate the corpus-harness question.\n\nBUT, hold on. Let me reconsider the scale. Implementing pipe_through on 6 backends (go, js, llvm, lua, py, rs) PLUS the checker, parser, tir, AND a streaming liveness test is a MASSIVE undertaking. This is easily a multi-hour implementation touching thousands of lines across 6 emit_*.rs files. And the streaming semantics (write to subprocess stdin while reading stdout, avoid deadlock) are subtle.\n\nGiven the constraints (expired runs, need commits, don't over-engineer), and the fundamental jq blocker requiring escalation... \n\nActually, let me reconsider the whole thing once more. Let me re-read the brief's very specific instruction: \"Add the corpus case FIRST (tests/corpus/, program/input/output per AGENTS.md), then implement parser/checker/backends until just check is green.\"\n\nThe brief is confident there will be a corpus case with program/input/output. This strongly implies the coordinator believed all 7 backends would handle it. But jq can't. So either:\n- The coordinator was wrong (likely), OR\n- There's an intended mechanism I'm missing.\n\nLet me search the codebase/docs for any hint about how pipe_through should work on jq. Let me check draft.md for the shell-out design and whether jq is addressed.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05edd9f77001M0ZAlpefagyS4T","sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","messageID":"msg_05edcd6a8001gwcLk67bNVbKr1"}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1788297978541,"sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","part":{"id":"prt_05edda2a70011e3bIynC7Bbuok","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"5a1012ab7eb7388a8de2a55a732be7fdbf823735","messageID":"msg_05edcd6a8001gwcLk67bNVbKr1","sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":81041,"input":45009,"output":0,"reasoning":1226,"cache":{"write":0,"read":34816}},"cost":0.003703321}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788297980894,"sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","part":{"id":"prt_05eddabd80012ThvqNNKPMY7B4","messageID":"msg_05edda2b7001uoGF81qefImkzD","sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","snapshot":"5a1012ab7eb7388a8de2a55a732be7fdbf823735","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1788297981896,"sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","part":{"id":"prt_05eddae1d001oftVkc3rkoFGfT","messageID":"msg_05edda2b7001uoGF81qefImkzD","sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","type":"text","text":"\n\n","time":{"start":1788297981469,"end":1788297981891}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788297981912,"sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_bcbbbfb4fd134dff86342069","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/draft.md","limit":160,"offset":1800},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/draft.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1800: in general. Q1 leans evaluation-level -- no `Stream<T>` type constructor, multiplicity born from\n1801: I/O and dying into values -- and that leaning is close to right but general enough to be\n1802: expensive to get wrong. What is decided here is the smallest concrete instance that neither\n1803: commits to nor contradicts it: one monomorphic, unparameterised primitive, not `Stream<T>` over\n1804: an arbitrary `T`.\n1805: \n1806: ### Pull, not push\n1807: \n1808: Every mature abstraction whose job is specifically \"do not let the producer outrun the consumer\"\n1809: turns out to be pull: Rust's `Iterator`, Python's generators, JavaScript's async iterators (the\n1810: layer people actually write against, even though the *raw* primitive underneath, `Readable` in\n1811: flowing mode, is push and is Node's own documented backpressure footgun), and jq itself --\n1812: verified empirically, `limit(3; range(100000000000))` returns three values instantly rather than\n1813: attempting to materialise an impossible sequence. Push-based designs need a second, explicit\n1814: protocol bolted on to get the same property (Node's `pause`/`resume`, Reactive Streams' `request`\n1815: demand signal); pull gets it as a consequence of the calling convention, for free.\n1816: \n1817: Two costs of this choice, named rather than discovered later. Fan-out -- using one stream in two\n1818: places -- is not supported; a pulled item is consumed once, and buffering it into a `Vec` first\n1819: (`collect`) is the only way to reuse it. Concurrency and overlap between pipeline stages are not\n1820: supported either; a pull chain does not run ahead of the consumer, so nothing overlaps with\n1821: anything. Both are named non-goals for this cut rather than gaps found later. What is not lost:\n1822: cross-process overlap, the kind `grep foo | wc -l` already gets from the kernel, is a property of\n1823: not pre-reading stdin into Rust before a subprocess backend runs, which this cut does -- an\n1824: architectural fact rather than a language feature.\n1825: \n1826: ### `lines`, `collect`, and nothing else exposed\n1827: \n1828: ```\n1829: unlines(collect(lines))\n1830: ```\n1831: \n1832: is the acceptance program: `cat`, echoing stdin back unchanged. `lines` is a bare keyword, zero\n1833: arguments, of one new monomorphic type with no spelling in the type grammar -- unlike `Str` or\n1834: `Int`, it cannot be a function's declared parameter or return type, which is what makes several\n1835: downstream checks unconditionally true rather than needing their own case: a `Lines` value can\n1836: never cross a function boundary, so a function body's synthesised type can never disagree with a\n1837: declared return type by containing one.\n1838: \n1839: No general `stdin` value is exposed. `input` (JSON, one document, read whole, validated before\n1840: any backend starts) is untouched, and a program may not use both: forced by jq specifically,\n1841: since raw-input mode (`-R`), needed for `[inputs]` to read lines rather than JSON, changes what\n1842: the whole invocation means, not just one call, and cannot coexist with `.`-is-the-document mode\n1843: in one run -- verified against real jq before the rule was written.\n1844: \n1845: `lines` may be referenced once, checked by the same mechanism that already caught `input` being\n1846: used at two disagreeing types, simplified to \"used at all, twice.\" Python's generators are the\n1847: cautionary example on the other side of this decision: consuming one twice silently returns\n1848: nothing, no error, and Rust's ownership model catching the equivalent mistake at compile time\n1849: (usually) is closer to what was chosen here on purpose.\n1850: \n1851: `Lines` is refused from a Vec, a record field, or the program's own printed result, checked at\n1852: three separate points rather than one, because a single \"does the top-level result contain\n1853: Lines\" scan does not catch every path: `collect([lines][0]!)` never makes Lines the program's own\n1854: result, but would have reached `to_slot`/`read_slot`/`go_type`'s struct-of-arrays or\n1855: closure-typing machinery with a type those never expected, on every backend, had the containment\n1856: check not been pushed back to where a Vec or record literal is built.\n1857: \n1858: ### The line rule, checked rather than assumed\n1859: \n1860: Split on `\\n` only. Strip it from each yielded string. No trailing empty entry when input ends in\n1861: `\\n`. The final line is still yielded even with none, which is deliberately not `wc -l`'s\n1862: mistake: `wc -l` counts newline characters rather than lines, so a file missing its last one is\n1863: undercounted -- verified directly, `printf 'a\\nb'` reports 1 rather than 2. A bare `\\r` is left\n1864: alone as ordinary content, matching `jq -R` and Python's own raw stdin iteration, neither of\n1865: which treats CRLF specially; Go's default `bufio.Scanner` split function does strip it, verified\n1866: directly, so the native Go split function is `bufio.ScanLines` with that one line removed rather\n1867: than the stdlib default.\n1868: \n1869: Every other backend's own native mechanism already agrees with the rule once verified: `getline`\n1870: (native), `io.lines()` (Lua, confirmed reading the real process stdin directly under `mlua`'s\n1871: default, unrestricted `Lua::new()`), raw `for line in sys.stdin` (Python), and a hand-written\n1872: `fs.readSync` chunk loop (JavaScript, which has no synchronous line reader built in -- the\n1873: well-known reason competitive-programming Node code writes this exact idiom by hand).\n1874: \n1875: ### What \"each backend reads for itself\" actually meant to build\n1876: \n1877: Two thirds of the six backends are separate OS processes Rust spawns (native, jq, Go, Python,\n1878: JavaScript); the toylang compiler is never a runtime dependency of what any of them emits, only\n1879: of producing it. The sixth, Lua, is not a process at all: `mlua` embeds the interpreter in the\n1880: same address space as the Rust harness, so \"reading stdin\" there is a function call, not IPC.\n1881: \n1882: For the five subprocesses, genuine streaming meant stopping Rust from reading all of stdin into a\n1883: `String` and re-`write_all`-ing it once, and instead connecting the real stdin straight through\n1884: (`Stdio::inherit()`) so the OS does the buffering it was always going to do. `main.rs`'s existing\n1885: gate -- read stdin only if `input` is used -- already did the right thing for `lines` by\n1886: accident, since neither construct needs Rust to touch the real stdin at all.\n1887: \n1888: What is fed depends on whether a caller supplied fixture text. `Some(text)` (a corpus case, most\n1889: often) is piped in verbatim and each backend does its own real splitting against it, which is the\n1890: only way the corpus can prove all six backends' splitting genuinely agrees rather than testing a\n1891: Rust-side reimplementation instead. `None` (the real command-line case) connects the live stdin.\n1892: Getting this distinction wrong the first time silently would have meant every corpus case for\n1893: this feature passing while testing nothing: the first version skipped writing fixture text to a\n1894: `lines` program's pipe entirely, matching `Stdio::inherit()`'s empty-until-corrected mental model\n1895: rather than the fixture's actual bytes, and would have reported every case as agreeing on an\n1896: empty string.\n1897: \n1898: Lua needed a different mechanism for the same distinction, since `Stdio` does not apply to a\n1899: function call in the same process, and `cargo test` runs many tests concurrently on shared\n1900: threads, which rules out redirecting the test process's own real stdin even temporarily. The\n1901: fixture is written to a file of its own, and the *global* `io.lines` function -- not `io.stdin`\n1902: -- is overridden to call the *real* `io.lines`, pointed at that file, so Lua's own line-splitting\n1903: still runs, verified rather than replaced by a Rust-side reimplementation feeding a pre-split\n1904: list.\n1905: \n1906: ### What this does not attempt\n1907: \n1908: No progressive output: a program still computes one final value and prints it once, unchanged\n1909: from every other program in the language. Real per-line output, and what a stream of *outputs*\n1910: would even mean, is [Q35](plans/questions.md#q35-what-are-stdout-and-stderr-and-does-a-program-write-or-return)'s\n1911: territory and stays there. No concurrency, no event loop, no `async`/`await` -- and not\n1912: having them is not merely smaller, it sidesteps two more named historical mistakes: JavaScript's\n1913: `Promise` starting eagerly with no cancellation designed in (irrelevant here, since nothing runs\n1914: until asked), and the sync/async colouring split that Rust and Python both still carry scars\n1915: from (irrelevant here, since there is only one calling convention). A future main loop, if one is\n1916: ever built, would not invalidate this: `stdin`/`lines` as a pull-shaped surface over an\n1917: event-loop-driven scheduler underneath is exactly how Python's `asyncio` and JavaScript's async\n1918: iterators are actually built, precedent rather than a hope.\n1919: \n1920: ## DECIDED: one stdin source is the destination\n1921: \n1922: Direction settled 2026-08-28: the three stdin keywords (`input`, `inputs`, `lines`) are\n1923: officially transitional. The destination is one raw source with parsing as ordinary,\n1924: visible steps -- the design rejected earlier *only* because expected types could not flow\n1925: into map bodies, a blocker the type-flow rework removes. Syntax is deliberately not settled\n1926: here; it gets its own session once type-flow lands. Until then: no fourth stdin reader, no\n1927: new exclusivity rules -- nothing that makes the migration bigger.\n1928: \n1929: ## DECIDED: `inputs`, eager, not an answer to Q1 either\n1930: \n1931: jq's own `inputs` -- every remaining JSON value on stdin, one per line -- was the obvious next\n1932: question once Field distributing over a Vec turned out to only need what CONTEXT.md's Spec\n1933: glossary already calls Keep and Narrow \"streamable\" for: an iterator, not storage. That\n1934: observation does not, on its own, get toylang a lazy `Stream<T>`,\n1935: because a program still has to terminate and return one value\n1936: ([Q35](plans/questions.md#q35-what-are-stdout-and-stderr-and-does-a-program-write-or-return) again): nothing can\n1937: consume a stream incrementally forever today, so the only thing genuine laziness would buy is\n1938: memory, not a new capability. `inputs` is eager instead: a bare keyword, typed `Vec<T>`, `T`\n1939: inferred from first use exactly the way `input`'s own type is -- read in full, parsed, collected,\n1940: before the program body runs. Ergonomics, not the general question; Q1 stays exactly as open as it\n1941: was.\n1942: \n1943: A more general alternative was considered first and rejected on a concrete, checkable ground\n1944: rather than taste: a `parse(s: Str) -> T` builtin, composing as `collect(lines) | map(parse(.))`,\n1945: would have been more reusable (also parsing a JSON string embedded in an ordinary field) and\n1946: would have needed no third stdin mode at all. It does not work with the checker as it stands.\n1947: `expect()` threads an expected type into exactly one form, `Expr::Input`; a `map` body is always\n1948: `synth`ed bottom-up, with nothing flowing in from whatever consumes the map's result. `parse(.)`\n1949: inside `map` has no expected type to resolve `T` against, the same hole an empty `[]` literal\n1950: falls into and for the same reason. Fixing that generally -- expected types flowing through `Map`,\n1951: at minimum -- is real, separate checker work, not scoped to this feature, and this would only be\n1952: its first forcing use case.\n1953: \n1954: All three stdin readers are now mutually exclusive, not just `input` and `lines` as before, and\n1955: the third leg was not a judgment call: Python's `input` reads all of stdin to EOF as one buffer\n1956: (`sys.stdin.buffer.read()`) before parsing, leaving nothing for `inputs` to read afterward even in\n1957: principle, and jq needs a different invocation flag for raw-line mode (`-R -n`) than for\n1958: parsed-JSON mode (`-n` alone) -- one process cannot run with both. `inputs` and `lines` could not\n1959: have coexisted on either backend regardless of what this document decided.\n\n(Showing lines 1800-1959 of 2480. Use offset=1960 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"in general. Q1 leans evaluation-level -- no `Stream<T>` type constructor, multiplicity born from\nI/O and dying into values -- and that leaning is close to right but general enough to be\nexpensive to get wrong. What is decided here is the smallest concrete instance that neither\ncommits to nor contradicts it: one monomorphic, unparameterised primitive, not `Stream<T>` over\nan arbitrary `T`.\n\n### Pull, not push\n\nEvery mature abstraction whose job is specifically \"do not let the producer outrun the consumer\"\nturns out to be pull: Rust's `Iterator`, Python's generators, JavaScript's async iterators (the\nlayer people actually write against, even though the *raw* primitive underneath, `Readable` in\nflowing mode, is push and is Node's own documented backpressure footgun), and jq itself --\nverified empirically, `limit(3; range(100000000000))` returns three values instantly rather than\nattempting to materialise an impossible sequence. Push-based designs need a second, explicit\nprotocol bolted on to get the same property (Node's `pause`/`resume`, Reactive Streams' `request`\ndemand signal); pull gets it as a consequence of the calling convention, for free.\n\nTwo costs of this choice, named rather than discovered later. Fan-out -- using one stream in two\nplaces -- is not supported; a pulled item is consumed once, and buffering it into a `Vec` first\n(`collect`) is the only way to reuse it. Concurrency and overlap between pipeline stages are not","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/draft.md","text":"in general. Q1 leans evaluation-level -- no `Stream<T>` type constructor, multiplicity born from\nI/O and dying into values -- and that leaning is close to right but general enough to be\nexpensive to get wrong. What is decided here is the smallest concrete instance that neither\ncommits to nor contradicts it: one monomorphic, unparameterised primitive, not `Stream<T>` over\nan arbitrary `T`.\n\n### Pull, not push\n\nEvery mature abstraction whose job is specifically \"do not let the producer outrun the consumer\"\nturns out to be pull: Rust's `Iterator`, Python's generators, JavaScript's async iterators (the\nlayer people actually write against, even though the *raw* primitive underneath, `Readable` in\nflowing mode, is push and is Node's own documented backpressure footgun), and jq itself --\nverified empirically, `limit(3; range(100000000000))` returns three values instantly rather than\nattempting to materialise an impossible sequence. Push-based designs need a second, explicit\nprotocol bolted on to get the same property (Node's `pause`/`resume`, Reactive Streams' `request`\ndemand signal); pull gets it as a consequence of the calling convention, for free.\n\nTwo costs of this choice, named rather than discovered later. Fan-out -- using one stream in two\nplaces -- is not supported; a pulled item is consumed once, and buffering it into a `Vec` first\n(`collect`) is the only way to reuse it. Concurrency and overlap between pipeline stages are not\nsupported either; a pull chain does not run ahead of the consumer, so nothing overlaps with\nanything. Both are named non-goals for this cut rather than gaps found later. What is not lost:\ncross-process overlap, the kind `grep foo | wc -l` already gets from the kernel, is a property of\nnot pre-reading stdin into Rust before a subprocess backend runs, which this cut does -- an\narchitectural fact rather than a language feature.\n\n### `lines`, `collect`, and nothing else exposed\n\n```\nunlines(collect(lines))\n```\n\nis the acceptance program: `cat`, echoing stdin back unchanged. `lines` is a bare keyword, zero\narguments, of one new monomorphic type with no spelling in the type grammar -- unlike `Str` or\n`Int`, it cannot be a function's declared parameter or return type, which is what makes several\ndownstream checks unconditionally true rather than needing their own case: a `Lines` value can\nnever cross a function boundary, so a function body's synthesised type can never disagree with a\ndeclared return type by containing one.\n\nNo general `stdin` value is exposed. `input` (JSON, one document, read whole, validated before\nany backend starts) is untouched, and a program may not use both: forced by jq specifically,\nsince raw-input mode (`-R`), needed for `[inputs]` to read lines rather than JSON, changes what\nthe whole invocation means, not just one call, and cannot coexist with `.`-is-the-document mode\nin one run -- verified against real jq before the rule was written.\n\n`lines` may be referenced once, checked by the same mechanism that already caught `input` being\nused at two disagreeing types, simplified to \"used at all, twice.\" Python's generators are the\ncautionary example on the other side of this decision: consuming one twice silently returns\nnothing, no error, and Rust's ownership model catching the equivalent mistake at compile time\n(usually) is closer to what was chosen here on purpose.\n\n`Lines` is refused from a Vec, a record field, or the program's own printed result, checked at\nthree separate points rather than one, because a single \"does the top-level result contain\nLines\" scan does not catch every path: `collect([lines][0]!)` never makes Lines the program's own\nresult, but would have reached `to_slot`/`read_slot`/`go_type`'s struct-of-arrays or\nclosure-typing machinery with a type those never expected, on every backend, had the containment\ncheck not been pushed back to where a Vec or record literal is built.\n\n### The line rule, checked rather than assumed\n\nSplit on `\\n` only. Strip it from each yielded string. No trailing empty entry when input ends in\n`\\n`. The final line is still yielded even with none, which is deliberately not `wc -l`'s\nmistake: `wc -l` counts newline characters rather than lines, so a file missing its last one is\nundercounted -- verified directly, `printf 'a\\nb'` reports 1 rather than 2. A bare `\\r` is left\nalone as ordinary content, matching `jq -R` and Python's own raw stdin iteration, neither of\nwhich treats CRLF specially; Go's default `bufio.Scanner` split function does strip it, verified\ndirectly, so the native Go split function is `bufio.ScanLines` with that one line removed rather\nthan the stdlib default.\n\nEvery other backend's own native mechanism already agrees with the rule once verified: `getline`\n(native), `io.lines()` (Lua, confirmed reading the real process stdin directly under `mlua`'s\ndefault, unrestricted `Lua::new()`), raw `for line in sys.stdin` (Python), and a hand-written\n`fs.readSync` chunk loop (JavaScript, which has no synchronous line reader built in -- the\nwell-known reason competitive-programming Node code writes this exact idiom by hand).\n\n### What \"each backend reads for itself\" actually meant to build\n\nTwo thirds of the six backends are separate OS processes Rust spawns (native, jq, Go, Python,\nJavaScript); the toylang compiler is never a runtime dependency of what any of them emits, only\nof producing it. The sixth, Lua, is not a process at all: `mlua` embeds the interpreter in the\nsame address space as the Rust harness, so \"reading stdin\" there is a function call, not IPC.\n\nFor the five subprocesses, genuine streaming meant stopping Rust from reading all of stdin into a\n`String` and re-`write_all`-ing it once, and instead connecting the real stdin straight through\n(`Stdio::inherit()`) so the OS does the buffering it was always going to do. `main.rs`'s existing\ngate -- read stdin only if `input` is used -- already did the right thing for `lines` by\naccident, since neither construct needs Rust to touch the real stdin at all.\n\nWhat is fed depends on whether a caller supplied fixture text. `Some(text)` (a corpus case, most\noften) is piped in verbatim and each backend does its own real splitting against it, which is the\nonly way the corpus can prove all six backends' splitting genuinely agrees rather than testing a\nRust-side reimplementation instead. `None` (the real command-line case) connects the live stdin.\nGetting this distinction wrong the first time silently would have meant every corpus case for\nthis feature passing while testing nothing: the first version skipped writing fixture text to a\n`lines` program's pipe entirely, matching `Stdio::inherit()`'s empty-until-corrected mental model\nrather than the fixture's actual bytes, and would have reported every case as agreeing on an\nempty string.\n\nLua needed a different mechanism for the same distinction, since `Stdio` does not apply to a\nfunction call in the same process, and `cargo test` runs many tests concurrently on shared\nthreads, which rules out redirecting the test process's own real stdin even temporarily. The\nfixture is written to a file of its own, and the *global* `io.lines` function -- not `io.stdin`\n-- is overridden to call the *real* `io.lines`, pointed at that file, so Lua's own line-splitting\nstill runs, verified rather than replaced by a Rust-side reimplementation feeding a pre-split\nlist.\n\n### What this does not attempt\n\nNo progressive output: a program still computes one final value and prints it once, unchanged\nfrom every other program in the language. Real per-line output, and what a stream of *outputs*\nwould even mean, is [Q35](plans/questions.md#q35-what-are-stdout-and-stderr-and-does-a-program-write-or-return)'s\nterritory and stays there. No concurrency, no event loop, no `async`/`await` -- and not\nhaving them is not merely smaller, it sidesteps two more named historical mistakes: JavaScript's\n`Promise` starting eagerly with no cancellation designed in (irrelevant here, since nothing runs\nuntil asked), and the sync/async colouring split that Rust and Python both still carry scars\nfrom (irrelevant here, since there is only one calling convention). A future main loop, if one is\never built, would not invalidate this: `stdin`/`lines` as a pull-shaped surface over an\nevent-loop-driven scheduler underneath is exactly how Python's `asyncio` and JavaScript's async\niterators are actually built, precedent rather than a hope.\n\n## DECIDED: one stdin source is the destination\n\nDirection settled 2026-08-28: the three stdin keywords (`input`, `inputs`, `lines`) are\nofficially transitional. The destination is one raw source with parsing as ordinary,\nvisible steps -- the design rejected earlier *only* because expected types could not flow\ninto map bodies, a blocker the type-flow rework removes. Syntax is deliberately not settled\nhere; it gets its own session once type-flow lands. Until then: no fourth stdin reader, no\nnew exclusivity rules -- nothing that makes the migration bigger.\n\n## DECIDED: `inputs`, eager, not an answer to Q1 either\n\njq's own `inputs` -- every remaining JSON value on stdin, one per line -- was the obvious next\nquestion once Field distributing over a Vec turned out to only need what CONTEXT.md's Spec\nglossary already calls Keep and Narrow \"streamable\" for: an iterator, not storage. That\nobservation does not, on its own, get toylang a lazy `Stream<T>`,\nbecause a program still has to terminate and return one value\n([Q35](plans/questions.md#q35-what-are-stdout-and-stderr-and-does-a-program-write-or-return) again): nothing can\nconsume a stream incrementally forever today, so the only thing genuine laziness would buy is\nmemory, not a new capability. `inputs` is eager instead: a bare keyword, typed `Vec<T>`, `T`\ninferred from first use exactly the way `input`'s own type is -- read in full, parsed, collected,\nbefore the program body runs. Ergonomics, not the general question; Q1 stays exactly as open as it\nwas.\n\nA more general alternative was considered first and rejected on a concrete, checkable ground\nrather than taste: a `parse(s: Str) -> T` builtin, composing as `collect(lines) | map(parse(.))`,\nwould have been more reusable (also parsing a JSON string embedded in an ordinary field) and\nwould have needed no third stdin mode at all. It does not work with the checker as it stands.\n`expect()` threads an expected type into exactly one form, `Expr::Input`; a `map` body is always\n`synth`ed bottom-up, with nothing flowing in from whatever consumes the map's result. `parse(.)`\ninside `map` has no expected type to resolve `T` against, the same hole an empty `[]` literal\nfalls into and for the same reason. Fixing that generally -- expected types flowing through `Map`,\nat minimum -- is real, separate checker work, not scoped to this feature, and this would only be\nits first forcing use case.\n\nAll three stdin readers are now mutually exclusive, not just `input` and `lines` as before, and\nthe third leg was not a judgment call: Python's `input` reads all of stdin to EOF as one buffer\n(`sys.stdin.buffer.read()`) before parsing, leaving nothing for `inputs` to read afterward even in\nprinciple, and jq needs a different invocation flag for raw-line mode (`-R -n`) than for\nparsed-JSON mode (`-n` alone) -- one process cannot run with both. `inputs` and `lines` could not\nhave coexisted on either backend regardless of what this document decided.","lineStart":1800,"lineEnd":1959,"totalLines":2480,"truncated":true}},"title":"draft.md","time":{"start":1788297981885,"end":1788297981909}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Let me look at the streaming section of draft.md around lines 1800-1979 to understand the effect layer and what \"program that writes as it goes\" means. This will clarify whether pipe_through could be defined as a sink or in terms of the effect layer.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05eddafb3001DvbtO3q3xXw0lx","sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","messageID":"msg_05edda2b7001uoGF81qefImkzD"}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1788297981923,"sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","part":{"id":"prt_05eddafdf001Q6Q95NAM2wxL1D","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"5a1012ab7eb7388a8de2a55a732be7fdbf823735","messageID":"msg_05edda2b7001uoGF81qefImkzD","sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":82615,"input":4894,"output":90,"reasoning":63,"cache":{"write":0,"read":77568}},"cost":0.001586738}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788297982951,"sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","part":{"id":"prt_05eddb3de001fdPy2sNnZdw2aK","messageID":"msg_05eddafec001CFqZSTz1Y4PBUt","sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","snapshot":"5a1012ab7eb7388a8de2a55a732be7fdbf823735","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1788297992712,"sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","part":{"id":"prt_05eddd915001hCcCjcYNVrVL3I","messageID":"msg_05eddafec001CFqZSTz1Y4PBUt","sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","type":"text","text":"\n\n","time":{"start":1788297992469,"end":1788297992697}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788297992720,"sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_1d67f96bfdac4f9b982344e9","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/tests/int64.rs"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/tests/int64.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: //! The Int64 surface (kantord/toylang#83): what the checker refuses, and the wrapping edges\n2: //! the corpus cannot carry.\n3: //!\n4: //! The happy paths live in the corpus (tests/corpus/int64_*.yaml) like every other feature's.\n5: //! What is here instead is the 2^63 boundary: jq computes Int64 in IEEE doubles, exact only\n6: //! within +/-2^53, so a program whose values cross that line cannot be a corpus case -- the\n7: //! corpus requires all seven backends to agree, and past 2^53 jq honestly does not. The other\n8: //! six do, and that agreement is pinned here with jq's divergence beside it rather than hidden.\n9: \n10: use toylang::Backend;\n11: \n12: /// `Int64::MAX + 1` wraps to `Int64::MIN` on every backend whose integers are exact at 64\n13: /// bits: everything but jq.\n14: #[test]\n15: fn int64_wraps_at_the_2_63_boundary() {\n16:     let src = \"fn big() -> Int64 = 9223372036854775807\\n\\nbig() + 1\\n\";\n17:     for backend in Backend::ALL {\n18:         if backend == Backend::Jq {\n19:             continue;\n20:         }\n21:         let out = toylang::run_on(src, None, backend).unwrap();\n22:         assert_eq!(\n23:             out,\n24:             \"-9223372036854775808\\n\",\n25:             \"{} does not wrap at 2^63\",\n26:             backend.name()\n27:         );\n28:     }\n29: }\n30: \n31: /// `MIN / -1` and `MIN % -1` wrap like everything else, extending ADR 0006's one-way-to-fail\n32: /// rule to the width where the underlying hardware division would otherwise trap.\n33: #[test]\n34: fn int64_min_over_minus_one_wraps() {\n35:     let div = \"fn min() -> Int64 = -9223372036854775807\\n\\n(min() - 1) / i64(0 - 1)\\n\";\n36:     let rem = \"fn min() -> Int64 = -9223372036854775807\\n\\n(min() - 1) % i64(0 - 1)\\n\";\n37:     for backend in Backend::ALL {\n38:         if backend == Backend::Jq {\n39:             continue;\n40:         }\n41:         assert_eq!(\n42:             toylang::run_on(div, None, backend).unwrap(),\n43:             \"-9223372036854775808\\n\",\n44:             \"{}: MIN / -1 is MIN\",\n45:             backend.name()\n46:         );\n47:         assert_eq!(\n48:             toylang::run_on(rem, None, backend).unwrap(),\n49:             \"0\\n\",\n50:             \"{}: MIN % -1 is 0\",\n51:             backend.name()\n52:         );\n53:     }\n54: }\n55: \n56: /// The documented boundary, observed rather than assumed: past 2^53 jq's doubles round, so\n57: /// the same program that wraps everywhere else prints the unwrapped, rounded sum here. If\n58: /// this snapshot ever changes, jq's Int64 story changed with it.\n59: #[test]\n60: fn jq_int64_is_inexact_past_2_53() {\n61:     let src = \"fn big() -> Int64 = 9223372036854775807\\n\\nbig() + 1\\n\";\n62:     let out = toylang::run_on(src, None, Backend::Jq).unwrap();\n63:     assert_eq!(out, \"9223372036854776000\\n\");\n64: }\n65: \n66: /// No implicit widening: the two integer types never meet in one operator, and the error\n67: /// names the bridge.\n68: #[test]\n69: fn arithmetic_does_not_mix_the_widths() {\n70:     insta::assert_snapshot!(\n71:         toylang::compile(\"fn big() -> Int64 = 5\\n\\n1 + big()\")\n72:             .map(|_| ())\n73:             .unwrap_err()\n74:             .to_string()\n75:     );\n76: }\n77: \n78: #[test]\n79: fn comparison_does_not_mix_the_widths() {\n80:     insta::assert_snapshot!(\n81:         toylang::compile(\"fn big() -> Int64 = 5\\n\\nbig() < 2 + 2\")\n82:             .map(|_| ())\n83:             .unwrap_err()\n84:             .to_string()\n85:     );\n86: }\n87: \n88: /// A too-big literal with no expectation stays an error: nothing guesses Int64.\n89: #[test]\n90: fn a_wide_literal_needs_a_position_that_expects_int64() {\n91:     insta::assert_snapshot!(\n92:         toylang::compile(\"600851475143\")\n93:             .map(|_| ())\n94:             .unwrap_err()\n95:             .to_string()\n96:     );\n97: }\n98: \n99: /// `i64` takes an Int, so its own argument obeys the 32-bit literal rule: a wide value enters\n100: /// as a literal only where an Int64 is already expected, never through the bridge.\n101: #[test]\n102: fn i64_takes_an_int() {\n103:     insta::assert_snapshot!(\n104:         toylang::compile(\"i64(9000000000)\")\n105:             .map(|_| ())\n106:             .unwrap_err()\n107:             .to_string()\n108:     );\n109: }\n110: \n111: /// Reading an Int64 back off the wire is refused until its codec is decided\n112: /// (JS parses JSON numbers into doubles), the same reversible direction Opt takes.\n113: #[test]\n114: fn input_cannot_be_int64() {\n115:     insta::assert_snapshot!(\n116:         toylang::compile(\"fn f(n: Int64) -> Int64 = n\\n\\nf(input)\")\n117:             .map(|_| ())\n118:             .unwrap_err()\n119:             .to_string()\n120:     );\n121: }\n122: \n123: #[test]\n124: fn inputs_cannot_carry_int64() {\n125:     insta::assert_snapshot!(\n126:         toylang::compile(\n127:             \"fn f(s: Stream<{ts: Int64}>) -> Stream<{ts: Int64}> = s\\n\\njsonlines(f(inputs))\"\n128:         )\n129:         .map(|_| ())\n130:         .unwrap_err()\n131:         .to_string()\n132:     );\n133: }\n134: \n135: /// `str` stays Int-only: an Int64 result prints bare as the program's own output, and\n136: /// whether `str` should widen is its own question, not decided here.\n137: #[test]\n138: fn str_does_not_take_an_int64() {\n139:     insta::assert_snapshot!(\n140:         toylang::compile(\"fn big() -> Int64 = 5\\n\\nstr(big())\")\n141:             .map(|_| ())\n142:             .unwrap_err()\n143:             .to_string()\n144:     );\n145: }\n\n(End of file - total 145 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"//! The Int64 surface (kantord/toylang#83): what the checker refuses, and the wrapping edges\n//! the corpus cannot carry.\n//!\n//! The happy paths live in the corpus (tests/corpus/int64_*.yaml) like every other feature's.\n//! What is here instead is the 2^63 boundary: jq computes Int64 in IEEE doubles, exact only\n//! within +/-2^53, so a program whose values cross that line cannot be a corpus case -- the\n//! corpus requires all seven backends to agree, and past 2^53 jq honestly does not. The other\n//! six do, and that agreement is pinned here with jq's divergence beside it rather than hidden.\n\nuse toylang::Backend;\n\n/// `Int64::MAX + 1` wraps to `Int64::MIN` on every backend whose integers are exact at 64\n/// bits: everything but jq.\n#[test]\nfn int64_wraps_at_the_2_63_boundary() {\n    let src = \"fn big() -> Int64 = 9223372036854775807\\n\\nbig() + 1\\n\";\n    for backend in Backend::ALL {\n        if backend == Backend::Jq {\n            continue;\n        }","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/tests/int64.rs","text":"//! The Int64 surface (kantord/toylang#83): what the checker refuses, and the wrapping edges\n//! the corpus cannot carry.\n//!\n//! The happy paths live in the corpus (tests/corpus/int64_*.yaml) like every other feature's.\n//! What is here instead is the 2^63 boundary: jq computes Int64 in IEEE doubles, exact only\n//! within +/-2^53, so a program whose values cross that line cannot be a corpus case -- the\n//! corpus requires all seven backends to agree, and past 2^53 jq honestly does not. The other\n//! six do, and that agreement is pinned here with jq's divergence beside it rather than hidden.\n\nuse toylang::Backend;\n\n/// `Int64::MAX + 1` wraps to `Int64::MIN` on every backend whose integers are exact at 64\n/// bits: everything but jq.\n#[test]\nfn int64_wraps_at_the_2_63_boundary() {\n    let src = \"fn big() -> Int64 = 9223372036854775807\\n\\nbig() + 1\\n\";\n    for backend in Backend::ALL {\n        if backend == Backend::Jq {\n            continue;\n        }\n        let out = toylang::run_on(src, None, backend).unwrap();\n        assert_eq!(\n            out,\n            \"-9223372036854775808\\n\",\n            \"{} does not wrap at 2^63\",\n            backend.name()\n        );\n    }\n}\n\n/// `MIN / -1` and `MIN % -1` wrap like everything else, extending ADR 0006's one-way-to-fail\n/// rule to the width where the underlying hardware division would otherwise trap.\n#[test]\nfn int64_min_over_minus_one_wraps() {\n    let div = \"fn min() -> Int64 = -9223372036854775807\\n\\n(min() - 1) / i64(0 - 1)\\n\";\n    let rem = \"fn min() -> Int64 = -9223372036854775807\\n\\n(min() - 1) % i64(0 - 1)\\n\";\n    for backend in Backend::ALL {\n        if backend == Backend::Jq {\n            continue;\n        }\n        assert_eq!(\n            toylang::run_on(div, None, backend).unwrap(),\n            \"-9223372036854775808\\n\",\n            \"{}: MIN / -1 is MIN\",\n            backend.name()\n        );\n        assert_eq!(\n            toylang::run_on(rem, None, backend).unwrap(),\n            \"0\\n\",\n            \"{}: MIN % -1 is 0\",\n            backend.name()\n        );\n    }\n}\n\n/// The documented boundary, observed rather than assumed: past 2^53 jq's doubles round, so\n/// the same program that wraps everywhere else prints the unwrapped, rounded sum here. If\n/// this snapshot ever changes, jq's Int64 story changed with it.\n#[test]\nfn jq_int64_is_inexact_past_2_53() {\n    let src = \"fn big() -> Int64 = 9223372036854775807\\n\\nbig() + 1\\n\";\n    let out = toylang::run_on(src, None, Backend::Jq).unwrap();\n    assert_eq!(out, \"9223372036854776000\\n\");\n}\n\n/// No implicit widening: the two integer types never meet in one operator, and the error\n/// names the bridge.\n#[test]\nfn arithmetic_does_not_mix_the_widths() {\n    insta::assert_snapshot!(\n        toylang::compile(\"fn big() -> Int64 = 5\\n\\n1 + big()\")\n            .map(|_| ())\n            .unwrap_err()\n            .to_string()\n    );\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn comparison_does_not_mix_the_widths() {\n    insta::assert_snapshot!(\n        toylang::compile(\"fn big() -> Int64 = 5\\n\\nbig() < 2 + 2\")\n            .map(|_| ())\n            .unwrap_err()\n            .to_string()\n    );\n}\n\n/// A too-big literal with no expectation stays an error: nothing guesses Int64.\n#[test]\nfn a_wide_literal_needs_a_position_that_expects_int64() {\n    insta::assert_snapshot!(\n        toylang::compile(\"600851475143\")\n            .map(|_| ())\n            .unwrap_err()\n            .to_string()\n    );\n}\n\n/// `i64` takes an Int, so its own argument obeys the 32-bit literal rule: a wide value enters\n/// as a literal only where an Int64 is already expected, never through the bridge.\n#[test]\nfn i64_takes_an_int() {\n    insta::assert_snapshot!(\n        toylang::compile(\"i64(9000000000)\")\n            .map(|_| ())\n            .unwrap_err()\n            .to_string()\n    );\n}\n\n/// Reading an Int64 back off the wire is refused until its codec is decided\n/// (JS parses JSON numbers into doubles), the same reversible direction Opt takes.\n#[test]\nfn input_cannot_be_int64() {\n    insta::assert_snapshot!(\n        toylang::compile(\"fn f(n: Int64) -> Int64 = n\\n\\nf(input)\")\n            .map(|_| ())\n            .unwrap_err()\n            .to_string()\n    );\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn inputs_cannot_carry_int64() {\n    insta::assert_snapshot!(\n        toylang::compile(\n            \"fn f(s: Stream<{ts: Int64}>) -> Stream<{ts: Int64}> = s\\n\\njsonlines(f(inputs))\"\n        )\n        .map(|_| ())\n        .unwrap_err()\n        .to_string()\n    );\n}\n\n/// `str` stays Int-only: an Int64 result prints bare as the program's own output, and\n/// whether `str` should widen is its own question, not decided here.\n#[test]\nfn str_does_not_take_an_int64() {\n    insta::assert_snapshot!(\n        toylang::compile(\"fn big() -> Int64 = 5\\n\\nstr(big())\")\n            .map(|_| ())\n            .unwrap_err()\n            .to_string()\n    );\n}","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":145,"totalLines":145,"truncated":false}},"title":"tests/int64.rs","time":{"start":1788297992695,"end":1788297992714}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"OK. Now I understand the design context deeply. \n\nSo the shape `lines | pipe_through(\"grep\", [\"foo\"]) | collect` — this is a stream-to-stream primitive. Given the all-or-nothing corpus rule, this cannot be a corpus `output` case unless jq can do it. jq can't.\n\n \n\nNow, the key question for me as the delegated worker: what does \"the most conservative continuation\" look like?\n\nLet me reconsider option D more concretely: implement pipe_through on the six backends that can spawn subprocesses (lua, js, native, go, py, rust), and NOT on jq. Test in a dedicated non-corpus test file. Escalate the corpus question.\n\n\n\nBut hold on. Actually, wait. Let me reconsider whether maybe the intended design is that pipe_through is *not* a stream-to-stream transform at the value level, but rather a primitive in the *effect layer* similar to jsonlines. In the shape `lines | pipe_through(\"grep\", [\"foo\"]) | collect`, pipe_through sits in the middle of a pipeline. It's stream-in, stream-out. So it's not a sink like jsonlines. It's a transform. For jq, the emitted code would have to be a filter over [inputs] that somehow... no.\n\n\n\nOK, I'm now very confident about the blocker. Let me also double-check: is there perhaps a corpus case mechanism I haven't seen, like `skip: [jq]`? deny_unknown_fields would reject unknown keys. And the Fields struct has exactly: program, input, output, refuses, snapshot, node_types. No skip. So no.\n\n.\n\n\n\nNow let me think about what to actually DO. Given the brief's definition of done: \"implementation complete; `just check` green from the worktree root\". And the escalation rule: \"If something needs deciding that this brief does not settle, write ESCALATION.md at the worktree root -- the question, the alternatives, their costs -- COMMIT it on this branch, take the most conservative continuation, and keep going; never stop to wait for a human.\"\n\n\n\nThe jq/corpus question is EXACTLY something the brief does not settle. So I must:\n1. Write ESCALATION.md (question, alternatives, costs),\n2. Commit it,\n3. Take the most conservative continuation and keep going.\n\n\n\nWhat's the most conservative continuation? I think it's: implement pipe_through on the backends that CAN support it (6 of 7), and test it in a way that doesn't break the corpus (i.e., a dedicated test file, not a corpus case,. And DON'T change the all-or-nothing corpus harness without authorization.\n\n\n\nBUT, hmm. Wait. Actually, let me reconsider. Maybe I should reconsider whether the corpus case should be `refuses: true` after all. Let me think about what pipe_through's semantics would be in a language that refuses it.\n\nNo. The issue says \"build a streaming pipe-to-subprocess primitive\". If all backends refuse it, it's not built. So no.\n\n.\n\n\n\nNow, before I commit to the huge implementation across 6 backends, let me assess the scope more carefully, and figure out the cleanest design that fits the existing architecture. Actually, wait. Given how the codebase is structured, maybe there's a much simpler intended implementation. Let me think about the semantics again:\n\n`lines | pipe_through(\"grep\", [\"foo\"]) | collect`\n\n- `lines`: Stream<Str> (source)\n- `pipe_through(cmd, args)`: Stream<Str> -> Stream<Str> (transform)\n- `collect`: Stream<Str> -> Vec<Str> (sink)\n\n\n\nUnder EAGER lowering (which the backends currently use for everything except the jsonlines fusion),the transform is easy: since the streams are materialized as Vecs anyway, pipe_through can be lowered as:\n```\nlines -> Vec<Str> (all stdin lines)\npipe_through: write all lines to the subprocess's stdin, close it, read all stdout lines -> Vec<Str> (result stream, materialized,\ncollect: identity\n```\n\nWait, but then it's not \"streaming\". But the issue's title says \"streaming pipe-to-subprocess\". And the corpus can't observe streaming anyway (it only checks output bytes. And tests/streaming.rs has liveness probes for the jsonlines fusion. For pipe_through, a liveness probe would require... hmm, a test that writes one line, waits for grep's output before stdin closes? That would actually be REAL streaming (grep produces output after each line even without EOF, when given enough buffering... but grep line-buffers when stdout is a tty, block-buffers otherwise. So a live probe would need careful buffering handling. That's complex.)\n\n\n\nGiven the brief says \"implement parser/checker/backends until just check is green\", and the corpus case is the test surface (per the brief), maybe the intended scope IS just: corpus case passes on all backends + the parser/checker plumbing. But jq...\n\n\n\nUgh. OK. Let me now actually look at how the check works to understand how much plumbing pipe_through needs (parser, ast, tir, ty, check). And let me look at how `collect` and `jsonlines` are checked, to mimic. But first, let me settle the escalation question by examining whether there is any other precedent in the repo for \"one backend can't run a corpus feature\" - i.e., a feature that works on 6 backends, tested outside the corpus. tests/int64.rs is exactly that (jq divergence pinned beside six others).. So precedent exists for option D.\n\n \n\nLet me look at tests/int64.rs briefly to understand the \"pinned across six backends with jq's divergent answer asserted beside them\" pattern.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05eddd9f3001M6PYFtJwpcFIWF","sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","messageID":"msg_05eddafec001CFqZSTz1Y4PBUt"}}
