{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788297993749,"sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","part":{"id":"prt_05eddde13001fFAzVaEgxalXA9","messageID":"msg_05eddda20001MXg8AMbfZOrhRB","sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","snapshot":"5a1012ab7eb7388a8de2a55a732be7fdbf823735","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788298008271,"sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_64af22cce9674812b33adc08","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/justfile"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/justfile</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # List available recipes.\n2: default:\n3:     @just --list\n4: \n5: # The final gate: the full test suite. Landing requires this green.\n6: test:\n7:     cargo nextest run\n8: \n9: # The fast inner loop: skips the docs mega-test, the suite's ~137s long pole. Gate on `just test`.\n10: check:\n11:     cargo nextest run -E 'not test(every_fragment_is_a_real_program)'\n12: \n13: # Opt-in: run the skipped Euler 8/11/13/18 programs against real puzzle data in DIR, outside\n14: # `just test`. DIR holds your own copies of the raw data texts; fails loudly, never skips.\n15: euler-data DIR:\n16:     EULER_DATA={{DIR}} cargo nextest run --run-ignored ignored-only -E 'test(euler_real_data)'\n17: \n18: # Re-run the slow-fragment tier: the same suite, but `slow` fragments are executed on every\n19: # backend rather than only type-checked and emitted. The tier exists so `just test` stays fast;\n20: # this is where the deferred execution claim is re-verified.\n21: slow-test:\n22:     TOYLANG_SLOW=1 cargo nextest run\n23: \n24: # Run clippy with the repo's lint set, the same surface the Stop hook checks.\n25: clippy:\n26:     cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets\n27: \n28: # Formatter check over every .toy file from the repo root down (exit 1 on drift).\n29: fmt:\n30:     cargo run -q -- fmt\n31: \n32: # Rewrite drifted files in place (same exit code as the check).\n33: fmt-write:\n34:     cargo run -q -- fmt --write\n35: \n36: # The repo's mechanical checks, the same surface the Stop hook runs.\n37: checks:\n38:     bash .claude/checks/run.sh\n39: \n40: # The autonomous drive loop: a tick every 600s, live colorized output, Ctrl-C to stop. Run ONE.\n41: drive:\n42:     .claude/scripts/drive-loop.sh\n43: \n44: # Fire one coordinator tick right now (zero tokens if there is nothing to do).\n45: tick:\n46:     .claude/scripts/drive-tick.sh\n47: \n48: # Watch the current coordinator tick live (detaches with Ctrl-C, tick untouched).\n49: peek:\n50:     .claude/scripts/tick-peek.sh\n\n(End of file - total 50 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# List available recipes.\ndefault:\n    @just --list\n\n# The final gate: the full test suite. Landing requires this green.\ntest:\n    cargo nextest run\n\n# The fast inner loop: skips the docs mega-test, the suite's ~137s long pole. Gate on `just test`.\ncheck:\n    cargo nextest run -E 'not test(every_fragment_is_a_real_program)'\n\n# Opt-in: run the skipped Euler 8/11/13/18 programs against real puzzle data in DIR, outside\n# `just test`. DIR holds your own copies of the raw data texts; fails loudly, never skips.\neuler-data DIR:\n    EULER_DATA={{DIR}} cargo nextest run --run-ignored ignored-only -E 'test(euler_real_data)'\n\n# Re-run the slow-fragment tier: the same suite, but `slow` fragments are executed on every\n# backend rather than only type-checked and emitted. The tier exists so `just test` stays fast;\n# this is where the deferred execution claim is re-verified.","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/justfile","text":"# List available recipes.\ndefault:\n    @just --list\n\n# The final gate: the full test suite. Landing requires this green.\ntest:\n    cargo nextest run\n\n# The fast inner loop: skips the docs mega-test, the suite's ~137s long pole. Gate on `just test`.\ncheck:\n    cargo nextest run -E 'not test(every_fragment_is_a_real_program)'\n\n# Opt-in: run the skipped Euler 8/11/13/18 programs against real puzzle data in DIR, outside\n# `just test`. DIR holds your own copies of the raw data texts; fails loudly, never skips.\neuler-data DIR:\n    EULER_DATA={{DIR}} cargo nextest run --run-ignored ignored-only -E 'test(euler_real_data)'\n\n# Re-run the slow-fragment tier: the same suite, but `slow` fragments are executed on every\n# backend rather than only type-checked and emitted. The tier exists so `just test` stays fast;\n# this is where the deferred execution claim is re-verified.\nslow-test:\n    TOYLANG_SLOW=1 cargo nextest run\n\n# Run clippy with the repo's lint set, the same surface the Stop hook checks.\nclippy:\n    cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets\n\n# Formatter check over every .toy file from the repo root down (exit 1 on drift).\nfmt:\n    cargo run -q -- fmt\n\n# Rewrite drifted files in place (same exit code as the check).\nfmt-write:\n    cargo run -q -- fmt --write\n\n# The repo's mechanical checks, the same surface the Stop hook runs.\nchecks:\n    bash .claude/checks/run.sh\n\n# The autonomous drive loop: a tick every 600s, live colorized output, Ctrl-C to stop. Run ONE.\ndrive:\n    .claude/scripts/drive-loop.sh\n\n# Fire one coordinator tick right now (zero tokens if there is nothing to do).\ntick:\n    .claude/scripts/drive-tick.sh\n\n# Watch the current coordinator tick live (detaches with Ctrl-C, tick untouched).\npeek:\n    .claude/scripts/tick-peek.sh","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":50,"totalLines":50,"truncated":false}},"title":"justfile","time":{"start":1788298008266,"end":1788298008270}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"This is the exact precedent. The corpus requires all seven backends to agree, and when jq can't, the feature's corpus cases stay out of the corpus, and the non-corpus test pins six with jq's behavior (refusal, or divergence) beside it.\n\n\n\nFor pipe_through: jq can't run it at all (not \"diverges\", it can't). So the non-corpus test would be: all seven backends attempt it; six run it and agree; jq... hmm, what does jq do? If jq's emit refuses pipe_through, then `run_on(program, input, Jq)` returns Err. So the test would assert six agree, and jq errors(assert its refusal) beside them. Like: \"jq cannot run pipe_through programs, pinned beside the six that can\".\n\n\n\nBUT the brief says: \"Add the corpus case FIRST (tests/corpus/, program/input/output per AGENTS.md)\". This explicitly says corpus case with output. Which cannot work for jq. So there's a genuine contradiction between the brief and reality. This is exactly an escalation situation.\n\n\n\nBut wait. Let me reconsider one more time. Am I 100% sure jq can't spawn a subprocess? Let me think about jq modules... jq has `import \"foo\" as foo;` - imports jq modules. `include \"foo\";` - includes. Both load jq source files. No exec.\n\nThere's `$ENV` and `env`... read environment. No exec.\n\n`debug`, `stderr`... write. No exec.\n\n\n\nDefinitively no exec. jq cannot run grep.\n\n\n\nSo, I'll escalate. But also, per the brief: \"take the most conservative continuation, and keep going\". I need to make a decision about what to DO while the human is away. \n\n\n\nHmm, let me reconsider the options with fresh eyes:\n\n\n\nOption E: The corpus case is written WITH `output`, and jq is made to \"refuse\" at run time... no, then harness reports BROKEN. Unless... wait. What if jq CAN be made to produce the output by having the *toylang compiler* implement pipe_through for jq at a different level: e.g., since the emitted jq program can't spawn grep, maybe the *compiler* (emit_jq) could... no. The emitted program must compute grep output at run time. Impossible.\n\n\n\nOption F: Pipe_through programs are REFUSED BY THE CHECKER (not per-backend, but at compile time, so all backends refuse identically without emitting anything). Then the corpus case is `refuses: true`, and... but then nothing is built. No.\n\n\n\nOption G: The feature is built for the six backends, the corpus case... hmm, what if the corpus case is `refuses: true` AND we ALSO implement it in six backends, such that: jq refuses at emit (clean refusal, like the cycle refusal),and the OTHER six backends... refuse too at emit? That's silly.\n\n\n\nHmm, actually, wait. Let me reconsider whether the corpus `refuses: true` case could be paired with the six-backend implementation DIFFERENTLY. The harness: for `refuses`, it calls run_on on all 7 and asserts each returns Err. If we implement pipe_through in 6 backends(so run_on succeeds there,, then those six would NOT refuse→harness fails. So no.\n\n\n\nOK. So truly: either all run it, or none run it, per case. jq can't run it. So no `output` corpus case. And a `refuses` corpus case builds nothing.\n\n So: implement on 6 backends, test outside the corpus, escalate. That is the only path that actually builds the feature while keeping the corpus honest. \n\n\n\nNow, the huge question: what's the actual implementation scope? This touches:\n- parser (parse `pipe_through` as a builtin call, or special-cased?)\n- ast\n- tir (Builtin::PipeThrough, maybe Stage::PipeThrough)\n- check/types.rs and check/mod.rs (signatures, stream typing, linearity, containment,\n- check/linearity.rs\n- emit_go, emit_js, emit_llvm, emit_lua, emit_py, emit_rs (subprocess lowering,\n- emit_jq (refuse,\n- tags.rs (for tag_corpus,\n- prelude? no, it's a builtin\n- tests: a corpus case... can't be corpus. A dedicated test file (tests/pipe_through.rs?,\n- tests/streaming.rs? a liveness probe?\n\n\n\nThat's an enormous amount of work. And the semantics are subtle: spawning a subprocess from each target language, writing the stream lines to its stdin, closing it, reading its stdout lines. And handling: the program might not be `lines`-sourced (streams from a function param;e.g. `fn f(s: Stream<Str>) -> Stream<Str> = s | pipe_through(...)`). And the fusion machinery (`tir::fusion`) only handles jsonlines-bodies. pipe_through in the middle of a pipeline is NOT fused by the jsonlines machinery - it's a stage. Under eager lowering, the whole stream is materialized as a Vec anyway,so pipe_through is: given a Vec<Str>, spawn subprocess, write all lines, close, read all output lines → Vec<Str>. That's a pure \"value\" transform on a materialized Vec. \n\n\n\nUnder the current eager lowering, `lines` becomes \"read all stdin lines as a Vec\". Then pipe_through transforms Vec<Str> → Vec<Str>. Then collect is identity. This means pipe_through can be implemented as an ordinary builtin: `Vec<Str> -> Vec<Str>`. NOT a Stage at all, because streams are materialized eagerly everywhere except the jsonlines fusion. Actually wait - is that true? Let me check: under eager lowering, how does `lines` produce a Vec? Let me look at how each backend lowers `Kind::Lines`. From the grep: js: `Kind::Lines => \"tl_collect_lines()\"` which reads all lines into a Vec. lua: `tl_collect_lines()`. py: `tl_collect_lines()`. llvm: `call_rt(rt.collect_lines)`. \n\nSo yes: eager lowering materializes `lines` as a Vec<Str>. Stream operations (map, select, etc.) lower to Vec operations. And `collect` on a stream... how is collect lowered? For an eager Vec, collect is identity. \n\n\n\nSo pipe_through can be an ordinary builtin `Vec<Str> -> Vec<Str>` under eager lowering: given the already-materialized Vec of lines, spawn the subprocess, write all lines, read back all output lines. This makes the implementation MUCH simpler - no fusion machinery changes needed for correctness on the corpus-level output. (The \"streaming\" aspect - not materializing all input before spawning the subprocess - is a *liveness* property that tests/streaming.rs would probe, not the corpus. The issue title says \"streaming\", and the design ruling picked \"full streaming\". But the minimum viable implementation for the brief's test surface (just check green = corpus+unit tests) could be eager. Hmm. But that would be dishonest to the design ruling (\"full streaming pipe-to-subprocess\"). Ugh.)\n\n\n\nHold on, let me reconsider scope once more. The brief says: \"Build the streaming pipe-to-subprocess primitive per the shell-out-design ruling\". And: \"Definition of done: implementation complete; `just check` green\". The fast loop `just check` runs... let me check what just check runs. And whether tests/streaming.rs is part of `just check`.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05ede16c3001V8wmhTag0Yk3VD","sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","messageID":"msg_05eddda20001MXg8AMbfZOrhRB"}}
