{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788298017465,"sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_53bea24b06734c63a2219e00","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"grep -n \"Call\\|call\" src/parse.rs | head -20; echo \"=====\"; grep -n \"Call\" src/ast.rs | head -20"},"output":"145:/// Skips trivia, then reads exactly one token from the front of `input`. Called fresh for every\n446:    /// Where a call was declined because its argument started on a later line: the argument\n447:    /// token's start, and the callee's name. Declining is usually right -- a definition's body\n450:    /// declined token, the intended reading was probably the call, and the error can name the\n456:    /// parenthesized group, a call argument, an arm's own guard -- puts it back to false, since\n484:    /// one a call declined to take as a cross-line argument (see `declined_cross_line`): the\n487:        if let Some((at, callee)) = &self.declined_cross_line\n494:                     write `{callee}(...)` to call across lines\"\n502:    /// call arguments: an argument, bare or parenthesized, must start on the same line as its\n509:    /// The same-line rule applied at one call site: true when the argument starting at\n510:    /// `arg_span` is on `callee_span`'s line. A cross-line decline is recorded (see\n512:    /// why the call was not taken.\n513:    fn takes_argument(&mut self, callee: &str, callee_span: Span, arg_span: Span) -> bool {\n514:        if self.same_line(callee_span.end, arg_span.start) {\n517:        self.declined_cross_line = Some((arg_span.start, callee.to_string()));\n521:    /// Runs `f` with `or` reading as `separates` says, restoring the caller's reading after.\n642:    /// A call's argument when it opens with `(` or `{`: a parenthesized expression, or a record\n689:    /// single type, spelled the way a call spells its argument: a record type directly in\n1130:    /// What a name means once read: a qualified variant (`Shape.circle`), a call when an\n1133:    /// A name followed by an argument is a call; bare application is the default spelling and\n=====\n220:    Call {\n290:    /// Only the qualified form needs its own node; a bare `circle{r: 1}` is an ordinary `Call`\n371:            | Expr::Call { span, .. }\n","metadata":{"output":"145:/// Skips trivia, then reads exactly one token from the front of `input`. Called fresh for every\n446:    /// Where a call was declined because its argument started on a later line: the argument\n447:    /// token's start, and the callee's name. Declining is usually right -- a definition's body\n450:    /// declined token, the intended reading was probably the call, and the error can name the\n456:    /// parenthesized group, a call argument, an arm's own guard -- puts it back to false, since\n484:    /// one a call declined to take as a cross-line argument (see `declined_cross_line`): the\n487:        if let Some((at, callee)) = &self.declined_cross_line\n494:                     write `{callee}(...)` to call across lines\"\n502:    /// call arguments: an argument, bare or parenthesized, must start on the same line as its\n509:    /// The same-line rule applied at one call site: true when the argument starting at\n510:    /// `arg_span` is on `callee_span`'s line. A cross-line decline is recorded (see\n512:    /// why the call was not taken.\n513:    fn takes_argument(&mut self, callee: &str, callee_span: Span, arg_span: Span) -> bool {\n514:        if self.same_line(callee_span.end, arg_span.start) {\n517:        self.declined_cross_line = Some((arg_span.start, callee.to_string()));\n521:    /// Runs `f` with `or` reading as `separates` says, restoring the caller's reading after.\n642:    /// A call's argument when it opens with `(` or `{`: a parenthesized expression, or a record\n689:    /// single type, spelled the way a call spells its argument: a record type directly in\n1130:    /// What a name means once read: a qualified variant (`Shape.circle`), a call when an\n1133:    /// A name followed by an argument is a call; bare application is the default spelling and\n=====\n220:    Call {\n290:    /// Only the qualified form needs its own node; a bare `circle{r: 1}` is an ordinary `Call`\n371:            | Expr::Call { span, .. }\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"grep -n \"Call\\|call\" src/parse.rs | head -20; echo \"=====\"; grep -n \"Call\" src/ast.rs | head -20","time":{"start":1788298017462,"end":1788298017464}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"`just check` = `cargo nextest run -E 'not test(every_fragment_is_a_real_program)'`. That runs ALL tests EXCEPT the docs mega-test. So tests/streaming.rs IS part of `just check`. And tests/corpus.rs too. So any test file I add (tests/pipe_through.rs) runs in `just check`.\n\nSo `just check` green requires: corpus cases pass on all 7 backends (no pipe_through corpus output case possible for jq), and tests/streaming.rs passes, etc.\n\n.\n\nNow I need to decide: given the jq blocker, what is the \"most conservative continuation\"?\n\nGiven the strong precedent (tests/int64.rs: corpus can't carry a jq-impossible feature; test it outside the corpus with jq's behavior pinned beside it,, the natural continuation is:\n\n1. Write ESCALATION.md: the question (how to build/test pipe_through given jq can't exec and the corpus is all-or-nothing), alternatives (A: corpus refusals:true + don't build; B: implement on six, test in dedicated non-corpus file, no corpus case;; C: extend the harness with per-backend refusal/expectation support so a corpus case can name which backends run; costs of each; my recommendation: B (matches int64 precedent, doesn't touch the harness, builds the feature).\n\n2. Commit it.\n\n\n3. Implement pipe_through on the six backends that can spawn subprocesses, test in a dedicated test file (tests/pipe_through.rs, pinned across six backends with jq's refusal beside it), get `just check` green.\n\n\n\nHmm, but honestly, implementing pipe_through across 6 backends including LLVM (native,) js, lua, go, py, rust is a MASSIVE job. Let me assess whether it's tractable in this session. The backends each need:\n- A helper that: spawns `cmd` with args, writes the Vec<Str> lines to its stdin, closes stdin, reads stdout lines into a Vec<Str>, handles failure (non-zero exit → what? runtime error/refusal. \n- Wiring for the new Builtin::PipeThrough in expr/match lowering.\n\n\n\nFor LLVM (emit_llvm.rs, 2586 lines,: a C runtime (RUNTIME_C,) and LLVM IR. I'd need to add a C helper function and wire it into IR. Complex but doable.\n.\n\nFor Go, JS, Lua, Py, Rust: each needs a helper and a wiring site. All straightforward.\n\n\n\nThis is a lot, but the brief explicitly says development time is not a cost, and to commit as pieces work. Let me also remember the scope-pruning guidance from the global CLAUDE.md: \"Starting with a slightly overscoped implementation is fine... As work progresses, actively prune.\" And AGENTS.md: \"Build for callers that exist.\"\n\n\n\nWait, actually. Before I commit to this giant path, let me reconsider whether maybe there's a smarter, smaller design. The shape: `pipe_through(cmd, args)` where `cmd: Str` and `args: Vec<Str>`. It's a builtin. The checker needs to: \n- typecheck it: takes (Str, Vec<Str>, and the stream subject. How does pipe_through appear syntactically? `lines | pipe_through(\"grep\", [\"foo\"])`. So it's a call with two args, piped into with the stream as left operand. In this language, `a | f(b)` means... `|` is Bind (a | body). Let me look at how `|` and calls work: `lines | map(. + \"!\")` - the pipe binds a stream to a lambda. For `lines | pipe_through(\"grep\", [\"foo\"])`, the call `pipe_through(\"grep\", [\"foo\"])` — how would the stream be passed? In `f(g)`, f is a function/builtin with one arg g. `pipe_through(\"grep\", [\"foo\"])` has two args. Hmm. How does the language do multi-arg calls? Let me look at the AST/parse for calls. Maybe there's a tuple or record arg, or multiple args supported.\n\n\n\nLet me look at how `jsonlines` is called with one arg, and how builtins with two args would be spelled. Actually, is there any 2-arg builtin or function? Let me check parse.rs for Call.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05ede3a9d001o46E4AW4hA3G3m","sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","messageID":"msg_05ede1720001rGQqaAoqd5H906"}}
