{"type":"text","timestamp":1788298031075,"sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","part":{"id":"prt_05ede6e05001UeJLDS6Z6V1pgh","messageID":"msg_05ede4cf1001t6Ef5UvTonrvMY","sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","type":"text","text":"\n\n","time":{"start":1788298030597,"end":1788298031070}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788298031076,"sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_a36d59a5d14146d0888a59e3","state":{"status":"error","input":{"command":"grep -n \"Comma\\|Tok::Comma\\|`,`\" src/parse.rs | head -20"},"error":"The user rejected permission to use this specific tool call.","time":{"start":1788298031062,"end":1788298031074}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Calls take exactly one argument. So `pipe_through(\"grep\", [\"foo\"])` needs a multi-arg calling convention. Options:\n1. Two-arg calls become supported (comma-separated args in parens, treating `f(a, b)` as f applied to a record/tuple? \n2. The ratified shape is shorthand for `pipe_through([\"grep\", \"foo\"])`? Hmm, no - the shape is `pipe_through(\"grep\", [\"args\"])`.\n3. A record argument: `pipe_through({cmd: \"grep\", args: [\"foo\"]})`. But the ratified shape says otherwise.\n\n\n\nThe issue's shape `pipe_through(\"grep\", [\"foo\"])` — this is the \"bash style\" idea the maintainer mentioned, so the syntax is literally a 2-arg call. This language currently has single-arg calls only. So implementing this shape requires adding multi-arg call parsing... OR treating it as sugar. \n\n\n\nHmm, wait. Let me check how `Range` etc. take args. `range(n)` one arg. `str(n)` one arg. Everything is unary. Multi-arg calls don't exist in the grammar yet.\n\n Let me verify by checking the checker's builtin() table and whether any builtin takes 2 args. From the Builtin enum in tir.rs: all take one `arg`. And functions take one param (Func.param: Option<String>). So the whole language is unary. Multi-arg calls would be a NEW language feature. \n\n\n\nSo the shape `pipe_through(\"grep\", [\"foo\"])` requires a decision about multi-arg calls. The issue says the shape is \"ratified\"... but the grammar doesn't support it. Hmm. Actually, wait - maybe the design intends `pipe_through` to take a *Vec<Str>* of the whole command, like `pipe_through([\"grep\", \"foo\"])`? But the shape explicitly writes `pipe_through(\"grep\", [\"foo\"])`. \n\n\n\nOK. Let me look at the issue text once more: \"Ruling from the call-syntax-and-outside-world grill round (shell-out-design board row): the maintainer picked **full streaming pipe-to-subprocess** over blocking-capture-only, a bash-style capture literal, or deferring entirely.\"\n\nAnd: \"Shape ratified (from the round's preview, PROPOSED/unverified):\n\n```toylang\nlines | pipe_through(\"grep\", [\"foo\"]) | collect\n```\"\n\nSo the shape is PROPOSED/unverified - NOT fully ratified. It says \"PROPOSED/unverified\". And the maintainer's note: \"the bash style capture literal for this full pipe to subprocess flow looks interesting too; but that would probably need to be a template string and if we do build template string then we may as well build universal template strings\" -- flagged as a related idea.\n\n\n\nSo the syntax `pipe_through(\"grep\", [\"foo\"])` is proposed, not confirmed. The unary-call grammar is a real constraint the design didn't account for. This is ANOTHER escalation-worthy decision: how to spell a 2-arg call in a unary language.\n\n OR maybe: the design intends `pipe_through` with a record arg or a Vec arg, and the \"(\"grep\", [\"foo\"])\" is just the bash-inspired shorthand for the grill round, not final syntax.\n\n\n\nGiven all this, I'm now quite convinced the right move is: write ESCALATION.md capturing BOTH blockers (the jq-impossibility + the multi-arg-call grammar gap), commit it, and then take the most conservative continuation. \n\n\n\nBut what IS the most conservative continuation that makes progress? Let me think hard about the minimal coherent piece I can build and commit:\n\nThe most conservative *buildable* piece: implement pipe_through as a UNARY builtin taking a Vec<Str> command (e.g. `pipe_through([\"grep\", \"foo\"])`)... but that departs from the ratified(proposed) shape. Hmm. That's a design decision I shouldn't make unilaterally... but it IS \"conservative\" in that it doesn't touch the multi-arg grammar. Actually wait, would it be? The shape `pipe_through(\"grep\", [\"foo\"])` says cmd is a Str and args a Vec<Str>. A unary form could be `pipe_through(\"grep\", [\"foo\"])` interpreted as sugar for a record... \n\n\n\nHmm. Let me look at whether the parser could support a comma'd call trivially. Let me look at how `argument()` parses a parenthesized group. `f(a, b)` — inside parens, `a, b` — is `,` a token? Let me check the tokenizer for `,`.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05ede6fcb001bjVRkYfyriLvca","sessionID":"ses_fa1260da9ffea7gMsosoWnpMXr","messageID":"msg_05ede4cf1001t6Ef5UvTonrvMY"}}
